- definition: `lines 137-179`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 165`\n  - observed lines: `165, 166, 170, 171`\n  - trace activity: 18 events, 3 calls, 12 line events, 3 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 5\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 165`: `@_retry_with_clean_cache(url, data_home)`\n    - `line 166`: `def _load_json():`\n    - `line 170`: `try:`\n- artifact: `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response`\n  - module: `sklearn.datasets._openml`\n  - definition: `lines 492-530`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 500`\n  - observed lines: `500, 502, 505, 507, 512, 514, 515, 516, ...`\n  - trace activity: 16 events, 1 calls, 13 line events, 1 returns, 1 exceptions, max depth 6\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 500`: `response = _open_openml_url(url, data_home)`\n    - `line 502`: `with closing(response):`\n    - `line 505`: `actual_md5_checksum = hashlib.md5()`\n- Additional artifact candidates omitted: `22`"}, {"content": "Let's start by exploring the `fetch_openml` function in `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py` to understand where the ARFF data is being downloaded and potentially processed.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:fetch_openml']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:fetch_openml`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:fetch_openml`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:fetch_openml`.\n```\n664 | def fetch_openml(\n665 |     name: Optional[str] = None,\n666 |     *,\n667 |     version: Union[str, int] = 'active',\n668 |     data_id: Optional[int] = None,\n669 |     data_home: Optional[str] = None,\n670 |     target_column: Optional[Union[str, List]] = 'default-target',\n671 |     cache: bool = True,\n672 |     return_X_y: bool = False,\n673 |     as_frame: bool = False\n674 | ):\n675 |     \"\"\"Fetch dataset from openml by name or dataset id.\n676 | \n677 |     Datasets are uniquely identified by either an integer ID or by a\n678 |     combination of name and version (i.e. there might be multiple\n679 |     versions of the 'iris' dataset). Please give either name or data_id\n680 |     (not both). In case a name is given, a version can also be\n681 |     provided.\n682 | \n683 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <openml>`.\n684 | \n685 |     .. versionadded:: 0.20\n686 | \n687 |     .. note:: EXPERIMENTAL\n688 | \n689 |         The API is experimental (particularly the return value structure),\n690 |         and might have small backward-incompatible changes in future releases.\n691 | \n692 |     Parameters\n693 |     ----------\n694 |     name : str, default=None\n695 |         String identifier of the dataset. Note that OpenML can have multiple\n696 |         datasets with the same name.\n697 | \n698 |     version : int or 'active', default='active'\n699 |         Version of the dataset. Can only be provided if also ``name`` is given.\n700 |         If 'active' the oldest version that's still active is used. Since\n701 |         there may be more than one active version of a dataset, and those\n702 |         versions may fundamentally be different from one another, setting an\n703 |         exact version is highly recommended.\n704 | \n705 |     data_id : int, default=None\n706 |         OpenML ID of the dataset. The most specific way of retrieving a\n707 |         dataset. If data_id is not given, name (and potential version) are\n708 |         used to obtain a dataset.\n709 | \n710 |     data_home : str, default=None\n711 |         Specify another download and cache folder for the data sets. By default\n712 |         all scikit-learn data is stored in '~/scikit_learn_data' subfolders.\n713 | \n714 |     target_column : str, list or None, default='default-target'\n715 |         Specify the column name in the data to use as target. If\n716 |         'default-target', the standard target column a stored on the server\n717 |         is used. If ``None``, all columns are returned as data and the\n718 |         target is ``None``. If list (of strings), all columns with these names\n719 |         are returned as multi-target (Note: not all scikit-learn classifiers\n720 |         can handle all types of multi-output combinations)\n721 | \n722 |     cache : bool, default=True\n723 |         Whether to cache downloaded datasets using joblib.\n724 | \n725 |     return_X_y : bool, default=False\n726 |         If True, returns ``(data, target)`` instead of a Bunch object. See\n727 |         below for more information about the `data` and `target` objects.\n728 | \n729 |     as_frame : bool or 'auto', default=False\n730 |         If True, the data is a pandas DataFrame including columns with\n731 |         appropriate dtypes (numeric, string or categorical). The target is\n732 |         a pandas DataFrame or Series depending on the number of target_columns.\n733 |         The Bunch will contain a ``frame`` attribute with the target and the\n734 |         data. If ``return_X_y`` is True, then ``(data, target)`` will be pandas\n735 |         DataFrames or Series as describe above.\n736 |         If as_frame is 'auto', the data and target will be converted to\n737 |         DataFrame or Series as if as_frame is set to True, unless the dataset\n738 |         is stored in sparse format.\n739 | \n740 |     Returns\n741 |     -------\n742 | \n743 |     data : :class:`~sklearn.utils.Bunch`\n744 |         Dictionary-like object, with the following attributes.\n745 | \n746 |         data : np.array, scipy.sparse.csr_matrix of floats, or pandas DataFrame\n747 |             The feature matrix. Categorical features are encoded as ordinals.\n748 |         target : np.array, pandas Series or DataFrame\n749 |             The regression target or classification labels, if applicable.\n750 |             Dtype is float if numeric, and object if categorical. If\n751 |             ``as_frame`` is True, ``target`` is a pandas object.\n752 |         DESCR : str\n753 |             The full description of the dataset\n754 |         feature_names : list\n755 |             The names of the dataset columns\n756 |         target_names: list\n757 |             The names of the target columns\n758 | \n759 |         .. versionadded:: 0.22\n760 | \n761 |         categories : dict or None\n762 |             Maps each categorical feature name to a list of values, such\n763 |             that the value encoded as i is ith in the list. If ``as_frame``\n764 |             is True, this is None.\n765 |         details : dict\n766 |             More metadata from OpenML\n767 |         frame : pandas DataFrame\n768 |             Only present when `as_frame=True`. DataFrame with ``data`` and\n769 |             ``target``.\n770 | \n771 |     (data, target) : tuple if ``return_X_y`` is True\n772 | \n773 |         .. note:: EXPERIMENTAL\n774 | \n775 |             This interface is **experimental** and subsequent releases may\n776 |             change attributes without notice (although there should only be\n777 |             minor changes to ``data`` and ``target``).\n778 | \n779 |         Missing values in the 'data' are represented as NaN's. Missing values\n780 |         in 'target' are represented as NaN's (numerical target) or None\n781 |         (categorical target)\n782 |     \"\"\"\n783 |     data_home = get_data_home(data_home=data_home)\n784 |     data_home = join(data_home, 'openml')\n785 |     if cache is False:\n786 |         # no caching will be applied\n787 |         data_home = None\n788 | \n789 |     # check valid function arguments. data_id XOR (name, version) should be\n790 |     # provided\n791 |     if name is not None:\n792 |         # OpenML is case-insensitive, but the caching mechanism is not\n793 |         # convert all data names (str) to lower case\n794 |         name = name.lower()\n795 |         if data_id is not None:\n796 |             raise ValueError(\n797 |                 \"Dataset data_id={} and name={} passed, but you can only \"\n798 |                 \"specify a numeric data_id or a name, not \"\n799 |                 \"both.\".format(data_id, name))\n800 |         data_info = _get_data_info_by_name(name, version, data_home)\n801 |         data_id = data_info['did']\n802 |     elif data_id is not None:\n803 |         # from the previous if statement, it is given that name is None\n804 |         if version != \"active\":\n805 |             raise ValueError(\n806 |                 \"Dataset data_id={} and version={} passed, but you can only \"\n807 |                 \"specify a numeric data_id or a version, not \"\n808 |                 \"both.\".format(data_id, name))\n809 |     else:\n810 |         raise ValueError(\n811 |             \"Neither name nor data_id are provided. Please provide name or \"\n812 |             \"data_id.\")\n813 | \n814 |     data_description = _get_data_description_by_id(data_id, data_home)\n815 |     if data_description['status'] != \"active\":\n816 |         warn(\"Version {} of dataset {} is inactive, meaning that issues have \"\n817 |              \"been found in the dataset. Try using a newer version from \"\n818 |              \"this URL: {}\".format(\n819 |                 data_description['version'],\n820 |                 data_description['name'],\n821 |                 data_description['url']))\n822 |     if 'error' in data_description:\n823 |         warn(\"OpenML registered a problem with the dataset. It might be \"\n824 |              \"unusable. Error: {}\".format(data_description['error']))\n825 |     if 'warning' in data_description:\n826 |         warn(\"OpenML raised a warning on the dataset. It might be \"\n827 |              \"unusable. Warning: {}\".format(data_description['warning']))\n828 | \n829 |     return_sparse = False\n830 |     if data_description['format'].lower() == 'sparse_arff':\n831 |         return_sparse = True\n832 | \n833 |     if as_frame == 'auto':\n834 |         as_frame = not return_sparse\n835 | \n836 |     if as_frame and return_sparse:\n837 |         raise ValueError('Cannot return dataframe with sparse data')\n838 | \n839 |     # download data features, meta-info about column types\n840 |     features_list = _get_data_features(data_id, data_home)\n841 | \n842 |     if not as_frame:\n843 |         for feature in features_list:\n844 |             if 'true' in (feature['is_ignore'], feature['is_row_identifier']):\n845 |                 continue\n846 |             if feature['data_type'] == 'string':\n847 |                 raise ValueError('STRING attributes are not supported for '\n848 |                                  'array representation. Try as_frame=True')\n849 | \n850 |     if target_column == \"default-target\":\n851 |         # determines the default target based on the data feature results\n852 |         # (which is currently more reliable than the data description;\n853 |         # see issue: https://github.com/openml/OpenML/issues/768)\n854 |         target_columns = [feature['name'] for feature in features_list\n855 |                           if feature['is_target'] == 'true']\n856 |     elif isinstance(target_column, str):\n857 |         # for code-simplicity, make target_column by default a list\n858 |         target_columns = [target_column]\n859 |     elif target_column is None:\n860 |         target_columns = []\n861 |     elif isinstance(target_column, list):\n862 |         target_columns = target_column\n863 |     else:\n864 |         raise TypeError(\"Did not recognize type of target_column\"\n865 |                         \"Should be str, list or None. Got: \"\n866 |                         \"{}\".format(type(target_column)))\n867 |     data_columns = _valid_data_column_names(features_list,\n868 |                                             target_columns)\n869 | \n870 |     shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]]\n871 |     # determine arff encoding to return\n872 |     if not return_sparse:\n873 |         # The shape must include the ignored features to keep the right indexes\n874 |         # during the arff data conversion.\n875 |         data_qualities = _get_data_qualities(data_id, data_home)\n876 |         shape = _get_num_samples(data_qualities), len(features_list)\n877 |     else:\n878 |         shape = None\n879 | \n880 |     # obtain the data\n881 |     url = _DATA_FILE.format(data_description['file_id'])\n882 |     bunch = _download_data_to_bunch(url, return_sparse, data_home,\n883 |                                     as_frame=as_frame,\n884 |                                     features_list=features_list, shape=shape,\n885 |                                     target_columns=target_columns,\n886 |                                     data_columns=data_columns)\n887 | \n888 |     if return_X_y:\n889 |         return bunch.data, bunch.target\n890 | \n891 |     description = \"{}\\n\\nDownloaded from openml.org.\".format(\n892 |         data_description.pop('description'))\n893 | \n894 |     bunch.update(\n895 |         DESCR=description, details=data_description,\n896 |         url=\"https://www.openml.org/d/{}\".format(data_id))\n897 | \n898 |     return bunch\n```\n"}, {"content": "The `fetch_openml` function appears to be responsible for downloading datasets from OpenML, including the ARFF files. It calls `_download_data_to_bunch` to handle the actual data fetching and conversion to a Bunch object. Given the problem statement, we should investigate `_download_data_to_bunch` to see where the ARFF data is being handled and where we can add the MD5 checksum validation.\n\nNext, let's explore `_download_data_to_bunch` in the same file.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_download_data_to_bunch`.\n```\n509 | def _download_data_to_bunch(\n510 |     url: str,\n511 |     sparse: bool,\n512 |     data_home: Optional[str],\n513 |     *,\n514 |     as_frame: bool,\n515 |     features_list: List,\n516 |     data_columns: List[int],\n517 |     target_columns: List,\n518 |     shape: Optional[Tuple[int, int]]\n519 | ):\n520 |     \"\"\"Download OpenML ARFF and convert to Bunch of data\n521 |     \"\"\"\n522 |     # NB: this function is long in order to handle retry for any failure\n523 |     #     during the streaming parse of the ARFF.\n524 | \n525 |     # Prepare which columns and data types should be returned for the X and y\n526 |     features_dict = {feature['name']: feature for feature in features_list}\n527 | \n528 |     # XXX: col_slice_y should be all nominal or all numeric\n529 |     _verify_target_data_type(features_dict, target_columns)\n530 | \n531 |     col_slice_y = [int(features_dict[col_name]['index'])\n532 |                    for col_name in target_columns]\n533 | \n534 |     col_slice_x = [int(features_dict[col_name]['index'])\n535 |                    for col_name in data_columns]\n536 |     for col_idx in col_slice_y:\n537 |         feat = features_list[col_idx]\n538 |         nr_missing = int(feat['number_of_missing_values'])\n539 |         if nr_missing > 0:\n540 |             raise ValueError('Target column {} has {} missing values. '\n541 |                              'Missing values are not supported for target '\n542 |                              'columns. '.format(feat['name'], nr_missing))\n543 | \n544 |     # Access an ARFF file on the OpenML server. Documentation:\n545 |     # https://www.openml.org/api_data_docs#!/data/get_download_id\n546 | \n547 |     if sparse is True:\n548 |         return_type = _arff.COO\n549 |     else:\n550 |         return_type = _arff.DENSE_GEN\n551 | \n552 |     frame = nominal_attributes = None\n553 | \n554 |     parse_arff: Callable\n555 |     postprocess: Callable\n556 |     if as_frame:\n557 |         columns = data_columns + target_columns\n558 |         parse_arff = partial(_convert_arff_data_dataframe, columns=columns,\n559 |                              features_dict=features_dict)\n560 | \n561 |         def postprocess(frame):\n562 |             X = frame[data_columns]\n563 |             if len(target_columns) >= 2:\n564 |                 y = frame[target_columns]\n565 |             elif len(target_columns) == 1:\n566 |                 y = frame[target_columns[0]]\n567 |             else:\n568 |                 y = None\n569 |             return X, y, frame, nominal_attributes\n570 |     else:\n571 |         def parse_arff(arff):\n572 |             X, y = _convert_arff_data(arff, col_slice_x, col_slice_y, shape)\n573 |             # nominal attributes is a dict mapping from the attribute name to\n574 |             # the possible values. Includes also the target column (which will\n575 |             # be popped off below, before it will be packed in the Bunch\n576 |             # object)\n577 |             nominal_attributes = {k: v for k, v in arff['attributes']\n578 |                                   if isinstance(v, list) and\n579 |                                   k in data_columns + target_columns}\n580 |             return X, y, nominal_attributes\n581 | \n582 |         def postprocess(X, y, nominal_attributes):\n583 |             is_classification = {col_name in nominal_attributes\n584 |                                  for col_name in target_columns}\n585 |             if not is_classification:\n586 |                 # No target\n587 |                 pass\n588 |             elif all(is_classification):\n589 |                 y = np.hstack([\n590 |                     np.take(\n591 |                         np.asarray(nominal_attributes.pop(col_name),\n592 |                                    dtype='O'),\n593 |                         y[:, i:i + 1].astype(int, copy=False))\n594 |                     for i, col_name in enumerate(target_columns)\n595 |                 ])\n596 |             elif any(is_classification):\n597 |                 raise ValueError('Mix of nominal and non-nominal targets is '\n598 |                                  'not currently supported')\n599 | \n600 |             # reshape y back to 1-D array, if there is only 1 target column;\n601 |             # back to None if there are not target columns\n602 |             if y.shape[1] == 1:\n603 |                 y = y.reshape((-1,))\n604 |             elif y.shape[1] == 0:\n605 |                 y = None\n606 |             return X, y, frame, nominal_attributes\n607 | \n608 |     out = _retry_with_clean_cache(url, data_home)(\n609 |         _load_arff_response)(url, data_home,\n610 |                              return_type=return_type,\n611 |                              encode_nominal=not as_frame,\n612 |                              parse_arff=parse_arff)\n613 |     X, y, frame, nominal_attributes = postprocess(*out)\n614 | \n615 |     return Bunch(data=X, target=y, frame=frame,\n616 |                  categories=nominal_attributes,\n617 |                  feature_names=data_columns,\n618 |                  target_names=target_columns)\n```\n"}, {"content": "The `_download_data_to_bunch` function handles the downloading and parsing of the ARFF data. It calls `_load_arff_response` to fetch the ARFF data, and it seems like the MD5 checksum verification should be added here or in a function called by `_load_arff_response`.\n\nNext, let's explore `_load_arff_response` to see where the ARFF data is being fetched and processed.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response`.\n```\n491 | def _load_arff_response(\n492 |     url: str,\n493 |     data_home: Optional[str],\n494 |     return_type, encode_nominal: bool,\n495 |     parse_arff: Callable[[ArffContainerType], Tuple]\n496 | ) -> Tuple:\n497 |     \"\"\"Load arff data with url and parses arff response with parse_arff\"\"\"\n498 |     response = _open_openml_url(url, data_home)\n499 | \n500 |     with closing(response):\n501 |         # Note that if the data is dense, no reading is done until the data\n502 |         # generator is iterated.\n503 |         arff = _arff.load((line.decode('utf-8') for line in response),\n504 |                           return_type=return_type,\n505 |                           encode_nominal=encode_nominal)\n506 |         return parse_arff(arff)\n```\n"}, {"content": "The `_load_arff_response` function fetches the ARFF data from the given URL and parses it using the `_arff.load` function. This is where we should add the MD5 checksum verification.\n\nNow, let's look at `_open_openml_url` to see how the response is being opened and streamed.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_open_openml_url']</parameter>\n\n\n\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_open_openml_url`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_open_openml_url`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_open_openml_url`.\n```\n 73 | def _open_openml_url(openml_path: str, data_home: Optional[str]):\n 74 |     \"\"\"\n 75 |     Returns a resource from OpenML.org. Caches it to data_home if required.\n 76 | \n 77 |     Parameters\n 78 |     ----------\n 79 |     openml_path : str\n 80 |         OpenML URL that will be accessed. This will be prefixes with\n 81 |         _OPENML_PREFIX\n 82 | \n 83 |     data_home : str\n 84 |         Directory to which the files will be cached. If None, no caching will\n 85 |         be applied.\n 86 | \n 87 |     Returns\n 88 |     -------\n 89 |     result : stream\n 90 |         A stream to the OpenML resource\n 91 |     \"\"\"\n 92 |     def is_gzip_encoded(_fsrc):\n 93 |         return _fsrc.info().get('Content-Encoding', '') == 'gzip'\n 94 | \n 95 |     req = Request(_OPENML_PREFIX + openml_path)\n 96 |     req.add_header('Accept-encoding', 'gzip')\n 97 | \n 98 |     if data_home is None:\n 99 |         fsrc = urlopen(req)\n100 |         if is_gzip_encoded(fsrc):\n101 |             return gzip.GzipFile(fileobj=fsrc, mode='rb')\n102 |         return fsrc\n103 | \n104 |     local_path = _get_local_path(openml_path, data_home)\n105 |     if not os.path.exists(local_path):\n106 |         try:\n107 |             os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(local_path))\n108 |         except OSError:\n109 |             # potentially, the directory has been created already\n110 |             pass\n111 | \n112 |         try:\n113 |             with closing(urlopen(req)) as fsrc:\n114 |                 opener: Callable\n115 |                 if is_gzip_encoded(fsrc):\n116 |                     opener = open\n117 |                 else:\n118 |                     opener = gzip.GzipFile\n119 |                 with opener(local_path, 'wb') as fdst:\n120 |                     shutil.copyfileobj(fsrc, fdst)\n121 |         except Exception:\n122 |             if os.path.exists(local_path):\n123 |                 os.unlink(local_path)\n124 |             raise\n125 | \n126 |     # XXX: First time, decompression will not be necessary (by using fsrc), but\n127 |     # it will happen nonetheless\n128 |     return gzip.GzipFile(local_path, 'rb')\n```\n"}, {"content": "The `_open_openml_url` function is responsible for opening and potentially caching the response from the OpenML URL. This function does not directly handle the MD5 checksum verification, but it provides the stream or file object that `_load_arff_response` uses to load the ARFF data.\n\nNext, let's look at the `_stream_checksum_generator` function mentioned in the unique artifacts. This function seems to be a good place to start for adding MD5 checksum verification since it processes the response stream.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_stream_checksum_generator']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_stream_checksum_generator`...\n### Search Result:\nInvalid name. \nHint: Valid entity name should be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" or just \"file_path\".\n"}, {"content": "It appears that `_stream_checksum_generator` is not a function in the `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py` file. Let's verify the `_load_arff_response` function again to ensure we are on the right track. We will also check the `_arff.load` function to see if there are any clues about handling the checksum there.\n\nFirst, let's confirm the `_load_arff_response` function.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py:_load_arff_response`.\n```\n491 | def _load_arff_response(\n492 |     url: str,\n493 |     data_home: Optional[str],\n494 |     return_type, encode_nominal: bool,\n495 |     parse_arff: Callable[[ArffContainerType], Tuple]\n496 | ) -> Tuple:\n497 |     \"\"\"Load arff data with url and parses arff response with parse_arff\"\"\"\n498 |     response = _open_openml_url(url, data_home)\n499 | \n500 |     with closing(response):\n501 |         # Note that if the data is dense, no reading is done until the data\n502 |         # generator is iterated.\n503 |         arff = _arff.load((line.decode('utf-8') for line in response),\n504 |                           return_type=return_type,\n505 |                           encode_nominal=encode_nominal)\n506 |         return parse_arff(arff)\n```\n"}, {"content": "Given that `_stream_checksum_generator` is not a valid function in `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py`, we will focus on adding MD5 checksum validation around the `_load_arff_response` function. Specifically, we can calculate the checksum while streaming the data and compare it with the expected MD5 checksum provided in the dataset description.\n\nSince `_load_arff_response` opens the URL and streams the data, we can modify this function or the surrounding code to include checksum validation.\n\nLet's also check the `_arff.load` function to see if there are any clues about handling the checksum there.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>['sklearn/externals/_arff.py:load']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sklearn/externals/_arff.py:load`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/externals/_arff.py:load`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sklearn/externals/_arff.py:load`.\n```\n1063 | def load(fp, encode_nominal=False, return_type=DENSE):\n1064 |     '''Load a file-like object containing the ARFF document and convert it into\n1065 |     a Python object.\n1066 | \n1067 |     :param fp: a file-like object.\n1068 |     :param encode_nominal: boolean, if True perform a label encoding\n1069 |         while reading the .arff file.\n1070 |     :param return_type: determines the data structure used to store the\n1071 |         dataset. Can be one of `arff.DENSE`, `arff.COO`, `arff.LOD`,\n1072 |         `arff.DENSE_GEN` or `arff.LOD_GEN`.\n1073 |         Consult the sections on `working with sparse data`_ and `loading\n1074 |         progressively`_.\n1075 |     :return: a dictionary.\n1076 |      '''\n1077 |     decoder = ArffDecoder()\n1078 |     return decoder.decode(fp, encode_nominal=encode_nominal,\n1079 |                           return_type=return_type)\n```\n"}, {"content": "The `_arff.load` function in `sklearn/externals/_arff.py` is responsible for decoding the ARFF file. However, it does not handle checksum validation. We should implement the checksum validation in the `_load_arff_response` function in `sklearn/datasets/_openml.py` or in a helper function that `_load_arff_response` can call.\n\nLet's now finalize the locations for potential modifications:\n\n```\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nfunction: _load_arff_response\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nfunction: _download_data_to_bunch\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nfunction: fetch_openml\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 500\n\nsklearn/datasets/_openml.py\nline: 501\n```\n\n<finish></finish>", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "explore_tree_structure", "description": "\nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"start_entities": {"description": "List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).", "type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "direction": {"description": "Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.", "type": "string", "enum": ["upstream", "downstream", "both"], "default": "downstream"}, "traversal_depth": {"description": "Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).", "type": "integer", "default": 2}, "entity_type_filter": {"description": "List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}, "dependency_type_filter": {"description": "List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}}, "required": ["start_entities"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 190061, "completion_tokens": 999}, "time": 92.0908682346344}]}}
{"instance_id": "pandas-dev__pandas-59900", "found_files": [["pandas/core/strings/accessor.py", "pandas/core/generic.py", "pandas/core/accessor.py", "pandas/core/construction.py", "pandas/core/internals/managers.py"]], "found_modules": [["pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame", "pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods", "pandas/core/arrays/numpy_.py:NumpyExtensionArray", "pandas/core/accessor.py:Accessor", "asv_bench/benchmarks/frame_methods.py:Apply", "pandas/core/construction.py:sanitize_array", "pandas/tseries/frequencies.py:_FrequencyInferer", "pandas/core/internals/managers.py:BaseBlockManager"]], "found_entities": [["pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.__getattr__", "pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods._validate", "pandas/core/arrays/numpy_.py:NumpyExtensionArray.isna", "pandas/core/accessor.py:Accessor.__get__", "asv_bench/benchmarks/frame_methods.py:Apply.time_apply_axis_1", "pandas/core/construction.py:sanitize_array", "pandas/tseries/frequencies.py:_FrequencyInferer.rep_stamp", "pandas/core/internals/managers.py:BaseBlockManager._validate_set_axis"]], "raw_output_loc": ["```\npandas/core/strings/accessor.py\nline: 258\nfunction: StringMethods._validate\n\npandas/core/generic.py\nline: 6056\nclass: NDFrame\nfunction: __getattr__\n\npandas/core/accessor.py\nline: 224\nclass: Accessor\nfunction: __get__\n\npandas/core/construction.py\nline: 526\nfunction: sanitize_array\n\npandas/core/internals/managers.py\nline: 275\nclass: BaseBlockManager\nfunction: _validate_set_axis\n```"], "meta_data": {"repo": "pandas-dev/pandas", "base_commit": "8d2ca0bf84bcf44a800ac19bdb4ed7ec88c555e2", "problem_statement": "ENH: increase verbosity on error\n### Feature Type\n\n- [ ] Adding new functionality to pandas\n\n- [X] Changing existing functionality in pandas\n\n- [ ] Removing existing functionality in pandas\n\n\n### Problem Description\n\nOn a specific type error the error causing type is not logged in the error\n\n### Feature Description\n\nBy replacing:\r\n`raise AttributeError(\"Can only use .str accessor with string values!\")`\r\nwith\r\n`raise AttributeError(f\"Can only use .str accessor with string values! Inferred dType: {inferred_dtype}\")`\r\nthe problem would be solved\r\n\r\nDetails can be found in [here](https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/pull/59649/files)\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\nUse a debugger and set a break point\n\n### Additional Context\n\n_No response_\n", "patch": "diff --git a/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py b/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py\nindex 3cb0e75cfb815..05e1a36877e06 100644\n--- a/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py\n+++ b/pandas/core/strings/accessor.py\n@@ -255,7 +255,9 @@ def _validate(data):\n         inferred_dtype = lib.infer_dtype(values, skipna=True)\n \n         if inferred_dtype not in allowed_types:\n-            raise AttributeError(\"Can only use .str accessor with string values!\")\n+            raise AttributeError(\n+                f\"Can only use .str accessor with string values, not {inferred_dtype}\"\n+            )\n         return inferred_dtype\n \n     def __getitem__(self, key):\n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 57031, "completion_tokens": 897}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\nexplore_graph_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 1, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Args:\n    start_entities:\n    direction:\n    traversal_depth:\n    entity_type_filter:\n    dependency_type_filter:\n    Returns:\n\nexplore_tree_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 2, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Analyzes and displays the dependency structure around specified entities in a code graph.\n    This function searches and presents relationships and dependencies for the specified entities (such as classes, functions, files, or directories) in a code graph.\n    It explores how the input entities relate to others, using defined types of dependencies, including 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes' and 'inherits'.\n    The search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n    Example Usage:\n    1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n    direction='downstream',\n    traversal_depth=2,\n    entity_type_filter=['class', 'function'],\n    dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n    2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n    direction='upstream',\n    traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n    Notes:\n    * Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n    * Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n    * Graph Context: The function operates on a pre-built code graph containing entities (e.g., files, classes and functions) and dependencies representing their interactions and relationships.\n    Parameters:\n    ----------\n    start_entities : list[str]\n    List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\n    - Entities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\"\n    (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\n    - For files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n    direction : str, optional\n    Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are:\n    - 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n    - 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others\n    (how others depend on them).\n    - 'both': Traversal in both directions.\n    Default is 'downstream'.\n    traversal_depth : int, optional\n    Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).\n    Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n    Default is 2.\n    entity_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all entity types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    dependency_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all dependency types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    Returns:\n    -------\n    result : object\n    An object representing the traversal results, which includes discovered entities and their dependencies.\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n\n- Comprehensive Dependency Analysis: Prioritize analyzing the code dependencies of functions, methods, or classes mentioned in the problem description or those identified in relevant locations. Use `search_invoke_and_reference` as the primary tool to ensure all interactions within the codebase are captured.\n\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #4: explore_tree_structure ----\nDescription: \nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n\nParameters:\n  (1) start_entities (array, required): List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n  (2) direction (string, optional): Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.\nAllowed values: [`upstream`, `downstream`, `both`]\n  (3) traversal_depth (integer, optional): Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n  (4) entity_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.\n  (5) dependency_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.\n---- END FUNCTION #4 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>value_1</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format, start with <function= and end with </function>\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- Only call one function at a time\n- You may provide optional reasoning for your function call in natural language BEFORE the function call, but NOT after.\n- If there is no function call available, answer the question like normal with your current knowledge and do not tell the user about function calls\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nGiven the following GitHub issue description, your objective is to localize the specific files, classes or functions, and lines of code that are relevant to the solution for the issue. More specifically, these issues are all \"feature request\" issues, which means that you need to find the locations the feature described in the issue.\n\nFollow these steps to localize the issue:\n## Step 1: Categorize and Extract Key Problem Information\n - Classify the issue description into the following categories:\n    Requested feature description, behavior with out this feature, expected behavior with the feature, and additional context.\n - Identify modules in the 'pandas-dev' package mentioned in each category.\n - Use extracted keywords and line numbers to search for relevant code references for additional context.\n\n## Step 2: Locate Referenced Modules\n- Accurately determine specific modules\n    - Explore the repo to familiarize yourself with its structure.\n    - Analyze the described workflow to identify specific modules or components being referenced.\n- Pay special attention to distinguishing between modules with similar names using context and described workflow.\n- Output Format for collected relevant modules:\n    - Use the format: 'file_path:QualifiedName'\n    - E.g., for a function `calculate_sum` in the `MathUtils` class located in `src/helpers/math_helpers.py`, represent it as: 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'.\n\n## Step 3: Analyze the feature\n- Understand the Purpose of the Feature in the Context of the Codebase\n    - Understand what the feature is supposed to do.\n    - Understand why it is needed.\n    - Understand how it fits into the overall functionality of the codebase.\n- Analyze the Feature's Relationship with the Codebase\n    - Identify the key affected components of the feature.\n    - Understand how these components interact with each other and with existing code.\n    - Consider the inputs, outputs, and side effects of the feature.\n\n## Step 4: Locate Areas for Feature Implementation\n- Locate specific files, functions, or lines of code implementing the key part of the requested feature or containing critical information for resolving the issue.\n- Consider upstream and downstream dependencies that may affect or be affected by the issue.\n- Think Thoroughly: List multiple potential solutions and consider edge cases that could impact the resolution.\n\n## Output Format for Final Results:\nYour final output should list the locations requiring modification, wrapped with triple backticks ```\nEach location should include the file path, class name (if applicable), function name, or line numbers, ordered by importance.\nYour answer would better include about 5 files.\n\nIMPORTANT FORMAT RULES:\n- Output ONLY the location block, followed by `<finish></finish>`.\n- Do NOT include explanations, bullets, headings, numbering, summaries, or notes before or after the location block.\n- Each file path MUST appear on its own line and end with `.py`.\n- Do NOT write `file.py:QualifiedName` on a single line in the final answer.\n- After a file path line, use separate `line:`, `class:`, and `function:` lines as needed.\n\n### Examples:\n```\nfull_path1/file1.py\nline: 10\nclass: MyClass1\nfunction: my_function1\n\nfull_path2/file2.py\nline: 76\nfunction: MyClass2.my_function2\n\nfull_path3/file3.py\nline: 24\nline: 156\nfunction: my_function3\n```\n\nReturn just the location(s)\n\nNote: Your thinking should be thorough and so it's fine if it's very long.\n\n--- BEGIN PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\nTitle: ENH: increase verbosity on error\n\n### Feature Type\n\n- [ ] Adding new functionality to pandas\n\n- [X] Changing existing functionality in pandas\n\n- [ ] Removing existing functionality in pandas\n\n\n### Problem Description\n\nOn a specific type error the error causing type is not logged in the error\n\n### Feature Description\n\nBy replacing:\r\n`raise AttributeError(\"Can only use .str accessor with string values!\")`\r\nwith\r\n`raise AttributeError(f\"Can only use .str accessor with string values! Inferred dType: {inferred_dtype}\")`\r\nthe problem would be solved\r\n\r\nDetails can be found in [here](https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/pull/59649/files)\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\nUse a debugger and set a break point\n\n### Additional Context\n\n_No response_\n--- END PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\n\nIMPORTANT: You should ONLY interact with the environment provided to you AND NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDon't include any lambda functions!\nYou should NOT modify any files!\n\n\nSupplemental context information:\n\nYou will receive supplemental context for the current task. Use it to better understand the issue and to localize the relevant code more accurately.\n\nTreat this context as additional evidence, not as the final answer.\n- It may contain noise.\n- It may be incomplete.\n- You should carefully analyze it and decide how to use it when searching for relevant code locations.\n\nDepending on the settings, the supplemental context may include:\n1. Unique artifacts\n2. Unique dependency tree\n\n## How to use this supplemental context\n- Use it to identify candidate files, classes, functions, and execution paths that may be relevant to the issue.\n- Cross-check it against the issue description and the repository evidence you collect.\n- Do not assume every artifact is important.\n- Do not assume the provided context is sufficient by itself.\n\n## What the provided information means\nAssume the issue sent to you is a feature request requesting feature F.\n\n1. Unique artifacts\n- The unique artifacts are obtained through a Software Reconnaissance-style analysis.\n- For the requested feature described in the issue text, two Python scripts, A.py and B.py, describing the same process are executed and traced.\n- The only difference is that A.py is executed with feature F enabled, while B.py is executed with the feature disabled.\n- By comparing the two tracing results, the overlapping part between the trace for A.py and B.py is subtracted.\n- The remaining artifacts are the unique artifacts that appear only in A.py.\n- These unique artifacts are likely to be related to the feature and thus to the issue.\n\n2. Unique dependency tree\n- The unique dependency tree is obtained by further analyzing the data dependencies of the unique artifacts.\n- It contains the dataflows associated with the unique artifacts.\n- It can help you understand how the unique artifacts are related to each other and how they are related to other parts of the codebase.\n\n\nHere is the information about the current task:\n1. Unique artifacts (obtained through Software Reconnaissance-style