       Parameters\n 943 |         ----------\n 944 |         filename : str\n 945 |             The output filename, e.g., :file:`mymovie.mp4`.\n 946 | \n 947 |         writer : `MovieWriter` or str, default: :rc:`animation.writer`\n 948 |             A `MovieWriter` instance to use or a key that identifies a\n 949 |             class to use, such as 'ffmpeg'.\n 950 | \n 951 |         fps : int, optional\n 952 |             Movie frame rate (per second).  If not set, the frame rate from the\n 953 |             animation's frame interval.\n 954 | \n 955 |         dpi : float, default: :rc:`savefig.dpi`\n 956 |             Controls the dots per inch for the movie frames.  Together with\n 957 |             the figure's size in inches, this controls the size of the movie.\n 958 | \n 959 |         codec : str, default: :rc:`animation.codec`.\n 960 |             The video codec to use.  Not all codecs are supported by a given\n 961 |             `MovieWriter`.\n 962 | \n 963 |         bitrate : int, default: :rc:`animation.bitrate`\n 964 |             The bitrate of the movie, in kilobits per second.  Higher values\n 965 |             means higher quality movies, but increase the file size.  A value\n 966 |             of -1 lets the underlying movie encoder select the bitrate.\n 967 | \n 968 |         extra_args : list of str or None, optional\n 969 |             Extra command-line arguments passed to the underlying movie encoder. These\n 970 |             arguments are passed last to the encoder, just before the output filename.\n 971 |             The default, None, means to use :rc:`animation.[name-of-encoder]_args` for\n 972 |             the builtin writers.\n 973 | \n 974 |         metadata : dict[str, str], default: {}\n 975 |             Dictionary of keys and values for metadata to include in\n 976 |             the output file. Some keys that may be of use include:\n 977 |             title, artist, genre, subject, copyright, srcform, comment.\n 978 | \n 979 |         extra_anim : list, default: []\n 980 |             Additional `Animation` objects that should be included\n 981 |             in the saved movie file. These need to be from the same\n 982 |             `.Figure` instance. Also, animation frames will\n 983 |             just be simply combined, so there should be a 1:1 correspondence\n 984 |             between the frames from the different animations.\n 985 | \n 986 |         savefig_kwargs : dict, default: {}\n 987 |             Keyword arguments passed to each `~.Figure.savefig` call used to\n 988 |             save the individual frames.\n 989 | \n 990 |         progress_callback : function, optional\n 991 |             A callback function that will be called for every frame to notify\n 992 |             the saving progress. It must have the signature ::\n 993 | \n 994 |                 def func(current_frame: int, total_frames: int) -> Any\n 995 | \n 996 |             where *current_frame* is the current frame number and *total_frames* is the\n 997 |             total number of frames to be saved. *total_frames* is set to None, if the\n 998 |             total number of frames cannot be determined. Return values may exist but are\n 999 |             ignored.\n1000 | \n1001 |             Example code to write the progress to stdout::\n1002 | \n1003 |                 progress_callback = lambda i, n: print(f'Saving frame {i}/{n}')\n1004 | \n1005 |         Notes\n1006 |         -----\n1007 |         *fps*, *codec*, *bitrate*, *extra_args* and *metadata* are used to\n1008 |         construct a `.MovieWriter` instance and can only be passed if\n1009 |         *writer* is a string.  If they are passed as non-*None* and *writer*\n1010 |         is a `.MovieWriter`, a `RuntimeError` will be raised.\n1011 |         \"\"\"\n1012 | \n1013 |         all_anim = [self]\n1014 |         if extra_anim is not None:\n1015 |             all_anim.extend(anim for anim in extra_anim\n1016 |                             if anim._