 - observed lines: `944, 945, 946, 948, 951, 953`\n  - trace activity: 10 events, 1 calls, 8 line events, 1 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 2\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 944`: `axes = range(self._AXIS_LEN) if axis is None else (self._get_axis_number(axis),)`\n    - `line 945`: `result = self.iloc[`\n    - `line 946`: `tuple(`\n- artifact: `pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame.axes`\n  - module: `pandas.core.frame`\n  - definition: `lines 993-1012`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 1012`\n  - observed lines: `1012`\n  - trace activity: 3 events, 1 calls, 1 line events, 1 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 3\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 1012`: `return [self.index, self.columns]`"}, {"content": "pandas/core/generic.py\nline: 841\nline: 953\nfunction: NDFrame.squeeze\n\npandas/core/generic.py\nline: 944\nline: 946\nline: 948\nline: 951\n\npandas/core/frame.py\nline: 1012\nfunction: DataFrame.axes\n\npandas/core/indexes/base.py\nclass: Index\n\npandas/core/indexes/multi.py\nclass: MultiIndex", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "user", "content": "Verify if the found locations contain all the necessary information to address the issue, and check for any relevant references in other parts of the codebase that may not have appeared in the search results. If not, continue searching for additional locations related to the issue.\nVerify that you have carefully analyzed the impact of the found locations on the repository, especially their dependencies. If you think you have solved the task, rewrite your final answer into the required location-only format, output ONLY the location block, and then call `finish` to finish.\nIMPORTANT: YOU SHOULD NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\n"}, {"content": "Let's verify the identified locations and search for any additional relevant references in the codebase.\n\nFirst, we'll retrieve the content of the `NDFrame.squeeze` function from `pandas/core/generic.py` to check if the type hint is indeed missing and if there are any relevant comments or references around it.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>\n['pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.squeeze']\n</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.squeeze`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.squeeze`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `pandas/core/generic.py:NDFrame.squeeze`.\n```\n841 | def squeeze(self, axis: Axis | None = None):\n842 |         \"\"\"\n843 |         Squeeze 1 dimensional axis objects into scalars.\n844 | \n845 |         Series or DataFrames with a single element are squeezed to a scalar.\n846 |         DataFrames with a single column or a single row are squeezed to a\n847 |         Series. Otherwise the object is unchanged.\n848 | \n849 |         This method is most useful when you don't know if your\n850 |         object is a Series or DataFrame, but you do know it has just a single\n851 |         column. In that case you can safely call `squeeze` to ensure you have a\n852 |         Series.\n853 | \n854 |         Parameters\n855 |         ----------\n856 |         axis : {0 or 'index', 1 or 'columns', None}, default None\n857 |             A specific axis to squeeze. By default, all length-1 axes are\n858 |             squeezed. For `Series` this parameter is unused and defaults to `None`.\n859 | \n860 |         Returns\n861 |         -------\n862 |         DataFrame, Series, or scalar\n863 |             The projection after squeezing `axis` or all the axes.\n864 | \n865 |         See Also\n866 |         --------\n867 |         Series.iloc : Integer-location based indexing for selecting scalars.\n868 |         DataFrame.iloc : Integer-location based indexing for selecting Series.\n869 |         Series.to_frame : Inverse of DataFrame.squeeze for a\n870 |             single-column DataFrame.\n871 | \n872 |         Examples\n873 |         --------\n874 |         >>> primes = pd.Series([2, 3, 5, 7])\n875 | \n876 |         Slicing might produce a Series with a single value:\n877 | \n878 |         >>> even_primes = primes[primes % 2 == 0]\n879 |         >>> even_primes\n880 |         0    2\n881 |         dtype: int64\n882 | \n883 |         >>> even_primes.squeeze()\n884 |         2\n885 | \n886 |         Squeezing objects with more than one value in every axis does nothing:\n887 | \n888 |         >>> odd_primes = primes[primes % 2 == 1]\n889 |         >>> odd_primes\n890 |         1    3\n891 |         2    5\n892 |         3    7\n893 |         dtype: int64\n894 | \n895 |         >>> odd_primes.squeeze()\n896 |         1    3\n897 |         2    5\n898 |         3    7\n899 |         dtype: int64\n900 | \n901 |         Squeezing is even more effective when used with DataFrames.\n902 | \n903 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame([[1, 2], [3, 4]], columns=[\"a\", \"b\"])\n904 |         >>> df\n905 |            a  b\n906 |         0  1  2\n907 |         1  3  4\n908 | \n909 |         Slicing a single column will produce a DataFrame with the columns\n910 |         having only one value:\n911 | \n912 |         >>> df_a = df[[\"a\"]]\n913 |         >>> df_a\n914 |            a\n915 |         0  1\n916 |         1  3\n917 | \n918 |         So the columns can be squeezed down, resulting in a Series:\n919 | \n920 |         >>> df_a.squeeze(\"columns\")\n921 |         0    1\n922 |         1    3\n923 |         Name: a, dtype: int64\n924 | \n925 |         Slicing a single row from a single column will produce a single\n926 |         scalar DataFrame:\n927 | \n928 |         >>> df_0a = df.loc[df.index < 1, [\"a\"]]\n929 |         >>> df_0a\n930 |            a\n931 |         0  1\n932 | \n933 |         Squeezing the rows produces a single scalar Series:\n934 | \n935 |         >>> df_0a.squeeze(\"rows\")\n936 |         a    1\n937 |         Name: 0, dtype: int64\n938 | \n939 |         Squeezing all axes will project directly into a scalar:\n940 | \n941 |         >>> df_0a.squeeze()\n942 |         1\n943 |         \"\"\"\n944 |         axes = range(self._AXIS_LEN) if axis is None else (self._get_axis_number(axis),)\n945 |         result = self.iloc[\n946 |             tuple(\n947 |                 0 if i in axes and len(a) == 1 else slice(None)\n948 |                 for i, a in enumerate(self.axes)\n949 |             )\n950 |         ]\n951 |         if isinstance(result, NDFrame):\n952 |             result = result.__finalize__(self, method=\"squeeze\")\n953 |         return result\n```\n"}, {"content": "The `NDFrame.squeeze` function in `pandas/core/generic.py` has a type hint for the `axis` parameter, but it does not have a return type hint. Given that the issue is specifically about adding a type hint for the `squeeze` method, we need to ensure that the return type hint is added.\n\nNext, let's check the `DataFrame.axes` function in `pandas/core/frame.py` to see if there are any relevant references or missing type hints.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>\n['pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame.axes']\n</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame.axes`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame.axes`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame.axes`.\n```\n 993 | def axes(self) -> list[Index]:\n 994 |         \"\"\"\n 995 |         Return a list representing the axes of the DataFrame.\n 996 | \n 997 |         It has the row axis labels and column axis labels as the only members.\n 998 |         They are returned in that order.\n 999 | \n1000 |         See Also\n1001 |         --------\n1002 |         DataFrame.index: The index (row labels) of the DataFrame.\n1003 |         DataFrame.columns: The column labels of the DataFrame.\n1004 | \n1005 |         Examples\n1006 |         --------\n1007 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\"col1\": [1, 2], \"col2\": [3, 4]})\n1008 |         >>> df.axes\n1009 |         [RangeIndex(start=0, stop=2, step=1), Index(['col1', 'col2'],\n1010 |         dtype='object')]\n1011 |         \"\"\"\n1012 |         return [self.index, self.columns]\n```\n"}, {"content": "The `DataFrame.axes` function in `pandas/core/frame.py` already has a return type hint, indicating it returns a list of `Index` objects. Since the issue is specifically about adding a type hint for the `squeeze` method, we do not need to modify this function.\n\nLet's check the `Index` and `MultiIndex` classes in `pandas/core/indexes/base.py` and `pandas/core/indexes/multi.py` respectively to ensure there are no other relevant references or missing type hints.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>\n['pandas/core/indexes/base.py:Index']\n</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `pandas/core/indexes/base.py:Index`...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `pandas/core/indexes/base.py:Index`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `pandas/core/indexes/base.py:Index`.\n```\n 320 | class Index(IndexOpsMixin, PandasObject):\n 321 |     \"\"\"\n 322 |     Immutable sequence used for indexing and alignment.\n 323 | \n 324 |     The basic object storing axis labels for all pandas objects.\n 325 | \n 326 |     .. versionchanged:: 2.0.0\n 327 | \n 328 |        Index can hold all numpy numeric dtypes (except float16). Previously only\n 329 |        int64/uint64/float64 dtypes were accepted.\n 330 | \n 331 |     Parameters\n 332 |     ----------\n 333 |     data : array-like (1-dimensional)\n 334 |         An array-like structure containing the data for the index. This could be a\n 335 |         Python list, a NumPy array, or a pandas Series.\n 336 |     dtype : str, numpy.dtype, or ExtensionDtype, optional\n 337 |         Data type for the output Index. If not specified, this will be\n 338 |         inferred from `data`.\n 339 |         See the :ref:`user guide <basics.dtypes>` for more usages.\n 340 |     copy : bool, default False\n 341 |         Copy input data.\n 342 |     name : object\n 343 |         Name to be stored in the index.\n 344 |     tupleize_cols : bool (default: True)\n 345 |         When True, attempt to create a MultiIndex if possible.\n 346 | \n 347 |     See Also\n 348 |     --------\n 349 |     RangeIndex : Index implementing a monotonic integer range.\n 350 |     CategoricalIndex : Index of :class:`Categorical` s.\n 351 |     MultiIndex : A multi-level, or hierarchical Index.\n 352 |     IntervalIndex : An Index of :class:`Interval` s.\n 353 |     DatetimeIndex : Index of datetime64 data.\n 354 |     TimedeltaIndex : Index of timedelta64 data.\n 355 |     PeriodIndex : Index of Period data.\n 356 | \n 357 |     Notes\n 358 |     -----\n 359 |     An Index instance can **only** contain hashable objects.\n 360 |     An Index instance *can not* hold numpy float16 dtype.\n 361 | \n 362 |     Examples\n 363 |     --------\n 364 |     >>> pd.Index([1, 2, 3])\n 365 |     Index([1, 2, 3], dtype='int64')\n 366 | \n 367 |     >>> pd.Index(list(\"abc\"))\n 368 |     Index(['a', 'b', 'c'], dtype='object')\n 369 | \n 370 |     >>> pd.Index([1, 2, 3], dtype=\"uint8\")\n 371 |     Index([1, 2, 3], dtype='uint8')\n 372 |     \"\"\"\n 373 | \n 374 |     # similar to __array_priority__, positions Index after Series and DataFrame\n 375 |     #  but before ExtensionArray.  Should NOT be overridden by subclasses.\n 376 |     __pandas_priority__ = 2000\n 377 | \n 378 |     # Cython methods; see github.com/cython/cython/issues/2647\n 379 |     #  for why we need to wrap these instead of making them class attributes\n 380 |     # Moreover, cython will choose the appropriate-dtyped sub-function\n 381 |     #  given the dtypes of the passed arguments\n 382 | \n 383 |     @final\n 384 |     def _left_indexer_unique(self, other: Self) -> npt.NDArray[np.intp]:\n 385 |         # Caller is responsible for ensuring other.dtype == self.dtype\n 386 |         sv = self._get_join_target()\n 387 |         ov = other._get_join_target()\n 388 |         # similar but not identical to ov.searchsorted(sv)\n 389 |         return libjoin.left_join_indexer_unique(sv, ov)\n 390 | \n 391 |     @final\n 392 |     def _left_indexer(\n 393 |         self, other: Self\n 394 |     ) -> tuple[ArrayLike, npt.NDArray[np.intp], npt.NDArray[np.intp]]:\n 395 |         # Caller is responsible for ensuring other.dtype == self.dtype\n 396 |         sv = self._get_join_target()\n 397 |         ov = other._get_join_target()\n 398 |         joined_ndarray, lidx, ridx = libjoin.left_join_indexer(sv, ov)\n 399 |         joined = self._from_join_target(joined_ndarray)\n 400 |         return joined, lidx, ridx\n 401 | \n 402 |     @final\n 403 |     def _inner_indexer(\n 404 |         self, other: Self\n 405 |     ) -> tuple[ArrayLike, npt.NDArray[np.intp], npt.NDArray[np.intp]]:\n 406 |         # Caller is responsible for ensuring other.dtype == self.dtype\n 407 |         sv = self._get_join_target()\n 408 |         ov = other._get_join_target()\n 409 |         joined_ndarray, lidx, ridx = libjoin.inner_join_indexer(sv, ov)\n 410 |         joined = self._from_join_target(joined_ndarray)\n 411 |         return joined, lidx, ridx\n 412 | \n 413 |     @final\n 414 |     def _outer_indexer(\n 415 |         self, other: Self\n 416 |     ) -> tuple[ArrayLike, npt.NDArray[np.intp], npt.NDArray[np.intp]]:\n 417 |         # Caller is responsible for ensuring other.dtype == self.dtype\n 418 |         sv = self._get_join_target()\n 419 |         ov = other._get_join_target()\n 420 |         joined_ndarray, lidx, ridx = libjoin.outer_join_indexer(sv, ov)\n 421 |         joined = self._from_join_target(joined_ndarray)\n 422 |         return joined, lidx, ridx\n 423 | \n 424 |     _typ: str = \"index\"\n 425 |     _data: ExtensionArray | np.ndarray\n 426 |     _data_cls: type[ExtensionArray] | tuple[type[np.ndarray], type[ExtensionArray]] = (\n 427 |         np.ndarray,\n 428 |         ExtensionArray,\n 429 |     )\n 430 |     _id: object | None = None\n 431 |     _name: Hashable = None\n 432 |     # MultiIndex.levels previously allowed setting the index name. We\n 433 |     # don't allow this anymore, and raise if it happens rather than\n 434 |     # failing silently.\n 435 |     _no_setting_name: bool = False\n 436 |     _comparables: list[str] = [\"name\"]\n 437 |     _attributes: list[str] = [\"name\"]\n 438 | \n 439 |     @cache_readonly\n 440 |     def _can_hold_strings(self) -> bool:\n 441 |         return not is_numeric_dtype(self.dtype)\n 442 | \n 443 |     _engine_types: dict[np.dtype | ExtensionDtype, type[libindex.IndexEngine]] = {\n 444 |         np.dtype(np.int8): libindex.Int8Engine,\n 445 |         np.dtype(np.int16): libindex.Int16Engine,\n 446 |         np.dtype(np.int32): libindex.Int32Engine,\n 447 |         np.dtype(np.int64): libindex.Int64Engine,\n 448 |         np.dtype(np.uint8): libindex.UInt8Engine,\n 449 |         np.dtype(np.uint16): libindex.UInt16Engine,\n 450 |         np.dtype(np.uint32): libindex.UInt32Engine,\n 451 |         np.dtype(np.uint64): libindex.UInt64Engine,\n 452 |         np.dtype(np.float32): libindex.Float32Engine,\n 453 |         np.dtype(np.float64): libindex.Float64Engine,\n 454 |         np.dtype(np.complex64): libindex.Complex64Engine,\n 455 |         np.dtype(np.complex128): libindex.Complex128Engine,\n 456 |     }\n 457 | \n 458 |     @property\n 459 |     def _engine_type(\n 460 |         self,\n 461 |     ) -> type[libindex.IndexEngine | libindex.ExtensionEngine]:\n 462 |         return self._engine_types.get(self.dtype, libindex.ObjectEngine)\n 463 | \n 464 |     # whether we support partial string indexing. Overridden\n 465 |     # in DatetimeIndex and PeriodIndex\n 466 |     _supports_partial_string_indexing = False\n 467 | \n 468 |     _accessors = {\"str\"}\n 469 | \n 470 |     str = Accessor(\"str\", StringMethods)\n 471 | \n 472 |     _references = None\n 473 | \n 474 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n 475 |     # Constructors\n 476 | \n 477 |     def __new__(\n 478 |         cls,\n 479 |         data=None,\n 480 |         dtype=None,\n 481 |         copy: bool = False,\n 482 |         name=None,\n 483 |         tupleize_cols: bool = True,\n 484 |     ) -> Self:\n 485 |         from pandas.core.indexes.range import RangeIndex\n 486 | \n 487 |         name = maybe_extract_name(name, data, cls)\n 488 | \n 489 |         if dtype is not None:\n 490 |             dtype = pandas_dtype(dtype)\n 491 | \n 492 |         data_dtype = getattr(data, \"dtype\", None)\n 493 | \n 494 |         refs = None\n 495 |         if not copy and isinstance(data, (ABCSeries, Index)):\n 496 |             refs = data._references\n 497 | \n 498 |         # range\n 499 |         if isinstance(data, (range, RangeIndex)):\n 500 |             result = RangeIndex(start=data, copy=copy, name=name)\n 501 |             if dtype is not None:\n 502 |                 return result.astype(dtype, copy=False)\n 503 |             # error: Incompatible return value type (got \"MultiIndex\",\n 504 |             # expected \"Self\")\n 505 |             return result  # type: ignore[return-value]\n 506 | \n 507 |         elif is_ea_or_datetimelike_dtype(dtype):\n 508 |             # non-EA dtype indexes have special casting logic, so we punt here\n 509 |             if isinstance(data, (set, frozenset)):\n 510 |                 data = list(data)\n 511 | \n 512 |         elif is_ea_or_datetimelike_dtype(data_dtype):\n 513 |             pass\n 514 | \n 515 |         elif isinstance(data, (np.ndarray, ABCMultiIndex)):\n 516 |             if isinstance(data, ABCMultiIndex):\n 517 |                 data = data._values\n 518 | \n 519 |             if data.dtype.kind not in \"iufcbmM\":\n 520 |                 # GH#11836 we need to avoid having numpy coerce\n 521 |                 # things that look like ints/floats to ints unless\n 522 |                 # they are actually ints, e.g. '0' and 0.0\n 523 |                 # should not be coerced\n 524 |                 data = com.asarray_tuplesafe(data, dtype=_dtype_obj)\n 525 |         elif isinstance(data, (ABCSeries, Index)):\n 526 |             # GH 56244: Avoid potential inference on object types\n 527 |             pass\n 528 |         elif is_scalar(data):\n 529 |             raise cls._raise_scalar_data_error(data)\n 530 |         elif hasattr(data, \"__array__\"):\n 531 |             return cls(np.asarray(data), dtype=dtype, copy=copy, name=name)\n 532 |         elif not is_list_like(data) and not isinstance(data, memoryview):\n 533 |             # 2022-11-16 the memoryview check is only necessary on some CI\n 534 |             #  builds, not clear why\n 535 |             raise cls._raise_scalar_data_error(data)\n 536 | \n 537 |         else:\n 538 |             if tupleize_cols:\n 539 |                 # GH21470: convert iterable to list before determining if empty\n 540 |                 if is_iterator(data):\n 541 |                     data = list(data)\n 542 | \n 543 |                 if data and all(isinstance(e, tuple) for e in data):\n 544 |                     # we must be all tuples, otherwise don't construct\n 545 |                     # 10697\n 546 |                     from pandas.core.indexes.multi import MultiIndex\n 547 | \n 548 |                     # error: Incompatible return value type (got \"MultiIndex\",\n 549 |                     # expected \"Self\")\n 550 |                     return MultiIndex.from_tuples(  # type: ignore[return-value]\n 551 |                         data, names=name\n 552 |                     )\n 553 |             # other iterable of some kind\n 554 | \n 555 |             if not isinstance(data, (list, tuple)):\n 556 |                 # we allow set/frozenset, which Series/sanitize_array does not, so\n 557 |                 #  cast to list here\n 558 |                 data = list(data)\n 559 |             if len(data) == 0:\n 560 |                 # unlike Series, we default to object dtype:\n 561 |                 data = np.array(data, dtype=object)\n 562 | \n 563 |             if len(data) and isinstance(data[0], tuple):\n 564 |                 # Ensure we get 1-D array of tuples instead of 2D array.\n 565 |                 data = com.asarray_tuplesafe(data, dtype=_dtype_obj)\n 566 | \n 567 |         try:\n 568 |             arr = sanitize_array(data, None, dtype=dtype, copy=copy)\n 569 |         except ValueError as err:\n 570 |             if \"index must be specified when data is not list-like\" in str(err):\n 571 |                 raise cls._raise_scalar_data_error(data) from err\n 572 |             if \"Data must be 1-dimensional\" in str(err):\n 573 |                 raise ValueError(\"Index data must be 1-dimensional\") from err\n 574 |             raise\n 575 |         arr = ensure_wrapped_if_datetimelike(arr)\n 576 | \n 577 |         klass = cls._dtype_to_subclass(arr.dtype)\n 578 | \n 579 |         arr = klass._ensure_array(arr, arr.dtype, copy=False)\n 580 |         return klass._simple_new(arr, name, refs=refs)\n 581 | \n 582 |     @classmethod\n 583 |     def _ensure_array(cls, data, dtype, copy: bool):\n 584 |         \"\"\"\n 585 |         Ensure we have a valid array to pass to _simple_new.\n 586 |         \"\"\"\n 587 |         if data.ndim > 1:\n 588 |             # GH#13601, GH#20285, GH#27125\n 589 |             raise ValueError(\"Index data must be 1-dimensional\")\n 590 |         elif dtype == np.float16:\n 591 |             # float16 not supported (no indexing engine)\n 592 |             raise NotImplementedError(\"float16 indexes are not supported\")\n 593 | \n 594 |         if copy:\n 595 |             # asarray_tuplesafe does not always copy underlying data,\n 596 |             #  so need to make sure that this happens\n 597 |             data = data.copy()\n 598 |         return data\n 599 | \n 600 |     @final\n 601 |     @classmethod\n 602 |     def _dtype_to_subclass(cls, dtype: DtypeObj):\n 603 |         # Delay import for perf. https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/pull/31423\n 604 | \n 605 |         if isinstance(dtype, ExtensionDtype):\n 606 |             return dtype.index_class\n 607 | \n 608 |         if dtype.kind == \"M\":\n 609 |             from pandas import DatetimeIndex\n 610 | \n 611 |             return DatetimeIndex\n 612 | \n 613 |         elif dtype.kind == \"m\":\n 614 |             from pandas import TimedeltaIndex\n 615 | \n 616 |             return TimedeltaIndex\n 617 | \n 618 |         elif dtype.kind == \"O\":\n 619 |             # NB: assuming away MultiIndex\n 620 |             return Index\n 621 | \n 622 |         elif issubclass(dtype.type, str) or is_numeric_dtype(dtype):\n 623 |             return Index\n 624 | \n 625 |         raise NotImplementedError(dtype)\n 626 | \n 627 |     # NOTE for new Index creation:\n 628 | \n 629 |     # - _simple_new: It returns new Index with the same type as the caller.\n 630 |     #   All metadata (such as name) must be provided by caller's responsibility.\n 631 |     #   Using _shallow_copy is recommended because it fills these metadata\n 632 |     #   otherwise specified.\n 633 | \n 634 |     # - _shallow_copy: It returns new Index with the same type (using\n 635 |     #   _simple_new), but fills caller's metadata otherwise specified. Passed\n 636 |     #   kwargs will overwrite corresponding metadata.\n 637 | \n 638 |     # See each method's docstring.\n 639 | \n 640 |     @classmethod\n 641 |     def _simple_new(\n 642 |         cls, values: ArrayLike, name: Hashable | None = None, refs=None\n 643 |     ) -> Self:\n 644 |         \"\"\"\n 645 |         We require that we have a dtype compat for the values. If we are passed\n 646 |         a non-dtype compat, then coerce using the constructor.\n 647 | \n 648 |         Must be careful not to recurse.\n 649 |         \"\"\"\n 650 |         assert isinstance(values, cls._data_cls), type(values)\n 651 | \n 652 |         result = object.__new__(cls)\n 653 |         result._data = values\n 654 |         result._name = name\n 655 |         result._cache = {}\n 656 |         result._reset_identity()\n 657 |         if refs is not None:\n 658 |             result._references = refs\n 659 |         else:\n 660 |             result._references = BlockValuesRefs()\n 661 |         result._references.add_index_reference(result)\n 662 | \n 663 |         return result\n 664 | \n 665 |     @classmethod\n 666 |     def _with_infer(cls, *args, **kwargs):\n 667 |         \"\"\"\n 668 |         Constructor that uses the 1.0.x behavior inferring numeric dtypes\n 669 |         for ndarray[object] inputs.\n 670 |         \"\"\"\n 671 |         result = cls(*args, **kwargs)\n 672 | \n 673 |         if result.dtype == _dtype_obj and not result._is_multi:\n 674 |             # error: Argument 1 to \"maybe_convert_objects\" has incompatible type\n 675 |             # \"Union[ExtensionArray, ndarray[Any, Any]]\"; expected\n 676 |             # \"ndarray[Any, Any]\"\n 677 |             values = lib.maybe_convert_objects(result._values)  # type: ignore[arg-type]\n 678 |             if values.dtype.kind in \"iufb\":\n 679 |                 return Index(values, name=result.name)\n 680 | \n 681 |         return result\n 682 | \n 683 |     @cache_readonly\n 684 |     def _constructor(self) -> type[Self]:\n 685 |         return type(self)\n 686 | \n 687 |     @final\n 688 |     def _maybe_check_unique(self) -> None:\n 689 |         \"\"\"\n 690 |         Check that an Index has no duplicates.\n 691 | \n 692 |         This is typically only called via\n 693 |         `NDFrame.flags.allows_duplicate_labels.setter` when it's set to\n 694 |         True (duplicates aren't allowed).\n 695 | \n 696 |         Raises\n 697 |         ------\n 698 |         DuplicateLabelError\n 699 |             When the index is not unique.\n 700 |         \"\"\"\n 701 |         if not self.is_unique:\n 702 |             msg = \"\"\"Index has duplicates.\"\"\"\n 703 |             duplicates = self._format_duplicate_message()\n 704 |             msg += f\"\\n{duplicates}\"\n 705 | \n 706 |             raise DuplicateLabelError(msg)\n 707 | \n 708 |     @final\n 709 |     def _format_duplicate_message(self) -> DataFrame:\n 710 |         \"\"\"\n 711 |         Construct the DataFrame for a DuplicateLabelError.\n 712 | \n 713 |         This returns a DataFrame indicating the labels and positions\n 714 |         of duplicates in an index. This should only be called when it's\n 715 |         already known that duplicates are present.\n 716 | \n 717 |         Examples\n 718 |         --------\n 719 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"a\", \"b\", \"a\"])\n 720 |         >>> idx._format_duplicate_message()\n 721 |             positions\n 722 |         label\n 723 |         a        [0, 2]\n 724 |         \"\"\"\n 725 |         from pandas import Series\n 726 | \n 727 |         duplicates = self[self.duplicated(keep=\"first\")].unique()\n 728 |         assert len(duplicates)\n 729 | \n 730 |         out = (\n 731 |             Series(np.arange(len(self)), copy=False)\n 732 |             .groupby(self, observed=False)\n 733 |             .agg(list)[duplicates]\n 734 |         )\n 735 |         if self._is_multi:\n 736 |             # test_format_duplicate_labels_message_multi\n 737 |             # error: \"Type[Index]\" has no attribute \"from_tuples\"  [attr-defined]\n 738 |             out.index = type(self).from_tuples(out.index)  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n 739 | \n 740 |         if self.nlevels == 1:\n 741 |             out = out.rename_axis(\"label\")\n 742 |         return out.to_frame(name=\"positions\")\n 743 | \n 744 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n 745 |     # Index Internals Methods\n 746 | \n 747 |     def _shallow_copy(self, values, name: Hashable = no_default) -> Self:\n 748 |         \"\"\"\n 749 |         Create a new Index with the same class as the caller, don't copy the\n 750 |         data, use the same object attributes with passed in attributes taking\n 751 |         precedence.\n 752 | \n 753 |         *this is an internal non-public method*\n 754 | \n 755 |         Parameters\n 756 |         ----------\n 757 |         values : the values to create the new Index, optional\n 758 |         name : Label, defaults to self.name\n 759 |         \"\"\"\n 760 |         name = self._name if name is no_default else name\n 761 | \n 762 |         return self._simple_new(values, name=name, refs=self._references)\n 763 | \n 764 |     def _view(self) -> Self:\n 765 |         \"\"\"\n 766 |         fastpath to make a shallow copy, i.e. new object with same data.\n 767 |         \"\"\"\n 768 |         result = self._simple_new(self._values, name=self._name, refs=self._references)\n 769 | \n 770 |         result._cache = self._cache\n 771 |         return result\n 772 | \n 773 |     @final\n 774 |     def _rename(self, name: Hashable) -> Self:\n 775 |         \"\"\"\n 776 |         fastpath for rename if new name is already validated.\n 777 |         \"\"\"\n 778 |         result = self._view()\n 779 |         result._name = name\n 780 |         return result\n 781 | \n 782 |     @final\n 783 |     def is_(self, other) -> bool:\n 784 |         \"\"\"\n 785 |         More flexible, faster check like ``is`` but that works through views.\n 786 | \n 787 |         Note: this is *not* the same as ``Index.identical()``, which checks\n 788 |         that metadata is also the same.\n 789 | \n 790 |         Parameters\n 791 |         ----------\n 792 |         other : object\n 793 |             Other object to compare against.\n 794 | \n 795 |         Returns\n 796 |         -------\n 797 |         bool\n 798 |             True if both have same underlying data, False otherwise.\n 799 | \n 800 |         See Also\n 801 |         --------\n 802 |         Index.identical : Works like ``Index.is_`` but also checks metadata.\n 803 | \n 804 |         Examples\n 805 |         --------\n 806 |         >>> idx1 = pd.Index([\"1\", \"2\", \"3\"])\n 807 |         >>> idx1.is_(idx1.view())\n 808 |         True\n 809 | \n 810 |         >>> idx1.is_(idx1.copy())\n 811 |         False\n 812 |         \"\"\"\n 813 |         if self is other:\n 814 |             return True\n 815 |         elif not hasattr(other, \"_id\"):\n 816 |             return False\n 817 |         elif self._id is None or other._id is None:\n 818 |             return False\n 819 |         else:\n 820 |             return self._id is other._id\n 821 | \n 822 |     @final\n 823 |     def _reset_identity(self) -> None:\n 824 |         \"\"\"\n 825 |         Initializes or resets ``_id`` attribute with new object.\n 826 |         \"\"\"\n 827 |         self._id = object()\n 828 | \n 829 |     @final\n 830 |     def _cleanup(self) -> None:\n 831 |         if \"_engine\" in self._cache:\n 832 |             self._engine.clear_mapping()\n 833 | \n 834 |     @cache_readonly\n 835 |     def _engine(\n 836 |         self,\n 837 |     ) -> libindex.IndexEngine | libindex.ExtensionEngine | libindex.MaskedIndexEngine:\n 838 |         # For base class (object dtype) we get ObjectEngine\n 839 |         target_values = self._get_engine_target()\n 840 | \n 841 |         if isinstance(self._values, ArrowExtensionArray) and self.dtype.kind in \"Mm\":\n 842 |             import pyarrow as pa\n 843 | \n 844 |             pa_type = self._values._pa_array.type\n 845 |             if pa.types.is_timestamp(pa_type):\n 846 |                 target_values = self._values._to_datetimearray()\n 847 |                 return libindex.DatetimeEngine(target_values._ndarray)\n 848 |             elif pa.types.is_duration(pa_type):\n 849 |                 target_values = self._values._to_timedeltaarray()\n 850 |                 return libindex.TimedeltaEngine(target_values._ndarray)\n 851 | \n 852 |         if isinstance(target_values, ExtensionArray):\n 853 |             if isinstance(target_values, (BaseMaskedArray, ArrowExtensionArray)):\n 854 |                 try:\n 855 |                     return _masked_engines[target_values.dtype.name](target_values)\n 856 |                 except KeyError:\n 857 |                     # Not supported yet e.g. decimal\n 858 |                     pass\n 859 |             elif self._engine_type is libindex.ObjectEngine:\n 860 |                 return libindex.ExtensionEngine(target_values)\n 861 | \n 862 |         target_values = cast(np.ndarray, target_values)\n 863 |         # to avoid a reference cycle, bind `target_values` to a local variable, so\n 864 |         # `self` is not passed into the lambda.\n 865 |         if target_values.dtype == bool:\n 866 |             return libindex.BoolEngine(target_values)\n 867 |         elif target_values.dtype == np.complex64:\n 868 |             return libindex.Complex64Engine(target_values)\n 869 |         elif target_values.dtype == np.complex128:\n 870 |             return libindex.Complex128Engine(target_values)\n 871 |         elif needs_i8_conversion(self.dtype):\n 872 |             # We need to keep M8/m8 dtype when initializing the Engine,\n 873 |             #  but don't want to change _get_engine_target bc it is used\n 874 |             #  elsewhere\n 875 |             # error: Item \"ExtensionArray\" of \"Union[ExtensionArray,\n 876 |             # ndarray[Any, Any]]\" has no attribute \"_ndarray\"  [union-attr]\n 877 |             target_values = self._data._ndarray  # type: ignore[union-attr]\n 878 |         elif is_string_dtype(self.dtype) and not is_object_dtype(self.dtype):\n 879 |             return libindex.StringObjectEngine(target_values, self.dtype.na_value)  # type: ignore[union-attr]\n 880 | \n 881 |         # error: Argument 1 to \"ExtensionEngine\" has incompatible type\n 882 |         # \"ndarray[Any, Any]\"; expected \"ExtensionArray\"\n 883 |         return self._engine_type(target_values)  # type: ignore[arg-type]\n 884 | \n 885 |     @final\n 886 |     @cache_readonly\n 887 |     def _dir_additions_for_owner(self) -> set[str_t]:\n 888 |         \"\"\"\n 889 |         Add the string-like labels to the owner dataframe/series dir output.\n 890 | \n 891 |         If this is a MultiIndex, it's first level values are used.\n 892 |         \"\"\"\n 893 |         return {\n 894 |             c\n 895 |             for c in self.unique(level=0)[: get_option(\"display.max_dir_items\")]\n 896 |             if isinstance(c, str) and c.isidentifier()\n 897 |         }\n 898 | \n 899 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n 900 |     # Array-Like Methods\n 901 | \n 902 |     # ndarray compat\n 903 |     def __len__(self) -> int:\n 904 |         \"\"\"\n 905 |         Return the length of the Index.\n 906 |         \"\"\"\n 907 |         return len(self._data)\n 908 | \n 909 |     def __array__(self, dtype=None, copy=None) -> np.ndarray:\n 910 |         \"\"\"\n 911 |         The array interface, return my values.\n 912 |         \"\"\"\n 913 |         if copy is None:\n 914 |             # Note, that the if branch exists for NumPy 1.x support\n 915 |             return np.asarray(self._data, dtype=dtype)\n 916 | \n 917 |         return np.array(self._data, dtype=dtype, copy=copy)\n 918 | \n 919 |     def __array_ufunc__(self, ufunc: np.ufunc, method: str_t, *inputs, **kwargs):\n 920 |         if any(isinstance(other, (ABCSeries, ABCDataFrame)) for other in inputs):\n 921 |             return NotImplemented\n 922 | \n 923 |         result = arraylike.maybe_dispatch_ufunc_to_dunder_op(\n 924 |             self, ufunc, method, *inputs, **kwargs\n 925 |         )\n 926 |         if result is not NotImplemented:\n 927 |             return result\n 928 | \n 929 |         if \"out\" in kwargs:\n 930 |             # e.g. test_dti_isub_tdi\n 931 |             return arraylike.dispatch_ufunc_with_out(\n 932 |                 self, ufunc, method, *inputs, **kwargs\n 933 |             )\n 934 | \n 935 |         if method == \"reduce\":\n 936 |             result = arraylike.dispatch_reduction_ufunc(\n 937 |                 self, ufunc, method, *inputs, **kwargs\n 938 |             )\n 939 |             if result is not NotImplemented:\n 940 |                 return result\n 941 | \n 942 |         new_inputs = [x if x is not self else x._values for x in inputs]\n 943 |         result = getattr(ufunc, method)(*new_inputs, **kwargs)\n 944 |         if ufunc.nout == 2:\n 945 |             # i.e. np.divmod, np.modf, np.frexp\n 946 |             return tuple(self.__array_wrap__(x) for x in result)\n 947 |         elif method == \"reduce\":\n 948 |             result = lib.item_from_zerodim(result)\n 949 |             return result\n 950 |         elif is_scalar(result):\n 951 |             # e.g. matmul\n 952 |             return result\n 953 | \n 954 |         if result.dtype == np.float16:\n 955 |             result = result.astype(np.float32)\n 956 | \n 957 |         return self.__array_wrap__(result)\n 958 | \n 959 |     @final\n 960 |     def __array_wrap__(self, result, context=None, return_scalar=False):\n 961 |         \"\"\"\n 962 |         Gets called after a ufunc and other functions e.g. np.split.\n 963 |         \"\"\"\n 964 |         result = lib.item_from_zerodim(result)\n 965 |         if (not isinstance(result, Index) and is_bool_dtype(result.dtype)) or np.ndim(\n 966 |             result\n 967 |         ) > 1:\n 968 |             # exclude Index to avoid warning from is_bool_dtype deprecation;\n 969 |             #  in the Index case it doesn't matter which path we go down.\n 970 |             # reached in plotting tests with e.g. np.nonzero(index)\n 971 |             return result\n 972 | \n 973 |         return Index(result, name=self.name)\n 974 | \n 975 |     @cache_readonly\n 976 |     def dtype(self) -> DtypeObj:\n 977 |         \"\"\"\n 978 |         Return the dtype object of the underlying data.\n 979 | \n 980 |         See Also\n 981 |         --------\n 982 |         Index.inferred_type: Return a string of the type inferred from the values.\n 983 | \n 984 |         Examples\n 985 |         --------\n 986 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3])\n 987 |         >>> idx\n 988 |         Index([1, 2, 3], dtype='int64')\n 989 |         >>> idx.dtype\n 990 |         dtype('int64')\n 991 |         \"\"\"\n 992 |         return self._data.dtype\n 993 | \n 994 |     @final\n 995 |     def ravel(self, order: str_t = \"C\") -> Self:\n 996 |         \"\"\"\n 997 |         Return a view on self.\n 998 | \n 999 |         Parameters\n1000 |         ----------\n1001 |         order : {'K', 'A', 'C', 'F'}, default 'C'\n1002 |             Specify the memory layout of the view. This parameter is not\n1003 |             implemented currently.\n1004 | \n1005 |         Returns\n1006 |         -------\n1007 |         Index\n1008 |             A view on self.\n1009 | \n1010 |         See Also\n1011 |         --------\n1012 |         numpy.ndarray.ravel : Return a flattened array.\n1013 | \n1014 |         Examples\n1015 |         --------\n1016 |         >>> s = pd.Series([1, 2, 3], index=[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"])\n1017 |         >>> s.index.ravel()\n1018 |         Index(['a', 'b', 'c'], dtype='object')\n1019 |         \"\"\"\n1020 |         return self[:]\n1021 | \n1022 |     def view(self, cls=None):\n1023 |         \"\"\"\n1024 |         Return a view of the Index with the specified dtype or a new Index instance.\n1025 | \n1026 |         This method returns a view of the calling Index object if no arguments are\n1027 |         provided. If a dtype is specified through the `cls` argument, it attempts\n1028 |         to return a view of the Index with the specified dtype. Note that viewing\n1029 |         the Index as a different dtype reinterprets the underlying data, which can\n1030 |         lead to unexpected results for non-numeric or incompatible dtype conversions.\n1031 | \n1032 |         Parameters\n1033 |         ----------\n1034 |         cls : data-type or ndarray sub-class, optional\n1035 |             Data-type descriptor of the returned view, e.g., float32 or int16.\n1036 |             Omitting it results in the view having the same data-type as `self`.\n1037 |             This argument can also be specified as an ndarray sub-class,\n1038 |             e.g., np.int64 or np.float32 which then specifies the type of\n1039 |             the returned object.\n1040 | \n1041 |         Returns\n1042 |         -------\n1043 |         Index or ndarray\n1044 |             A view of the Index. If `cls` is None, the returned object is an Index\n1045 |             view with the same dtype as the calling object. If a numeric `cls` is\n1046 |             specified an ndarray view with the new dtype is returned.\n1047 | \n1048 |         Raises\n1049 |         ------\n1050 |         ValueError\n1051 |             If attempting to change to a dtype in a way that is not compatible with\n1052 |             the original dtype's memory layout, for example, viewing an 'int64' Index\n1053 |             as 'str'.\n1054 | \n1055 |         See Also\n1056 |         --------\n1057 |         Index.copy : Returns a copy of the Index.\n1058 |         numpy.ndarray.view : Returns a new view of array with the same data.\n1059 | \n1060 |         Examples\n1061 |         --------\n1062 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([-1, 0, 1])\n1063 |         >>> idx.view()\n1064 |         Index([-1, 0, 1], dtype='int64')\n1065 | \n1066 |         >>> idx.view(np.uint64)\n1067 |         array([18446744073709551615,                    0,                    1],\n1068 |           dtype=uint64)\n1069 | \n1070 |         Viewing as 'int32' or 'float32' reinterprets the memory, which may lead to\n1071 |         unexpected behavior:\n1072 | \n1073 |         >>> idx.view(\"float32\")\n1074 |         array([   nan,    nan, 0.e+00, 0.e+00, 1.e-45, 0.e+00], dtype=float32)\n1075 |         \"\"\"\n1076 |         # we need to see if we are subclassing an\n1077 |         # index type here\n1078 |         if cls is not None:\n1079 |             dtype = cls\n1080 |             if isinstance(cls, str):\n1081 |                 dtype = pandas_dtype(cls)\n1082 | \n1083 |             if needs_i8_conversion(dtype):\n1084 |                 idx_cls = self._dtype_to_subclass(dtype)\n1085 |                 arr = self.array.view(dtype)\n1086 |                 if isinstance(arr, ExtensionArray):\n1087 |                     # here we exclude non-supported dt64/td64 dtypes\n1088 |                     return idx_cls._simple_new(\n1089 |                         arr, name=self.name, refs=self._references\n1090 |                     )\n1091 |                 return arr\n1092 | \n1093 |             result = self._data.view(cls)\n1094 |         else:\n1095 |             result = self._view()\n1096 |         if isinstance(result, Index):\n1097 |             result._id = self._id\n1098 |         return result\n1099 | \n1100 |     def astype(self, dtype: Dtype, copy: bool = True):\n1101 |         \"\"\"\n1102 |         Create an Index with values cast to dtypes.\n1103 | \n1104 |         The class of a new Index is determined by dtype. When conversion is\n1105 |         impossible, a TypeError exception is raised.\n1106 | \n1107 |         Parameters\n1108 |         ----------\n1109 |         dtype : numpy dtype or pandas type\n1110 |             Note that any signed integer `dtype` is treated as ``'int64'``,\n1111 |             and any unsigned integer `dtype` is treated as ``'uint64'``,\n1112 |             regardless of the size.\n1113 |         copy : bool, default True\n1114 |             By default, astype always returns a newly allocated object.\n1115 |             If copy is set to False and internal requirements on dtype are\n1116 |             satisfied, the original data is used to create a new Index\n1117 |             or the original Index is returned.\n1118 | \n1119 |         Returns\n1120 |         -------\n1121 |         Index\n1122 |             Index with values cast to specified dtype.\n1123 | \n1124 |         See Also\n1125 |         --------\n1126 |         Index.dtype: Return the dtype object of the underlying data.\n1127 |         Index.dtypes: Return the dtype object of the underlying data.\n1128 |         Index.convert_dtypes: Convert columns to the best possible dtypes.\n1129 | \n1130 |         Examples\n1131 |         --------\n1132 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3])\n1133 |         >>> idx\n1134 |         Index([1, 2, 3], dtype='int64')\n1135 |         >>> idx.astype(\"float\")\n1136 |         Index([1.0, 2.0, 3.0], dtype='float64')\n1137 |         \"\"\"\n1138 |         if dtype is not None:\n1139 |             dtype = pandas_dtype(dtype)\n1140 | \n1141 |         if self.dtype == dtype:\n1142 |             # Ensure that self.astype(self.dtype) is self\n1143 |             return self.copy() if copy else self\n1144 | \n1145 |         values = self._data\n1146 |         if isinstance(values, ExtensionArray):\n1147 |             with rewrite_exception(type(values).__name__, type(self).__name__):\n1148 |                 new_values = values.astype(dtype, copy=copy)\n1149 | \n1150 |         elif isinstance(dtype, ExtensionDtype):\n1151 |             cls = dtype.construct_array_type()\n1152 |             # Note: for RangeIndex and CategoricalDtype self vs self._values\n1153 |             #  behaves differently here.\n1154 |             new_values = cls._from_sequence(self, dtype=dtype, copy=copy)\n1155 | \n1156 |         else:\n1157 |             # GH#13149 specifically use astype_array instead of astype\n1158 |             new_values = astype_array(values, dtype=dtype, copy=copy)\n1159 | \n1160 |         # pass copy=False because any copying will be done in the astype above\n1161 |         result = Index(new_values, name=self.name, dtype=new_values.dtype, copy=False)\n1162 |         if (\n1163 |             not copy\n1164 |             and self._references is not None\n1165 |             and astype_is_view(self.dtype, dtype)\n1166 |         ):\n1167 |             result._references = self._references\n1168 |             result._references.add_index_reference(result)\n1169 |         return result\n1170 | \n1171 |     _index_shared_docs[\"take\"] = \"\"\"\n1172 |         Return a new %(klass)s of the values selected by the indices.\n1173 | \n1174 |         For internal compatibility with numpy arrays.\n1175 | \n1176 |         Parameters\n1177 |         ----------\n1178 |         indices : array-like\n1179 |             Indices to be taken.\n1180 |         axis : int, optional\n1181 |             The axis over which to select values, always 0.\n1182 |         allow_fill : bool, default True\n1183 |             How to handle negative values in `indices`.\n1184 | \n1185 |             * False: negative values in `indices` indicate positional indices\n1186 |               from the right (the default). This is similar to\n1187 |               :func:`numpy.take`.\n1188 | \n1189 |             * True: negative values in `indices` indicate\n1190 |               missing values. These values are set to `fill_value`. Any other\n1191 |               other negative values raise a ``ValueError``.\n1192 | \n1193 |         fill_value : scalar, default None\n1194 |             If allow_fill=True and fill_value is not None, indices specified by\n1195 |             -1 are regarded as NA. If Index doesn't hold NA, raise ValueError.\n1196 |         **kwargs\n1197 |             Required for compatibility with numpy.\n1198 | \n1199 |         Returns\n1200 |         -------\n1201 |         Index\n1202 |             An index formed of elements at the given indices. Will be the same\n1203 |             type as self, except for RangeIndex.\n1204 | \n1205 |         See Also\n1206 |         --------\n1207 |         numpy.ndarray.take: Return an array formed from the\n1208 |             elements of a at the given indices.\n1209 | \n1210 |         Examples\n1211 |         --------\n1212 |         >>> idx = pd.Index(['a', 'b', 'c'])\n1213 |         >>> idx.take([2, 2, 1, 2])\n1214 |         Index(['c', 'c', 'b', 'c'], dtype='object')\n1215 |         \"\"\"\n1216 | \n1217 |     @Appender(_index_shared_docs[\"take\"] % _index_doc_kwargs)\n1218 |     def take(\n1219 |         self,\n1220 |         indices,\n1221 |         axis: Axis = 0,\n1222 |         allow_fill: bool = True,\n1223 |         fill_value=None,\n1224 |         **kwargs,\n1225 |     ) -> Self:\n1226 |         if kwargs:\n1227 |             nv.validate_take((), kwargs)\n1228 |         if is_scalar(indices):\n1229 |             raise TypeError(\"Expected indices to be array-like\")\n1230 |         indices = ensure_platform_int(indices)\n1231 |         allow_fill = self._maybe_disallow_fill(allow_fill, fill_value, indices)\n1232 | \n1233 |         if indices.ndim == 1 and lib.is_range_indexer(indices, len(self)):\n1234 |             return self.copy()\n1235 | \n1236 |         # Note: we discard fill_value and use self._na_value, only relevant\n1237 |         #  in the case where allow_fill is True and fill_value is not None\n1238 |         values = self._values\n1239 |         if isinstance(values, np.ndarray):\n1240 |             taken = algos.take(\n1241 |                 values, indices, allow_fill=allow_fill, fill_value=self._na_value\n1242 |             )\n1243 |         else:\n1244 |             # algos.take passes 'axis' keyword which not all EAs accept\n1245 |             taken = values.take(\n1246 |                 indices, allow_fill=allow_fill, fill_value=self._na_value\n1247 |             )\n1248 |         return self._constructor._simple_new(taken, name=self.name)\n1249 | \n1250 |     @final\n1251 |     def _maybe_disallow_fill(self, allow_fill: bool, fill_value, indices) -> bool:\n1252 |         \"\"\"\n1253 |         We only use pandas-style take when allow_fill is True _and_\n1254 |         fill_value is not None.\n1255 |         \"\"\"\n1256 |         if allow_fill and fill_value is not None:\n1257 |             # only fill if we are passing a non-None fill_value\n1258 |             if self._can_hold_na:\n1259 |                 if (indices < -1).any():\n1260 |                     raise ValueError(\n1261 |                         \"When allow_fill=True and fill_value is not None, \"\n1262 |                         \"all indices must be >= -1\"\n1263 |                     )\n1264 |             else:\n1265 |                 cls_name = type(self).__name__\n1266 |                 raise ValueError(\n1267 |                     f\"Unable to fill values because {cls_name} cannot contain NA\"\n1268 |                 )\n1269 |         else:\n1270 |             allow_fill = False\n1271 |         return allow_fill\n1272 | \n1273 |     _index_shared_docs[\"repeat\"] = \"\"\"\n1274 |         Repeat elements of a %(klass)s.\n1275 | \n1276 |         Returns a new %(klass)s where each element of the current %(klass)s\n1277 |         is repeated consecutively a given number of times.\n1278 | \n1279 |         Parameters\n1280 |         ----------\n1281 |         repeats : int or array of ints\n1282 |             The number of repetitions for each element. This should be a\n1283 |             non-negative integer. Repeating 0 times will return an empty\n1284 |             %(klass)s.\n1285 |         axis : None\n1286 |             Must be ``None``. Has no effect but is accepted for compatibility\n1287 |             with numpy.\n1288 | \n1289 |         Returns\n1290 |         -------\n1291 |         %(klass)s\n1292 |             Newly created %(klass)s with repeated elements.\n1293 | \n1294 |         See Also\n1295 |         --------\n1296 |         Series.repeat : Equivalent function for Series.\n1297 |         numpy.repeat : Similar method for :class:`numpy.ndarray`.\n1298 | \n1299 |         Examples\n1300 |         --------\n1301 |         >>> idx = pd.Index(['a', 'b', 'c'])\n1302 |         >>> idx\n1303 |         Index(['a', 'b', 'c'], dtype='object')\n1304 |         >>> idx.repeat(2)\n1305 |         Index(['a', 'a', 'b', 'b', 'c', 'c'], dtype='object')\n1306 |         >>> idx.repeat([1, 2, 3])\n1307 |         Index(['a', 'b', 'b', 'c', 'c', 'c'], dtype='object')\n1308 |         \"\"\"\n1309 | \n1310 |     @Appender(_index_shared_docs[\"repeat\"] % _index_doc_kwargs)\n1311 |     def repeat(self, repeats, axis: None = None) -> Self:\n1312 |         repeats = ensure_platform_int(repeats)\n1313 |         nv.validate_repeat((), {\"axis\": axis})\n1314 |         res_values = self._values.repeat(repeats)\n1315 | \n1316 |         # _constructor so RangeIndex-> Index with an int64 dtype\n1317 |         return self._constructor._simple_new(res_values, name=self.name)\n1318 | \n1319 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n1320 |     # Copying Methods\n1321 | \n1322 |     def copy(\n1323 |         self,\n1324 |         name: Hashable | None = None,\n1325 |         deep: bool = False,\n1326 |     ) -> Self:\n1327 |         \"\"\"\n1328 |         Make a copy of this object.\n1329 | \n1330 |         Name is set on the new object.\n1331 | \n1332 |         Parameters\n1333 |         ----------\n1334 |         name : Label, optional\n1335 |             Set name for new object.\n1336 |         deep : bool, default False\n1337 |             If True attempts to make a deep copy of the Index.\n1338 |                 Else makes a shallow copy.\n1339 | \n1340 |         Returns\n1341 |         -------\n1342 |         Index\n1343 |             Index refer to new object which is a copy of this object.\n1344 | \n1345 |         See Also\n1346 |         --------\n1347 |         Index.delete: Make new Index with passed location(-s) deleted.\n1348 |         Index.drop: Make new Index with passed list of labels deleted.\n1349 | \n1350 |         Notes\n1351 |         -----\n1352 |         In most cases, there should be no functional difference from using\n1353 |         ``deep``, but if ``deep`` is passed it will attempt to deepcopy.\n1354 | \n1355 |         Examples\n1356 |         --------\n1357 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"])\n1358 |         >>> new_idx = idx.copy()\n1359 |         >>> idx is new_idx\n1360 |         False\n1361 |         \"\"\"\n1362 | \n1363 |         name = self._validate_names(name=name, deep=deep)[0]\n1364 |         if deep:\n1365 |             new_data = self._data.copy()\n1366 |             new_index = type(self)._simple_new(new_data, name=name)\n1367 |         else:\n1368 |             new_index = self._rename(name=name)\n1369 |         return new_index\n1370 | \n1371 |     @final\n1372 |     def __copy__(self, **kwargs) -> Self:\n1373 |         return self.copy(**kwargs)\n1374 | \n1375 |     @final\n1376 |     def __deepcopy__(self, memo=None) -> Self:\n1377 |         \"\"\"\n1378 |         Parameters\n1379 |         ----------\n1380 |         memo, default None\n1381 |             Standard signature. Unused\n1382 |         \"\"\"\n1383 |         return self.copy(deep=True)\n1384 | \n1385 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n1386 |     # Rendering Methods\n1387 | \n1388 |     @final\n1389 |     def __repr__(self) -> str_t:\n1390 |         \"\"\"\n1391 |         Return a string representation for this object.\n1392 |         \"\"\"\n1393 |         klass_name = type(self).__name__\n1394 |         data = self._format_data()\n1395 |         attrs = self._format_attrs()\n1396 |         attrs_str = [f\"{k}={v}\" for k, v in attrs]\n1397 |         prepr = \", \".join(attrs_str)\n1398 | \n1399 |         return f\"{klass_name}({data}{prepr})\"\n1400 | \n1401 |     @property\n1402 |     def _formatter_func(self):\n1403 |         \"\"\"\n1404 |         Return the formatter function.\n1405 |         \"\"\"\n1406 |         return default_pprint\n1407 | \n1408 |     @final\n1409 |     def _format_data(self, name=None) -> str_t:\n1410 |         \"\"\"\n1411 |         Return the formatted data as a unicode string.\n1412 |         \"\"\"\n1413 |         # do we want to justify (only do so for non-objects)\n1414 |         is_justify = True\n1415 | \n1416 |         if self.inferred_type == \"string\":\n1417 |             is_justify = False\n1418 |         elif isinstance(self.dtype, CategoricalDtype):\n1419 |             self = cast(\"CategoricalIndex\", self)\n1420 |             if is_object_dtype(self.categories.dtype):\n1421 |                 is_justify = False\n1422 |         elif isinstance(self, ABCRangeIndex):\n1423 |             # We will do the relevant formatting via attrs\n1424 |             return \"\"\n1425 | \n1426 |         return format_object_summary(\n1427 |             self,\n1428 |             self._formatter_func,\n1429 |             is_justify=is_justify,\n1430 |             name=name,\n1431 |             line_break_each_value=self._is_multi,\n1432 |         )\n1433 | \n1434 |     def _format_attrs(self) -> list[tuple[str_t, str_t | int | bool | None]]:\n1435 |         \"\"\"\n1436 |         Return a list of tuples of the (attr,formatted_value).\n1437 |         \"\"\"\n1438 |         attrs: list[tuple[str_t, str_t | int | bool | None]] = []\n1439 | \n1440 |         if not self._is_multi:\n1441 |             attrs.append((\"dtype\", f\"'{self.dtype}'\"))\n1442 | \n1443 |         if self.name is not None:\n1444 |             attrs.append((\"name\", default_pprint(self.name)))\n1445 |         elif self._is_multi and any(x is not None for x in self.names):\n1446 |             attrs.append((\"names\", default_pprint(self.names)))\n1447 | \n1448 |         max_seq_items = get_option(\"display.max_seq_items\") or len(self)\n1449 |         if len(self) > max_seq_items:\n1450 |             attrs.append((\"length\", len(self)))\n1451 |         return attrs\n1452 | \n1453 |     @final\n1454 |     def _get_level_names(self) -> range | Sequence[Hashable]:\n1455 |         \"\"\"\n1456 |         Return a name or list of names with None replaced by the level number.\n1457 |         \"\"\"\n1458 |         if self._is_multi:\n1459 |             return maybe_sequence_to_range(\n1460 |                 [\n1461 |                     level if name is None else name\n1462 |                     for level, name in enumerate(self.names)\n1463 |                 ]\n1464 |             )\n1465 |         else:\n1466 |             return range(1) if self.name is None else [self.name]\n1467 | \n1468 |     @final\n1469 |     def _mpl_repr(self) -> np.ndarray:\n1470 |         # how to represent ourselves to matplotlib\n1471 |         if isinstance(self.dtype, np.dtype) and self.dtype.kind != \"M\":\n1472 |             return cast(np.ndarray, self.values)\n1473 |         return self.astype(object, copy=False)._values\n1474 | \n1475 |     _default_na_rep = \"NaN\"\n1476 | \n1477 |     @final\n1478 |     def _format_flat(\n1479 |         self,\n1480 |         *,\n1481 |         include_name: bool,\n1482 |         formatter: Callable | None = None,\n1483 |     ) -> list[str_t]:\n1484 |         \"\"\"\n1485 |         Render a string representation of the Index.\n1486 |         \"\"\"\n1487 |         header = []\n1488 |         if include_name:\n1489 |             header.append(\n1490 |                 pprint_thing(self.name, escape_chars=(\"\\t\", \"\\r\", \"\\n\"))\n1491 |                 if self.name is not None\n1492 |                 else \"\"\n1493 |             )\n1494 | \n1495 |         if formatter is not None:\n1496 |             return header + list(self.map(formatter))\n1497 | \n1498 |         return self._format_with_header(header=header, na_rep=self._default_na_rep)\n1499 | \n1500 |     def _format_with_header(self, *, header: list[str_t], na_rep: str_t) -> list[str_t]:\n1501 |         from pandas.io.formats.format import format_array\n1502 | \n1503 |         values = self._values\n1504 | \n1505 |         if (\n1506 |             is_object_dtype(values.dtype)\n1507 |             or is_string_dtype(values.dtype)\n1508 |             or isinstance(self.dtype, (IntervalDtype, CategoricalDtype))\n1509 |         ):\n1510 |             # TODO: why do we need different justify for these cases?\n1511 |             justify = \"all\"\n1512 |         else:\n1513 |             justify = \"left\"\n1514 |         # passing leading_space=False breaks test_format_missing,\n1515 |         #  test_index_repr_in_frame_with_nan, but would otherwise make\n1516 |         #  trim_front unnecessary\n1517 |         formatted = format_array(values, None, justify=justify)\n1518 |         result = trim_front(formatted)\n1519 |         return header + result\n1520 | \n1521 |     def _get_values_for_csv(\n1522 |         self,\n1523 |         *,\n1524 |         na_rep: str_t = \"\",\n1525 |         decimal: str_t = \".\",\n1526 |         float_format=None,\n1527 |         date_format=None,\n1528 |         quoting=None,\n1529 |     ) -> npt.NDArray[np.object_]:\n1530 |         return get_values_for_csv(\n1531 |             self._values,\n1532 |             na_rep=na_rep,\n1533 |             decimal=decimal,\n1534 |             float_format=float_format,\n1535 |             date_format=date_format,\n1536 |             quoting=quoting,\n1537 |         )\n1538 | \n1539 |     def _summary(self, name=None) -> str_t:\n1540 |         \"\"\"\n1541 |         Return a summarized representation.\n1542 | \n1543 |         Parameters\n1544 |         ----------\n1545 |         name : str\n1546 |             name to use in the summary representation\n1547 | \n1548 |         Returns\n1549 |         -------\n1550 |         String with a summarized representation of the index\n1551 |         \"\"\"\n1552 |         if len(self) > 0:\n1553 |             head = self[0]\n1554 |             if hasattr(head, \"format\") and not isinstance(head, str):\n1555 |                 head = head.format()\n1556 |             elif needs_i8_conversion(self.dtype):\n1557 |                 # e.g. Timedelta, display as values, not quoted\n1558 |                 head = self._formatter_func(head).replace(\"'\", \"\")\n1559 |             tail = self[-1]\n1560 |             if hasattr(tail, \"format\") and not isinstance(tail, str):\n1561 |                 tail = tail.format()\n1562 |             elif needs_i8_conversion(self.dtype):\n1563 |                 # e.g. Timedelta, display as values, not quoted\n1564 |                 tail = self._formatter_func(tail).replace(\"'\", \"\")\n1565 | \n1566 |             index_summary = f\", {head} to {tail}\"\n1567 |         else:\n1568 |             index_summary = \"\"\n1569 | \n1570 |         if name is None:\n1571 |             name = type(self).__name__\n1572 |         return f\"{name}: {len(self)} entries{index_summary}\"\n1573 | \n1574 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n1575 |     # Conversion Methods\n1576 | \n1577 |     def to_flat_index(self) -> Self:\n1578 |         \"\"\"\n1579 |         Identity method.\n1580 | \n1581 |         This is implemented for compatibility with subclass implementations\n1582 |         when chaining.\n1583 | \n1584 |         Returns\n1585 |         -------\n1586 |         pd.Index\n1587 |             Caller.\n1588 | \n1589 |         See Also\n1590 |         --------\n1591 |         MultiIndex.to_flat_index : Subclass implementation.\n1592 |         \"\"\"\n1593 |         return self\n1594 | \n1595 |     @final\n1596 |     def to_series(self, index=None, name: Hashable | None = None) -> Series:\n1597 |         \"\"\"\n1598 |         Create a Series with both index and values equal to the index keys.\n1599 | \n1600 |         Useful with map for returning an indexer based on an index.\n1601 | \n1602 |         Parameters\n1603 |         ----------\n1604 |         index : Index, optional\n1605 |             Index of resulting Series. If None, defaults to original index.\n1606 |         name : str, optional\n1607 |             Name of resulting Series. If None, defaults to name of original\n1608 |             index.\n1609 | \n1610 |         Returns\n1611 |         -------\n1612 |         Series\n1613 |             The dtype will be based on the type of the Index values.\n1614 | \n1615 |         See Also\n1616 |         --------\n1617 |         Index.to_frame : Convert an Index to a DataFrame.\n1618 |         Series.to_frame : Convert Series to DataFrame.\n1619 | \n1620 |         Examples\n1621 |         --------\n1622 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"Ant\", \"Bear\", \"Cow\"], name=\"animal\")\n1623 | \n1624 |         By default, the original index and original name is reused.\n1625 | \n1626 |         >>> idx.to_series()\n1627 |         animal\n1628 |         Ant      Ant\n1629 |         Bear    Bear\n1630 |         Cow      Cow\n1631 |         Name: animal, dtype: object\n1632 | \n1633 |         To enforce a new index, specify new labels to ``index``:\n1634 | \n1635 |         >>> idx.to_series(index=[0, 1, 2])\n1636 |         0     Ant\n1637 |         1    Bear\n1638 |         2     Cow\n1639 |         Name: animal, dtype: object\n1640 | \n1641 |         To override the name of the resulting column, specify ``name``:\n1642 | \n1643 |         >>> idx.to_series(name=\"zoo\")\n1644 |         animal\n1645 |         Ant      Ant\n1646 |         Bear    Bear\n1647 |         Cow      Cow\n1648 |         Name: zoo, dtype: object\n1649 |         \"\"\"\n1650 |         from pandas import Series\n1651 | \n1652 |         if index is None:\n1653 |             index = self._view()\n1654 |         if name is None:\n1655 |             name = self.name\n1656 | \n1657 |         return Series(self._values.copy(), index=index, name=name)\n1658 | \n1659 |     def to_frame(\n1660 |         self, index: bool = True, name: Hashable = lib.no_default\n1661 |     ) -> DataFrame:\n1662 |         \"\"\"\n1663 |         Create a DataFrame with a column containing the Index.\n1664 | \n1665 |         Parameters\n1666 |         ----------\n1667 |         index : bool, default True\n1668 |             Set the index of the returned DataFrame as the original Index.\n1669 | \n1670 |         name : object, defaults to index.name\n1671 |             The passed name should substitute for the index name (if it has\n1672 |             one).\n1673 | \n1674 |         Returns\n1675 |         -------\n1676 |         DataFrame\n1677 |             DataFrame containing the original Index data.\n1678 | \n1679 |         See Also\n1680 |         --------\n1681 |         Index.to_series : Convert an Index to a Series.\n1682 |         Series.to_frame : Convert Series to DataFrame.\n1683 | \n1684 |         Examples\n1685 |         --------\n1686 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"Ant\", \"Bear\", \"Cow\"], name=\"animal\")\n1687 |         >>> idx.to_frame()\n1688 |                animal\n1689 |         animal\n1690 |         Ant       Ant\n1691 |         Bear     Bear\n1692 |         Cow       Cow\n1693 | \n1694 |         By default, the original Index is reused. To enforce a new Index:\n1695 | \n1696 |         >>> idx.to_frame(index=False)\n1697 |             animal\n1698 |         0   Ant\n1699 |         1  Bear\n1700 |         2   Cow\n1701 | \n1702 |         To override the name of the resulting column, specify `name`:\n1703 | \n1704 |         >>> idx.to_frame(index=False, name=\"zoo\")\n1705 |             zoo\n1706 |         0   Ant\n1707 |         1  Bear\n1708 |         2   Cow\n1709 |         \"\"\"\n1710 |         from pandas import DataFrame\n1711 | \n1712 |         if name is lib.no_default:\n1713 |             result_name = self._get_level_names()\n1714 |         else:\n1715 |             result_name = Index([name])  # type: ignore[assignment]\n1716 |         result = DataFrame(self, copy=False)\n1717 |         result.columns = result_name\n1718 | \n1719 |         if index:\n1720 |             result.index = self\n1721 |         return result\n1722 | \n1723 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n1724 |     # Name-Centric Methods\n1725 | \n1726 |     @property\n1727 |     def name(self) -> Hashable:\n1728 |         \"\"\"\n1729 |         Return Index or MultiIndex name.\n1730 | \n1731 |         See Also\n1732 |         --------\n1733 |         Index.set_names: Able to set new names partially and by level.\n1734 |         Index.rename: Able to set new names partially and by level.\n1735 | \n1736 |         Examples\n1737 |         --------\n1738 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3], name=\"x\")\n1739 |         >>> idx\n1740 |         Index([1, 2, 3], dtype='int64',  name='x')\n1741 |         >>> idx.name\n1742 |         'x'\n1743 |         \"\"\"\n1744 |         return self._name\n1745 | \n1746 |     @name.setter\n1747 |     def name(self, value: Hashable) -> None:\n1748 |         if self._no_setting_name:\n1749 |             # Used in MultiIndex.levels to avoid silently ignoring name updates.\n1750 |             raise RuntimeError(\n1751 |                 \"Cannot set name on a level of a MultiIndex. Use \"\n1752 |                 \"'MultiIndex.set_names' instead.\"\n1753 |             )\n1754 |         maybe_extract_name(value, None, type(self))\n1755 |         self._name = value\n1756 | \n1757 |     @final\n1758 |     def _validate_names(\n1759 |         self, name=None, names=None, deep: bool = False\n1760 |     ) -> list[Hashable]:\n1761 |         \"\"\"\n1762 |         Handles the quirks of having a singular 'name' parameter for general\n1763 |         Index and plural 'names' parameter for MultiIndex.\n1764 |         \"\"\"\n1765 |         from copy import deepcopy\n1766 | \n1767 |         if names is not None and name is not None:\n1768 |             raise TypeError(\"Can only provide one of `names` and `name`\")\n1769 |         if names is None and name is None:\n1770 |             new_names = deepcopy(self.names) if deep else self.names\n1771 |         elif names is not None:\n1772 |             if not is_list_like(names):\n1773 |                 raise TypeError(\"Must pass list-like as `names`.\")\n1774 |             new_names = names\n1775 |         elif not is_list_like(name):\n1776 |             new_names = [name]\n1777 |         else:\n1778 |             new_names = name\n1779 | \n1780 |         if len(new_names) != len(self.names):\n1781 |             raise ValueError(\n1782 |                 f\"Length of new names must be {len(self.names)}, got {len(new_names)}\"\n1783 |             )\n1784 | \n1785 |         # All items in 'new_names' need to be hashable\n1786 |         validate_all_hashable(*new_names, error_name=f\"{type(self).__name__}.name\")\n1787 | \n1788 |         return new_names\n1789 | \n1790 |     def _get_default_index_names(\n1791 |         self, names: Hashable | Sequence[Hashable] | None = None, default=None\n1792 |     ) -> list[Hashable]:\n1793 |         \"\"\"\n1794 |         Get names of index.\n1795 | \n1796 |         Parameters\n1797 |         ----------\n1798 |         names : int, str or 1-dimensional list, default None\n1799 |             Index names to set.\n1800 |         default : str\n1801 |             Default name of index.\n1802 | \n1803 |         Raises\n1804 |         ------\n1805 |         TypeError\n1806 |             if names not str or list-like\n1807 |         \"\"\"\n1808 |         from pandas.core.indexes.multi import MultiIndex\n1809 | \n1810 |         if names is not None:\n1811 |             if isinstance(names, (int, str)):\n1812 |                 names = [names]\n1813 | \n1814 |         if not isinstance(names, list) and names is not None:\n1815 |             raise ValueError(\"Index names must be str or 1-dimensional list\")\n1816 | \n1817 |         if not names:\n1818 |             if isinstance(self, MultiIndex):\n1819 |                 names = com.fill_missing_names(self.names)\n1820 |             else:\n1821 |                 names = [default] if self.name is None else [self.name]\n1822 | \n1823 |         return names\n1824 | \n1825 |     def _get_names(self) -> FrozenList:\n1826 |         \"\"\"\n1827 |         Get names on index.\n1828 | \n1829 |         This method returns a FrozenList containing the names of the object.\n1830 |         It's primarily intended for internal use.\n1831 | \n1832 |         Returns\n1833 |         -------\n1834 |         FrozenList\n1835 |             A FrozenList containing the object's names, contains None if the object\n1836 |             does not have a name.\n1837 | \n1838 |         See Also\n1839 |         --------\n1840 |         Index.name : Index name as a string, or None for MultiIndex.\n1841 | \n1842 |         Examples\n1843 |         --------\n1844 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3], name=\"x\")\n1845 |         >>> idx.names\n1846 |         FrozenList(['x'])\n1847 | \n1848 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3], name=(\"x\", \"y\"))\n1849 |         >>> idx.names\n1850 |         FrozenList([('x', 'y')])\n1851 | \n1852 |         If the index does not have a name set:\n1853 | \n1854 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3])\n1855 |         >>> idx.names\n1856 |         FrozenList([None])\n1857 |         \"\"\"\n1858 |         return FrozenList((self.name,))\n1859 | \n1860 |     def _set_names(self, values, *, level=None) -> None:\n1861 |         \"\"\"\n1862 |         Set new names on index. Each name has to be a hashable type.\n1863 | \n1864 |         Parameters\n1865 |         ----------\n1866 |         values : str or sequence\n1867 |             name(s) to set\n1868 |         level : int, level name, or sequence of int/level names (default None)\n1869 |             If the index is a MultiIndex (hierarchical), level(s) to set (None\n1870 |             for all levels).  Otherwise level must be None\n1871 | \n1872 |         Raises\n1873 |         ------\n1874 |         TypeError if each name is not hashable.\n1875 |         \"\"\"\n1876 |         if not is_list_like(values):\n1877 |             raise ValueError(\"Names must be a list-like\")\n1878 |         if len(values) != 1:\n1879 |             raise ValueError(f\"Length of new names must be 1, got {len(values)}\")\n1880 | \n1881 |         # GH 20527\n1882 |         # All items in 'name' need to be hashable:\n1883 |         validate_all_hashable(*values, error_name=f\"{type(self).__name__}.name\")\n1884 | \n1885 |         self._name = values[0]\n1886 | \n1887 |     names = property(fset=_set_names, fget=_get_names)\n1888 | \n1889 |     @overload\n1890 |     def set_names(self, names, *, level=..., inplace: Literal[False] = ...) -> Self: ...\n1891 | \n1892 |     @overload\n1893 |     def set_names(self, names, *, level=..., inplace: Literal[True]) -> None: ...\n1894 | \n1895 |     @overload\n1896 |     def set_names(self, names, *, level=..., inplace: bool = ...) -> Self | None: ...\n1897 | \n1898 |     def set_names(self, names, *, level=None, inplace: bool = False) -> Self | None:\n1899 |         \"\"\"\n1900 |         Set Index or MultiIndex name.\n1901 | \n1902 |         Able to set new names partially and by level.\n1903 | \n1904 |         Parameters\n1905 |         ----------\n1906 | \n1907 |         names : label or list of label or dict-like for MultiIndex\n1908 |             Name(s) to set.\n1909 | \n1910 |             .. versionchanged:: 1.3.0\n1911 | \n1912 |         level : int, label or list of int or label, optional\n1913 |             If the index is a MultiIndex and names is not dict-like, level(s) to set\n1914 |             (None for all levels). Otherwise level must be None.\n1915 | \n1916 |             .. versionchanged:: 1.3.0\n1917 | \n1918 |         inplace : bool, default False\n1919 |             Modifies the object directly, instead of creating a new Index or\n1920 |             MultiIndex.\n1921 | \n1922 |         Returns\n1923 |         -------\n1924 |         Index or None\n1925 |             The same type as the caller or None if ``inplace=True``.\n1926 | \n1927 |         See Also\n1928 |         --------\n1929 |         Index.rename : Able to set new names without level.\n1930 | \n1931 |         Examples\n1932 |         --------\n1933 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3, 4])\n1934 |         >>> idx\n1935 |         Index([1, 2, 3, 4], dtype='int64')\n1936 |         >>> idx.set_names(\"quarter\")\n1937 |         Index([1, 2, 3, 4], dtype='int64', name='quarter')\n1938 | \n1939 |         >>> idx = pd.MultiIndex.from_product([[\"python\", \"cobra\"], [2018, 2019]])\n1940 |         >>> idx\n1941 |         MultiIndex([('python', 2018),\n1942 |                     ('python', 2019),\n1943 |                     ( 'cobra', 2018),\n1944 |                     ( 'cobra', 2019)],\n1945 |                    )\n1946 |         >>> idx = idx.set_names([\"kind\", \"year\"])\n1947 |         >>> idx.set_names(\"species\", level=0)\n1948 |         MultiIndex([('python', 2018),\n1949 |                     ('python', 2019),\n1950 |                     ( 'cobra', 2018),\n1951 |                     ( 'cobra', 2019)],\n1952 |                    names=['species', 'year'])\n1953 | \n1954 |         When renaming levels with a dict, levels can not be passed.\n1955 | \n1956 |         >>> idx.set_names({\"kind\": \"snake\"})\n1957 |         MultiIndex([('python', 2018),\n1958 |                     ('python', 2019),\n1959 |                     ( 'cobra', 2018),\n1960 |                     ( 'cobra', 2019)],\n1961 |                    names=['snake', 'year'])\n1962 |         \"\"\"\n1963 |         if level is not None and not isinstance(self, ABCMultiIndex):\n1964 |             raise ValueError(\"Level must be None for non-MultiIndex\")\n1965 | \n1966 |         if level is not None and not is_list_like(level) and is_list_like(names):\n1967 |             raise TypeError(\"Names must be a string when a single level is provided.\")\n1968 | \n1969 |         if not is_list_like(names) and level is None and self.nlevels > 1:\n1970 |             raise TypeError(\"Must pass list-like as `names`.\")\n1971 | \n1972 |         if is_dict_like(names) and not isinstance(self, ABCMultiIndex):\n1973 |             raise TypeError(\"Can only pass dict-like as `names` for MultiIndex.\")\n1974 | \n1975 |         if is_dict_like(names) and level is not None:\n1976 |             raise TypeError(\"Can not pass level for dictlike `names`.\")\n1977 | \n1978 |         if isinstance(self, ABCMultiIndex) and is_dict_like(names) and level is None:\n1979 |             # Transform dict to list of new names and corresponding levels\n1980 |             level, names_adjusted = [], []\n1981 |             for i, name in enumerate(self.names):\n1982 |                 if name in names.keys():\n1983 |                     level.append(i)\n1984 |                     names_adjusted.append(names[name])\n1985 |             names = names_adjusted\n1986 | \n1987 |         if not is_list_like(names):\n1988 |             names = [names]\n1989 |         if level is not None and not is_list_like(level):\n1990 |             level = [level]\n1991 | \n1992 |         if inplace:\n1993 |             idx = self\n1994 |         else:\n1995 |             idx = self._view()\n1996 | \n1997 |         idx._set_names(names, level=level)\n1998 |         if not inplace:\n1999 |             return idx\n2000 |         return None\n2001 | \n2002 |     @overload\n2003 |     def rename(self, name, *, inplace: Literal[False] = ...) -> Self: ...\n2004 | \n2005 |     @overload\n2006 |     def rename(self, name, *, inplace: Literal[True]) -> None: ...\n2007 | \n2008 |     def rename(self, name, *, inplace: bool = False) -> Self | None:\n2009 |         \"\"\"\n2010 |         Alter Index or MultiIndex name.\n2011 | \n2012 |         Able to set new names without level. Defaults to returning new index.\n2013 |         Length of names must match number of levels in MultiIndex.\n2014 | \n2015 |         Parameters\n2016 |         ----------\n2017 |         name : label or list of labels\n2018 |             Name(s) to set.\n2019 |         inplace : bool, default False\n2020 |             Modifies the object directly, instead of creating a new Index or\n2021 |             MultiIndex.\n2022 | \n2023 |         Returns\n2024 |         -------\n2025 |         Index or None\n2026 |             The same type as the caller or None if ``inplace=True``.\n2027 | \n2028 |         See Also\n2029 |         --------\n2030 |         Index.set_names : Able to set new names partially and by level.\n2031 | \n2032 |         Examples\n2033 |         --------\n2034 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"A\", \"C\", \"A\", \"B\"], name=\"score\")\n2035 |         >>> idx.rename(\"grade\")\n2036 |         Index(['A', 'C', 'A', 'B'], dtype='object', name='grade')\n2037 | \n2038 |         >>> idx = pd.MultiIndex.from_product(\n2039 |         ...     [[\"python\", \"cobra\"], [2018, 2019]], names=[\"kind\", \"year\"]\n2040 |         ... )\n2041 |         >>> idx\n2042 |         MultiIndex([('python', 2018),\n2043 |                     ('python', 2019),\n2044 |                     ( 'cobra', 2018),\n2045 |                     ( 'cobra', 2019)],\n2046 |                    names=['kind', 'year'])\n2047 |         >>> idx.rename([\"species\", \"year\"])\n2048 |         MultiIndex([('python', 2018),\n2049 |                     ('python', 2019),\n2050 |                     ( 'cobra', 2018),\n2051 |                     ( 'cobra', 2019)],\n2052 |                    names=['species', 'year'])\n2053 |         >>> idx.rename(\"species\")\n2054 |         Traceback (most recent call last):\n2055 |         TypeError: Must pass list-like as `names`.\n2056 |         \"\"\"\n2057 |         return self.set_names([name], inplace=inplace)\n2058 | \n2059 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n2060 |     # Level-Centric Methods\n2061 | \n2062 |     @property\n2063 |     def nlevels(self) -> int:\n2064 |         \"\"\"\n2065 |         Number of levels.\n2066 |         \"\"\"\n2067 |         return 1\n2068 | \n2069 |     def _sort_levels_monotonic(self) -> Self:\n2070 |         \"\"\"\n2071 |         Compat with MultiIndex.\n2072 |         \"\"\"\n2073 |         return self\n2074 | \n2075 |     @final\n2076 |     def _validate_index_level(self, level) -> None:\n2077 |         \"\"\"\n2078 |         Validate index level.\n2079 | \n2080 |         For single-level Index getting level number is a no-op, but some\n2081 |         verification must be done like in MultiIndex.\n2082 | \n2083 |         \"\"\"\n2084 |         if isinstance(level, int):\n2085 |             if level < 0 and level != -1:\n2086 |                 raise IndexError(\n2087 |                     \"Too many levels: Index has only 1 level, \"\n2088 |                     f\"{level} is not a valid level number\"\n2089 |                 )\n2090 |             if level > 0:\n2091 |                 raise IndexError(\n2092 |                     f\"Too many levels: Index has only 1 level, not {level + 1}\"\n2093 |                 )\n2094 |         elif level != self.name:\n2095 |             raise KeyError(\n2096 |                 f\"Requested level ({level}) does not match index name ({self.name})\"\n2097 |             )\n2098 | \n2099 |     def _get_level_number(self, level) -> int:\n2100 |         self._validate_index_level(level)\n2101 |         return 0\n2102 | \n2103 |     def sortlevel(\n2104 |         self,\n2105 |         level=None,\n2106 |         ascending: bool | list[bool] = True,\n2107 |         sort_remaining=None,\n2108 |         na_position: NaPosition = \"first\",\n2109 |     ) -> tuple[Self, np.ndarray]:\n2110 |         \"\"\"\n2111 |         For internal compatibility with the Index API.\n2112 | \n2113 |         Sort the Index. This is for compat with MultiIndex\n2114 | \n2115 |         Parameters\n2116 |         ----------\n2117 |         ascending : bool, default True\n2118 |             False to sort in descending order\n2119 |         na_position : {'first' or 'last'}, default 'first'\n2120 |             Argument 'first' puts NaNs at the beginning, 'last' puts NaNs at\n2121 |             the end.\n2122 | \n2123 |             .. versionadded:: 2.1.0\n2124 | \n2125 |         level, sort_remaining are compat parameters\n2126 | \n2127 |         Returns\n2128 |         -------\n2129 |         Index\n2130 |         \"\"\"\n2131 |         if not isinstance(ascending, (list, bool)):\n2132 |             raise TypeError(\n2133 |                 \"ascending must be a single bool value or\"\n2134 |                 \"a list of bool values of length 1\"\n2135 |             )\n2136 | \n2137 |         if isinstance(ascending, list):\n2138 |             if len(ascending) != 1:\n2139 |                 raise TypeError(\"ascending must be a list of bool values of length 1\")\n2140 |             ascending = ascending[0]\n2141 | \n2142 |         if not isinstance(ascending, bool):\n2143 |             raise TypeError(\"ascending must be a bool value\")\n2144 | \n2145 |         return self.sort_values(\n2146 |             return_indexer=True, ascending=ascending, na_position=na_position\n2147 |         )\n2148 | \n2149 |     def _get_level_values(self, level) -> Index:\n2150 |         \"\"\"\n2151 |         Return an Index of values for requested level.\n2152 | \n2153 |         This is primarily useful to get an individual level of values from a\n2154 |         MultiIndex, but is provided on Index as well for compatibility.\n2155 | \n2156 |         Parameters\n2157 |         ----------\n2158 |         level : int or str\n2159 |             It is either the integer position or the name of the level.\n2160 | \n2161 |         Returns\n2162 |         -------\n2163 |         Index\n2164 |             Calling object, as there is only one level in the Index.\n2165 | \n2166 |         See Also\n2167 |         --------\n2168 |         MultiIndex.get_level_values : Get values for a level of a MultiIndex.\n2169 | \n2170 |         Notes\n2171 |         -----\n2172 |         For Index, level should be 0, since there are no multiple levels.\n2173 | \n2174 |         Examples\n2175 |         --------\n2176 |         >>> idx = pd.Index(list(\"abc\"))\n2177 |         >>> idx\n2178 |         Index(['a', 'b', 'c'], dtype='object')\n2179 | \n2180 |         Get level values by supplying `level` as integer:\n2181 | \n2182 |         >>> idx.get_level_values(0)\n2183 |         Index(['a', 'b', 'c'], dtype='object')\n2184 |         \"\"\"\n2185 |         self._validate_index_level(level)\n2186 |         return self\n2187 | \n2188 |     get_level_values = _get_level_values\n2189 | \n2190 |     @final\n2191 |     def droplevel(self, level: IndexLabel = 0):\n2192 |         \"\"\"\n2193 |         Return index with requested level(s) removed.\n2194 | \n2195 |         If resulting index has only 1 level left, the result will be\n2196 |         of Index type, not MultiIndex. The original index is not modified inplace.\n2197 | \n2198 |         Parameters\n2199 |         ----------\n2200 |         level : int, str, or list-like, default 0\n2201 |             If a string is given, must be the name of a level\n2202 |             If list-like, elements must be names or indexes of levels.\n2203 | \n2204 |         Returns\n2205 |         -------\n2206 |         Index or MultiIndex\n2207 |             Returns an Index or MultiIndex object, depending on the resulting index\n2208 |             after removing the requested level(s).\n2209 | \n2210 |         See Also\n2211 |         --------\n2212 |         Index.dropna : Return Index without NA/NaN values.\n2213 | \n2214 |         Examples\n2215 |         --------\n2216 |         >>> mi = pd.MultiIndex.from_arrays(\n2217 |         ...     [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]], names=[\"x\", \"y\", \"z\"]\n2218 |         ... )\n2219 |         >>> mi\n2220 |         MultiIndex([(1, 3, 5),\n2221 |                     (2, 4, 6)],\n2222 |                    names=['x', 'y', 'z'])\n2223 | \n2224 |         >>> mi.droplevel()\n2225 |         MultiIndex([(3, 5),\n2226 |                     (4, 6)],\n2227 |                    names=['y', 'z'])\n2228 | \n2229 |         >>> mi.droplevel(2)\n2230 |         MultiIndex([(1, 3),\n2231 |                     (2, 4)],\n2232 |                    names=['x', 'y'])\n2233 | \n2234 |         >>> mi.droplevel(\"z\")\n2235 |         MultiIndex([(1, 3),\n2236 |                     (2, 4)],\n2237 |                    names=['x', 'y'])\n2238 | \n2239 |         >>> mi.droplevel([\"x\", \"y\"])\n2240 |         Index([5, 6], dtype='int64', name='z')\n2241 |         \"\"\"\n2242 |         if not isinstance(level, (tuple, list)):\n2243 |             level = [level]\n2244 | \n2245 |         levnums = sorted((self._get_level_number(lev) for lev in level), reverse=True)\n2246 | \n2247 |         return self._drop_level_numbers(levnums)\n2248 | \n2249 |     @final\n2250 |     def _drop_level_numbers(self, levnums: list[int]):\n2251 |         \"\"\"\n2252 |         Drop MultiIndex levels by level _number_, not name.\n2253 |         \"\"\"\n2254 | \n2255 |         if not levnums and not isinstance(self, ABCMultiIndex):\n2256 |             return self\n2257 |         if len(levnums) >= self.nlevels:\n2258 |             raise ValueError(\n2259 |                 f\"Cannot remove {len(levnums)} levels from an index with \"\n2260 |                 f\"{self.nlevels} levels: at least one level must be left.\"\n2261 |             )\n2262 |         # The two checks above guarantee that here self is a MultiIndex\n2263 |         self = cast(\"MultiIndex\", self)\n2264 | \n2265 |         new_levels = list(self.levels)\n2266 |         new_codes = list(self.codes)\n2267 |         new_names = list(self.names)\n2268 | \n2269 |         for i in levnums:\n2270 |             new_levels.pop(i)\n2271 |             new_codes.pop(i)\n2272 |             new_names.pop(i)\n2273 | \n2274 |         if len(new_levels) == 1:\n2275 |             lev = new_levels[0]\n2276 | \n2277 |             if len(lev) == 0:\n2278 |                 # If lev is empty, lev.take will fail GH#42055\n2279 |                 if len(new_codes[0]) == 0:\n2280 |                     # GH#45230 preserve RangeIndex here\n2281 |                     #  see test_reset_index_empty_rangeindex\n2282 |                     result = lev[:0]\n2283 |                 else:\n2284 |                     res_values = algos.take(lev._values, new_codes[0], allow_fill=True)\n2285 |                     # _constructor instead of type(lev) for RangeIndex compat GH#35230\n2286 |                     result = lev._constructor._simple_new(res_values, name=new_names[0])\n2287 |             else:\n2288 |                 # set nan if needed\n2289 |                 mask = new_codes[0] == -1\n2290 |                 result = new_levels[0].take(new_codes[0])\n2291 |                 if mask.any():\n2292 |                     result = result.putmask(mask, np.nan)\n2293 | \n2294 |                 result._name = new_names[0]\n2295 | \n2296 |             return result\n2297 |         else:\n2298 |             from pandas.core.indexes.multi import MultiIndex\n2299 | \n2300 |             return MultiIndex(\n2301 |                 levels=new_levels,\n2302 |                 codes=new_codes,\n2303 |                 names=new_names,\n2304 |                 verify_integrity=False,\n2305 |             )\n2306 | \n2307 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n2308 |     # Introspection Methods\n2309 | \n2310 |     @cache_readonly\n2311 |     @final\n2312 |     def _can_hold_na(self) -> bool:\n2313 |         if isinstance(self.dtype, ExtensionDtype):\n2314 |             return self.dtype._can_hold_na\n2315 |         if self.dtype.kind in \"iub\":\n2316 |             return False\n2317 |         return True\n2318 | \n2319 |     @property\n2320 |     def is_monotonic_increasing(self) -> bool:\n2321 |         \"\"\"\n2322 |         Return a boolean if the values are equal or increasing.\n2323 | \n2324 |         Returns\n2325 |         -------\n2326 |         bool\n2327 | \n2328 |         See Also\n2329 |         --------\n2330 |         Index.is_monotonic_decreasing : Check if the values are equal or decreasing.\n2331 | \n2332 |         Examples\n2333 |         --------\n2334 |         >>> pd.Index([1, 2, 3]).is_monotonic_increasing\n2335 |         True\n2336 |         >>> pd.Index([1, 2, 2]).is_monotonic_increasing\n2337 |         True\n2338 |         >>> pd.Index([1, 3, 2]).is_monotonic_increasing\n2339 |         False\n2340 |         \"\"\"\n2341 |         return self._engine.is_monotonic_increasing\n2342 | \n2343 |     @property\n2344 |     def is_monotonic_decreasing(self) -> bool:\n2345 |         \"\"\"\n2346 |         Return a boolean if the values are equal or decreasing.\n2347 | \n2348 |         Returns\n2349 |         -------\n2350 |         bool\n2351 | \n2352 |         See Also\n2353 |         --------\n2354 |         Index.is_monotonic_increasing : Check if the values are equal or increasing.\n2355 | \n2356 |         Examples\n2357 |         --------\n2358 |         >>> pd.Index([3, 2, 1]).is_monotonic_decreasing\n2359 |         True\n2360 |         >>> pd.Index([3, 2, 2]).is_monotonic_decreasing\n2361 |         True\n2362 |         >>> pd.Index([3, 1, 2]).is_monotonic_decreasing\n2363 |         False\n2364 |         \"\"\"\n2365 |         return self._engine.is_monotonic_decreasing\n2366 | \n2367 |     @final\n2368 |     @property\n2369 |     def _is_strictly_monotonic_increasing(self) -> bool:\n2370 |         \"\"\"\n2371 |         Return if the index is strictly monotonic increasing\n2372 |         (only increasing) values.\n2373 | \n2374 |         Examples\n2375 |         --------\n2376 |         >>> Index([1, 2, 3])._is_strictly_monotonic_increasing\n2377 |         True\n2378 |         >>> Index([1, 2, 2])._is_strictly_monotonic_increasing\n2379 |         False\n2380 |         >>> Index([1, 3, 2])._is_strictly_monotonic_increasing\n2381 |         False\n2382 |         \"\"\"\n2383 |         return self.is_unique and self.is_monotonic_increasing\n2384 | \n2385 |     @final\n2386 |     @property\n2387 |     def _is_strictly_monotonic_decreasing(self) -> bool:\n2388 |         \"\"\"\n2389 |         Return if the index is strictly monotonic decreasing\n2390 |         (only decreasing) values.\n2391 | \n2392 |         Examples\n2393 |         --------\n2394 |         >>> Index([3, 2, 1])._is_strictly_monotonic_decreasing\n2395 |         True\n2396 |         >>> Index([3, 2, 2])._is_strictly_monotonic_decreasing\n2397 |         False\n2398 |         >>> Index([3, 1, 2])._is_strictly_monotonic_decreasing\n2399 |         False\n2400 |         \"\"\"\n2401 |         return self.is_unique and self.is_monotonic_decreasing\n2402 | \n2403 |     @cache_readonly\n2404 |     def is_unique(self) -> bool:\n2405 |         \"\"\"\n2406 |         Return if the index has unique values.\n2407 | \n2408 |         Returns\n2409 |         -------\n2410 |         bool\n2411 | \n2412 |         See Also\n2413 |         --------\n2414 |         Index.has_duplicates : Inverse method that checks if it has duplicate values.\n2415 | \n2416 |         Examples\n2417 |         --------\n2418 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 5, 7, 7])\n2419 |         >>> idx.is_unique\n2420 |         False\n2421 | \n2422 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 5, 7])\n2423 |         >>> idx.is_unique\n2424 |         True\n2425 | \n2426 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"Watermelon\", \"Orange\", \"Apple\", \"Watermelon\"]).astype(\n2427 |         ...     \"category\"\n2428 |         ... )\n2429 |         >>> idx.is_unique\n2430 |         False\n2431 | \n2432 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"Orange\", \"Apple\", \"Watermelon\"]).astype(\"category\")\n2433 |         >>> idx.is_unique\n2434 |         True\n2435 |         \"\"\"\n2436 |         return self._engine.is_unique\n2437 | \n2438 |     @final\n2439 |     @property\n2440 |     def has_duplicates(self) -> bool:\n2441 |         \"\"\"\n2442 |         Check if the Index has duplicate values.\n2443 | \n2444 |         Returns\n2445 |         -------\n2446 |         bool\n2447 |             Whether or not the Index has duplicate values.\n2448 | \n2449 |         See Also\n2450 |         --------\n2451 |         Index.is_unique : Inverse method that checks if it has unique values.\n2452 | \n2453 |         Examples\n2454 |         --------\n2455 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 5, 7, 7])\n2456 |         >>> idx.has_duplicates\n2457 |         True\n2458 | \n2459 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 5, 7])\n2460 |         >>> idx.has_duplicates\n2461 |         False\n2462 | \n2463 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"Watermelon\", \"Orange\", \"Apple\", \"Watermelon\"]).astype(\n2464 |         ...     \"category\"\n2465 |         ... )\n2466 |         >>> idx.has_duplicates\n2467 |         True\n2468 | \n2469 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"Orange\", \"Apple\", \"Watermelon\"]).astype(\"category\")\n2470 |         >>> idx.has_duplicates\n2471 |         False\n2472 |         \"\"\"\n2473 |         return not self.is_unique\n2474 | \n2475 |     @cache_readonly\n2476 |     def inferred_type(self) -> str_t:\n2477 |         \"\"\"\n2478 |         Return a string of the type inferred from the values.\n2479 | \n2480 |         See Also\n2481 |         --------\n2482 |         Index.dtype : Return the dtype object of the underlying data.\n2483 | \n2484 |         Examples\n2485 |         --------\n2486 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3])\n2487 |         >>> idx\n2488 |         Index([1, 2, 3], dtype='int64')\n2489 |         >>> idx.inferred_type\n2490 |         'integer'\n2491 |         \"\"\"\n2492 |         return lib.infer_dtype(self._values, skipna=False)\n2493 | \n2494 |     @cache_readonly\n2495 |     @final\n2496 |     def _is_all_dates(self) -> bool:\n2497 |         \"\"\"\n2498 |         Whether or not the index values only consist of dates.\n2499 |         \"\"\"\n2500 |         if needs_i8_conversion(self.dtype):\n2501 |             return True\n2502 |         elif self.dtype != _dtype_obj:\n2503 |             # TODO(ExtensionIndex): 3rd party EA might override?\n2504 |             # Note: this includes IntervalIndex, even when the left/right\n2505 |             #  contain datetime-like objects.\n2506 |             return False\n2507 |         elif self._is_multi:\n2508 |             return False\n2509 |         return is_datetime_array(ensure_object(self._values))\n2510 | \n2511 |     @final\n2512 |     @cache_readonly\n2513 |     def _is_multi(self) -> bool:\n2514 |         \"\"\"\n2515 |         Cached check equivalent to isinstance(self, MultiIndex)\n2516 |         \"\"\"\n2517 |         return isinstance(self, ABCMultiIndex)\n2518 | \n2519 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n2520 |     # Pickle Methods\n2521 | \n2522 |     def __reduce__(self):\n2523 |         d = {\"data\": self._data, \"name\": self.name}\n2524 |         return _new_Index, (type(self), d), None\n2525 | \n2526 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n2527 |     # Null Handling Methods\n2528 | \n2529 |     @cache_readonly\n2530 |     def _na_value(self):\n2531 |         \"\"\"The expected NA value to use with this index.\"\"\"\n2532 |         dtype = self.dtype\n2533 |         if isinstance(dtype, np.dtype):\n2534 |             if dtype.kind in \"mM\":\n2535 |                 return NaT\n2536 |             return np.nan\n2537 |         return dtype.na_value\n2538 | \n2539 |     @cache_readonly\n2540 |     def _isnan(self) -> npt.NDArray[np.bool_]:\n2541 |         \"\"\"\n2542 |         Return if each value is NaN.\n2543 |         \"\"\"\n2544 |         if self._can_hold_na:\n2545 |             return isna(self)\n2546 |         else:\n2547 |             # shouldn't reach to this condition by checking hasnans beforehand\n2548 |             values = np.empty(len(self), dtype=np.bool_)\n2549 |             values.fill(False)\n2550 |             return values\n2551 | \n2552 |     @cache_readonly\n2553 |     def hasnans(self) -> bool:\n2554 |         \"\"\"\n2555 |         Return True if there are any NaNs.\n2556 | \n2557 |         Enables various performance speedups.\n2558 | \n2559 |         Returns\n2560 |         -------\n2561 |         bool\n2562 | \n2563 |         See Also\n2564 |         --------\n2565 |         Index.isna : Detect missing values.\n2566 |         Index.dropna : Return Index without NA/NaN values.\n2567 |         Index.fillna : Fill NA/NaN values with the specified value.\n2568 | \n2569 |         Examples\n2570 |         --------\n2571 |         >>> s = pd.Series([1, 2, 3], index=[\"a\", \"b\", None])\n2572 |         >>> s\n2573 |         a    1\n2574 |         b    2\n2575 |         None 3\n2576 |         dtype: int64\n2577 |         >>> s.index.hasnans\n2578 |         True\n2579 |         \"\"\"\n2580 |         if self._can_hold_na:\n2581 |             return bool(self._isnan.any())\n2582 |         else:\n2583 |             return False\n2584 | \n2585 |     @final\n2586 |     def isna(self) -> npt.NDArray[np.bool_]:\n2587 |         \"\"\"\n2588 |         Detect missing values.\n2589 | \n2590 |         Return a boolean same-sized object indicating if the values are NA.\n2591 |         NA values, such as ``None``, :attr:`numpy.NaN` or :attr:`pd.NaT`, get\n2592 |         mapped to ``True`` values.\n2593 |         Everything else get mapped to ``False`` values. Characters such as\n2594 |         empty strings `''` or :attr:`numpy.inf` are not considered NA values.\n2595 | \n2596 |         Returns\n2597 |         -------\n2598 |         numpy.ndarray[bool]\n2599 |             A boolean array of whether my values are NA.\n2600 | \n2601 |         See Also\n2602 |         --------\n2603 |         Index.notna : Boolean inverse of isna.\n2604 |         Index.dropna : Omit entries with missing values.\n2605 |         isna : Top-level isna.\n2606 |         Series.isna : Detect missing values in Series object.\n2607 | \n2608 |         Examples\n2609 |         --------\n2610 |         Show which entries in a pandas.Index are NA. The result is an\n2611 |         array.\n2612 | \n2613 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([5.2, 6.0, np.nan])\n2614 |         >>> idx\n2615 |         Index([5.2, 6.0, nan], dtype='float64')\n2616 |         >>> idx.isna()\n2617 |         array([False, False,  True])\n2618 | \n2619 |         Empty strings are not considered NA values. None is considered an NA\n2620 |         value.\n2621 | \n2622 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"black\", \"\", \"red\", None])\n2623 |         >>> idx\n2624 |         Index(['black', '', 'red', None], dtype='object')\n2625 |         >>> idx.isna()\n2626 |         array([False, False, False,  True])\n2627 | \n2628 |         For datetimes, `NaT` (Not a Time) is considered as an NA value.\n2629 | \n2630 |         >>> idx = pd.DatetimeIndex(\n2631 |         ...     [pd.Timestamp(\"1940-04-25\"), pd.Timestamp(\"\"), None, pd.NaT]\n2632 |         ... )\n2633 |         >>> idx\n2634 |         DatetimeIndex(['1940-04-25', 'NaT', 'NaT', 'NaT'],\n2635 |                       dtype='datetime64[s]', freq=None)\n2636 |         >>> idx.isna()\n2637 |         array([False,  True,  True,  True])\n2638 |         \"\"\"\n2639 |         return self._isnan\n2640 | \n2641 |     isnull = isna\n2642 | \n2643 |     @final\n2644 |     def notna(self) -> npt.NDArray[np.bool_]:\n2645 |         \"\"\"\n2646 |         Detect existing (non-missing) values.\n2647 | \n2648 |         Return a boolean same-sized object indicating if the values are not NA.\n2649 |         Non-missing values get mapped to ``True``. Characters such as empty\n2650 |         strings ``''`` or :attr:`numpy.inf` are not considered NA values.\n2651 |         NA values, such as None or :attr:`numpy.NaN`, get mapped to ``False``\n2652 |         values.\n2653 | \n2654 |         Returns\n2655 |         -------\n2656 |         numpy.ndarray[bool]\n2657 |             Boolean array to indicate which entries are not NA.\n2658 | \n2659 |         See Also\n2660 |         --------\n2661 |         Index.notnull : Alias of notna.\n2662 |         Index.isna: Inverse of notna.\n2663 |         notna : Top-level notna.\n2664 | \n2665 |         Examples\n2666 |         --------\n2667 |         Show which entries in an Index are not NA. The result is an\n2668 |         array.\n2669 | \n2670 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([5.2, 6.0, np.nan])\n2671 |         >>> idx\n2672 |         Index([5.2, 6.0, nan], dtype='float64')\n2673 |         >>> idx.notna()\n2674 |         array([ True,  True, False])\n2675 | \n2676 |         Empty strings are not considered NA values. None is considered a NA\n2677 |         value.\n2678 | \n2679 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"black\", \"\", \"red\", None])\n2680 |         >>> idx\n2681 |         Index(['black', '', 'red', None], dtype='object')\n2682 |         >>> idx.notna()\n2683 |         array([ True,  True,  True, False])\n2684 |         \"\"\"\n2685 |         return ~self.isna()\n2686 | \n2687 |     notnull = notna\n2688 | \n2689 |     def fillna(self, value):\n2690 |         \"\"\"\n2691 |         Fill NA/NaN values with the specified value.\n2692 | \n2693 |         Parameters\n2694 |         ----------\n2695 |         value : scalar\n2696 |             Scalar value to use to fill holes (e.g. 0).\n2697 |             This value cannot be a list-likes.\n2698 | \n2699 |         Returns\n2700 |         -------\n2701 |         Index\n2702 |            NA/NaN values replaced with `value`.\n2703 | \n2704 |         See Also\n2705 |         --------\n2706 |         DataFrame.fillna : Fill NaN values of a DataFrame.\n2707 |         Series.fillna : Fill NaN Values of a Series.\n2708 | \n2709 |         Examples\n2710 |         --------\n2711 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([np.nan, np.nan, 3])\n2712 |         >>> idx.fillna(0)\n2713 |         Index([0.0, 0.0, 3.0], dtype='float64')\n2714 |         \"\"\"\n2715 |         if not is_scalar(value):\n2716 |             raise TypeError(f\"'value' must be a scalar, passed: {type(value).__name__}\")\n2717 | \n2718 |         if self.hasnans:\n2719 |             result = self.putmask(self._isnan, value)\n2720 |             # no need to care metadata other than name\n2721 |             # because it can't have freq if it has NaTs\n2722 |             # _with_infer needed for test_fillna_categorical\n2723 |             return Index._with_infer(result, name=self.name)\n2724 |         return self._view()\n2725 | \n2726 |     def dropna(self, how: AnyAll = \"any\") -> Self:\n2727 |         \"\"\"\n2728 |         Return Index without NA/NaN values.\n2729 | \n2730 |         Parameters\n2731 |         ----------\n2732 |         how : {'any', 'all'}, default 'any'\n2733 |             If the Index is a MultiIndex, drop the value when any or all levels\n2734 |             are NaN.\n2735 | \n2736 |         Returns\n2737 |         -------\n2738 |         Index\n2739 |             Returns an Index object after removing NA/NaN values.\n2740 | \n2741 |         See Also\n2742 |         --------\n2743 |         Index.fillna : Fill NA/NaN values with the specified value.\n2744 |         Index.isna : Detect missing values.\n2745 | \n2746 |         Examples\n2747 |         --------\n2748 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, np.nan, 3])\n2749 |         >>> idx.dropna()\n2750 |         Index([1.0, 3.0], dtype='float64')\n2751 |         \"\"\"\n2752 |         if how not in (\"any\", \"all\"):\n2753 |             raise ValueError(f\"invalid how option: {how}\")\n2754 | \n2755 |         if self.hasnans:\n2756 |             res_values = self._values[~self._isnan]\n2757 |             return type(self)._simple_new(res_values, name=self.name)\n2758 |         return self._view()\n2759 | \n2760 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n2761 |     # Uniqueness Methods\n2762 | \n2763 |     def unique(self, level: Hashable | None = None) -> Self:\n2764 |         \"\"\"\n2765 |         Return unique values in the index.\n2766 | \n2767 |         Unique values are returned in order of appearance, this does NOT sort.\n2768 | \n2769 |         Parameters\n2770 |         ----------\n2771 |         level : int or hashable, optional\n2772 |             Only return values from specified level (for MultiIndex).\n2773 |             If int, gets the level by integer position, else by level name.\n2774 | \n2775 |         Returns\n2776 |         -------\n2777 |         Index\n2778 |             Unique values in the index.\n2779 | \n2780 |         See Also\n2781 |         --------\n2782 |         unique : Numpy array of unique values in that column.\n2783 |         Series.unique : Return unique values of Series object.\n2784 | \n2785 |         Examples\n2786 |         --------\n2787 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 1, 2, 3, 3])\n2788 |         >>> idx.unique()\n2789 |         Index([1, 2, 3], dtype='int64')\n2790 |         \"\"\"\n2791 |         if level is not None:\n2792 |             self._validate_index_level(level)\n2793 | \n2794 |         if self.is_unique:\n2795 |             return self._view()\n2796 | \n2797 |         result = super().unique()\n2798 |         return self._shallow_copy(result)\n2799 | \n2800 |     def drop_duplicates(self, *, keep: DropKeep = \"first\") -> Self:\n2801 |         \"\"\"\n2802 |         Return Index with duplicate values removed.\n2803 | \n2804 |         Parameters\n2805 |         ----------\n2806 |         keep : {'first', 'last', ``False``}, default 'first'\n2807 |             - 'first' : Drop duplicates except for the first occurrence.\n2808 |             - 'last' : Drop duplicates except for the last occurrence.\n2809 |             - ``False`` : Drop all duplicates.\n2810 | \n2811 |         Returns\n2812 |         -------\n2813 |         Index\n2814 |             A new Index object with the duplicate values removed.\n2815 | \n2816 |         See Also\n2817 |         --------\n2818 |         Series.drop_duplicates : Equivalent method on Series.\n2819 |         DataFrame.drop_duplicates : Equivalent method on DataFrame.\n2820 |         Index.duplicated : Related method on Index, indicating duplicate\n2821 |             Index values.\n2822 | \n2823 |         Examples\n2824 |         --------\n2825 |         Generate an pandas.Index with duplicate values.\n2826 | \n2827 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"llama\", \"cow\", \"llama\", \"beetle\", \"llama\", \"hippo\"])\n2828 | \n2829 |         The `keep` parameter controls  which duplicate values are removed.\n2830 |         The value 'first' keeps the first occurrence for each\n2831 |         set of duplicated entries. The default value of keep is 'first'.\n2832 | \n2833 |         >>> idx.drop_duplicates(keep=\"first\")\n2834 |         Index(['llama', 'cow', 'beetle', 'hippo'], dtype='object')\n2835 | \n2836 |         The value 'last' keeps the last occurrence for each set of duplicated\n2837 |         entries.\n2838 | \n2839 |         >>> idx.drop_duplicates(keep=\"last\")\n2840 |         Index(['cow', 'beetle', 'llama', 'hippo'], dtype='object')\n2841 | \n2842 |         The value ``False`` discards all sets of duplicated entries.\n2843 | \n2844 |         >>> idx.drop_duplicates(keep=False)\n2845 |         Index(['cow', 'beetle', 'hippo'], dtype='object')\n2846 |         \"\"\"\n2847 |         if self.is_unique:\n2848 |             return self._view()\n2849 | \n2850 |         return super().drop_duplicates(keep=keep)\n2851 | \n2852 |     def duplicated(self, keep: DropKeep = \"first\") -> npt.NDArray[np.bool_]:\n2853 |         \"\"\"\n2854 |         Indicate duplicate index values.\n2855 | \n2856 |         Duplicated values are indicated as ``True`` values in the resulting\n2857 |         array. Either all duplicates, all except the first, or all except the\n2858 |         last occurrence of duplicates can be indicated.\n2859 | \n2860 |         Parameters\n2861 |         ----------\n2862 |         keep : {'first', 'last', False}, default 'first'\n2863 |             The value or values in a set of duplicates to mark as missing.\n2864 | \n2865 |             - 'first' : Mark duplicates as ``True`` except for the first\n2866 |               occurrence.\n2867 |             - 'last' : Mark duplicates as ``True`` except for the last\n2868 |               occurrence.\n2869 |             - ``False`` : Mark all duplicates as ``True``.\n2870 | \n2871 |         Returns\n2872 |         -------\n2873 |         np.ndarray[bool]\n2874 |             A numpy array of boolean values indicating duplicate index values.\n2875 | \n2876 |         See Also\n2877 |         --------\n2878 |         Series.duplicated : Equivalent method on pandas.Series.\n2879 |         DataFrame.duplicated : Equivalent method on pandas.DataFrame.\n2880 |         Index.drop_duplicates : Remove duplicate values from Index.\n2881 | \n2882 |         Examples\n2883 |         --------\n2884 |         By default, for each set of duplicated values, the first occurrence is\n2885 |         set to False and all others to True:\n2886 | \n2887 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"llama\", \"cow\", \"llama\", \"beetle\", \"llama\"])\n2888 |         >>> idx.duplicated()\n2889 |         array([False, False,  True, False,  True])\n2890 | \n2891 |         which is equivalent to\n2892 | \n2893 |         >>> idx.duplicated(keep=\"first\")\n2894 |         array([False, False,  True, False,  True])\n2895 | \n2896 |         By using 'last', the last occurrence of each set of duplicated values\n2897 |         is set on False and all others on True:\n2898 | \n2899 |         >>> idx.duplicated(keep=\"last\")\n2900 |         array([ True, False,  True, False, False])\n2901 | \n2902 |         By setting keep on ``False``, all duplicates are True:\n2903 | \n2904 |         >>> idx.duplicated(keep=False)\n2905 |         array([ True, False,  True, False,  True])\n2906 |         \"\"\"\n2907 |         if self.is_unique:\n2908 |             # fastpath available bc we are immutable\n2909 |             return np.zeros(len(self), dtype=bool)\n2910 |         return self._duplicated(keep=keep)\n2911 | \n2912 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n2913 |     # Arithmetic & Logical Methods\n2914 | \n2915 |     def __iadd__(self, other):\n2916 |         # alias for __add__\n2917 |         return self + other\n2918 | \n2919 |     @final\n2920 |     def __bool__(self) -> NoReturn:\n2921 |         raise ValueError(\n2922 |             f\"The truth value of a {type(self).__name__} is ambiguous. \"\n2923 |             \"Use a.empty, a.bool(), a.item(), a.any() or a.all().\"\n2924 |         )\n2925 | \n2926 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n2927 |     # Set Operation Methods\n2928 | \n2929 |     def _get_reconciled_name_object(self, other):\n2930 |         \"\"\"\n2931 |         If the result of a set operation will be self,\n2932 |         return self, unless the name changes, in which\n2933 |         case make a shallow copy of self.\n2934 |         \"\"\"\n2935 |         name = get_op_result_name(self, other)\n2936 |         if self.name is not name:\n2937 |             return self.rename(name)\n2938 |         return self\n2939 | \n2940 |     @final\n2941 |     def _validate_sort_keyword(self, sort) -> None:\n2942 |         if sort not in [None, False, True]:\n2943 |             raise ValueError(\n2944 |                 \"The 'sort' keyword only takes the values of \"\n2945 |                 f\"None, True, or False; {sort} was passed.\"\n2946 |             )\n2947 | \n2948 |     @final\n2949 |     def _dti_setop_align_tzs(self, other: Index, setop: str_t) -> tuple[Index, Index]:\n2950 |         \"\"\"\n2951 |         With mismatched timezones, cast both to UTC.\n2952 |         \"\"\"\n2953 |         # Caller is responsible for checking\n2954 |         #  `self.dtype != other.dtype`\n2955 |         if (\n2956 |             isinstance(self, ABCDatetimeIndex)\n2957 |             and isinstance(other, ABCDatetimeIndex)\n2958 |             and self.tz is not None\n2959 |             and other.tz is not None\n2960 |         ):\n2961 |             # GH#39328, GH#45357\n2962 |             left = self.tz_convert(\"UTC\")\n2963 |             right = other.tz_convert(\"UTC\")\n2964 |             return left, right\n2965 |         return self, other\n2966 | \n2967 |     @final\n2968 |     def union(self, other, sort: bool | None = None):\n2969 |         \"\"\"\n2970 |         Form the union of two Index objects.\n2971 | \n2972 |         If the Index objects are incompatible, both Index objects will be\n2973 |         cast to dtype('object') first.\n2974 | \n2975 |         Parameters\n2976 |         ----------\n2977 |         other : Index or array-like\n2978 |             Index or an array-like object containing elements to form the union\n2979 |             with the original Index.\n2980 |         sort : bool or None, default None\n2981 |             Whether to sort the resulting Index.\n2982 | \n2983 |             * None : Sort the result, except when\n2984 | \n2985 |               1. `self` and `other` are equal.\n2986 |               2. `self` or `other` has length 0.\n2987 |               3. Some values in `self` or `other` cannot be compared.\n2988 |                  A RuntimeWarning is issued in this case.\n2989 | \n2990 |             * False : do not sort the result.\n2991 |             * True : Sort the result (which may raise TypeError).\n2992 | \n2993 |         Returns\n2994 |         -------\n2995 |         Index\n2996 |             Returns a new Index object with all unique elements from both the original\n2997 |             Index and the `other` Index.\n2998 | \n2999 |         See Also\n3000 |         --------\n3001 |         Index.unique : Return unique values in the index.\n3002 |         Index.intersection : Form the intersection of two Index objects.\n3003 |         Index.difference : Return a new Index with elements of index not in `other`.\n3004 | \n3005 |         Examples\n3006 |         --------\n3007 |         Union matching dtypes\n3008 | \n3009 |         >>> idx1 = pd.Index([1, 2, 3, 4])\n3010 |         >>> idx2 = pd.Index([3, 4, 5, 6])\n3011 |         >>> idx1.union(idx2)\n3012 |         Index([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], dtype='int64')\n3013 | \n3014 |         Union mismatched dtypes\n3015 | \n3016 |         >>> idx1 = pd.Index([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\"])\n3017 |         >>> idx2 = pd.Index([1, 2, 3, 4])\n3018 |         >>> idx1.union(idx2)\n3019 |         Index(['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 1, 2, 3, 4], dtype='object')\n3020 | \n3021 |         MultiIndex case\n3022 | \n3023 |         >>> idx1 = pd.MultiIndex.from_arrays(\n3024 |         ...     [[1, 1, 2, 2], [\"Red\", \"Blue\", \"Red\", \"Blue\"]]\n3025 |         ... )\n3026 |         >>> idx1\n3027 |         MultiIndex([(1,  'Red'),\n3028 |             (1, 'Blue'),\n3029 |             (2,  'Red'),\n3030 |             (2, 'Blue')],\n3031 |            )\n3032 |         >>> idx2 = pd.MultiIndex.from_arrays(\n3033 |         ...     [[3, 3, 2, 2], [\"Red\", \"Green\", \"Red\", \"Green\"]]\n3034 |         ... )\n3035 |         >>> idx2\n3036 |         MultiIndex([(3,   'Red'),\n3037 |             (3, 'Green'),\n3038 |             (2,   'Red'),\n3039 |             (2, 'Green')],\n3040 |            )\n3041 |         >>> idx1.union(idx2)\n3042 |         MultiIndex([(1,  'Blue'),\n3043 |             (1,   'Red'),\n3044 |             (2,  'Blue'),\n3045 |             (2, 'Green'),\n3046 |             (2,   'Red'),\n3047 |             (3, 'Green'),\n3048 |             (3,   'Red')],\n3049 |            )\n3050 |         >>> idx1.union(idx2, sort=False)\n3051 |         MultiIndex([(1,   'Red'),\n3052 |             (1,  'Blue'),\n3053 |             (2,   'Red'),\n3054 |             (2,  'Blue'),\n3055 |             (3,   'Red'),\n3056 |             (3, 'Green'),\n3057 |             (2, 'Green')],\n3058 |            )\n3059 |         \"\"\"\n3060 |         self._validate_sort_keyword(sort)\n3061 |         self._assert_can_do_setop(other)\n3062 |         other, result_name = self._convert_can_do_setop(other)\n3063 | \n3064 |         if self.dtype != other.dtype:\n3065 |             if (\n3066 |                 isinstance(self, ABCMultiIndex)\n3067 |                 and not is_object_dtype(_unpack_nested_dtype(other))\n3068 |                 and len(other) > 0\n3069 |             ):\n3070 |                 raise NotImplementedError(\n3071 |                     \"Can only union MultiIndex with MultiIndex or Index of tuples, \"\n3072 |                     \"try mi.to_flat_index().union(other) instead.\"\n3073 |                 )\n3074 |             self, other = self._dti_setop_align_tzs(other, \"union\")\n3075 | \n3076 |             dtype = self._find_common_type_compat(other)\n3077 |             left = self.astype(dtype, copy=False)\n3078 |             right = other.astype(dtype, copy=False)\n3079 |             return left.union(right, sort=sort)\n3080 | \n3081 |         elif not len(other) or self.equals(other):\n3082 |             # NB: whether this (and the `if not len(self)` check below) come before\n3083 |             #  or after the dtype equality check above affects the returned dtype\n3084 |             result = self._get_reconciled_name_object(other)\n3085 |             if sort is True:\n3086 |                 return result.sort_values()\n3087 |             return result\n3088 | \n3089 |         elif not len(self):\n3090 |             result = other._get_reconciled_name_object(self)\n3091 |             if sort is True:\n3092 |                 return result.sort_values()\n3093 |             return result\n3094 | \n3095 |         result = self._union(other, sort=sort)\n3096 | \n3097 |         return self._wrap_setop_result(other, result)\n3098 | \n3099 |     def _union(self, other: Index, sort: bool | None):\n3100 |         \"\"\"\n3101 |         Specific union logic should go here. In subclasses, union behavior\n3102 |         should be overwritten here rather than in `self.union`.\n3103 | \n3104 |         Parameters\n3105 |         ----------\n3106 |         other : Index or array-like\n3107 |         sort : False or None, default False\n3108 |             Whether to sort the resulting index.\n3109 | \n3110 |             * True : sort the result\n3111 |             * False : do not sort the result.\n3112 |             * None : sort the result, except when `self` and `other` are equal\n3113 |               or when the values cannot be compared.\n3114 | \n3115 |         Returns\n3116 |         -------\n3117 |         Index\n3118 |         \"\"\"\n3119 |         lvals = self._values\n3120 |         rvals = other._values\n3121 | \n3122 |         if (\n3123 |             sort in (None, True)\n3124 |             and (self.is_unique or other.is_unique)\n3125 |             and self._can_use_libjoin\n3126 |             and other._can_use_libjoin\n3127 |         ):\n3128 |             # Both are monotonic and at least one is unique, so can use outer join\n3129 |             #  (actually don't need either unique, but without this restriction\n3130 |             #  test_union_same_value_duplicated_in_both fails)\n3131 |             try:\n3132 |                 return self._outer_indexer(other)[0]\n3133 |             except (TypeError, IncompatibleFrequency):\n3134 |                 # incomparable objects; should only be for object dtype\n3135 |                 value_list = list(lvals)\n3136 | \n3137 |                 # worth making this faster? a very unusual case\n3138 |                 value_set = set(lvals)\n3139 |                 value_list.extend(x for x in rvals if x not in value_set)\n3140 |                 # If objects are unorderable, we must have object dtype.\n3141 |                 return np.array(value_list, dtype=object)\n3142 | \n3143 |         elif not other.is_unique:\n3144 |             # other has duplicates\n3145 |             result_dups = algos.union_with_duplicates(self, other)\n3146 |             return _maybe_try_sort(result_dups, sort)\n3147 | \n3148 |         # The rest of this method is analogous to Index._intersection_via_get_indexer\n3149 | \n3150 |         # Self may have duplicates; other already checked as unique\n3151 |         # find indexes of things in \"other\" that are not in \"self\"\n3152 |         if self._index_as_unique:\n3153 |             indexer = self.get_indexer(other)\n3154 |             missing = (indexer == -1).nonzero()[0]\n3155 |         else:\n3156 |             missing = algos.unique1d(self.get_indexer_non_unique(other)[1])\n3157 | \n3158 |         result: Index | MultiIndex | ArrayLike\n3159 |         if self._is_multi:\n3160 |             # Preserve MultiIndex to avoid losing dtypes\n3161 |             result = self.append(other.take(missing))\n3162 | \n3163 |         else:\n3164 |             if len(missing) > 0:\n3165 |                 other_diff = rvals.take(missing)\n3166 |                 result = concat_compat((lvals, other_diff))\n3167 |             else:\n3168 |                 result = lvals\n3169 | \n3170 |         if not self.is_monotonic_increasing or not other.is_monotonic_increasing:\n3171 |             # if both are monotonic then result should already be sorted\n3172 |             result = _maybe_try_sort(result, sort)\n3173 | \n3174 |         return result\n3175 | \n3176 |     @final\n3177 |     def _wrap_setop_result(self, other: Index, result) -> Index:\n3178 |         name = get_op_result_name(self, other)\n3179 |         if isinstance(result, Index):\n3180 |             if result.name != name:\n3181 |                 result = result.rename(name)\n3182 |         else:\n3183 |             result = self._shallow_copy(result, name=name)\n3184 |         return result\n3185 | \n3186 |     @final\n3187 |     def intersection(self, other, sort: bool = False):\n3188 |         # default sort keyword is different here from other setops intentionally\n3189 |         #  done in GH#25063\n3190 |         \"\"\"\n3191 |         Form the intersection of two Index objects.\n3192 | \n3193 |         This returns a new Index with elements common to the index and `other`.\n3194 | \n3195 |         Parameters\n3196 |         ----------\n3197 |         other : Index or array-like\n3198 |             An Index or an array-like object containing elements to form the\n3199 |             intersection with the original Index.\n3200 |         sort : True, False or None, default False\n3201 |             Whether to sort the resulting index.\n3202 | \n3203 |             * None : sort the result, except when `self` and `other` are equal\n3204 |               or when the values cannot be compared.\n3205 |             * False : do not sort the result.\n3206 |             * True : Sort the result (which may raise TypeError).\n3207 | \n3208 |         Returns\n3209 |         -------\n3210 |         Index\n3211 |             Returns a new Index object with elements common to both the original Index\n3212 |             and the `other` Index.\n3213 | \n3214 |         See Also\n3215 |         --------\n3216 |         Index.union : Form the union of two Index objects.\n3217 |         Index.difference : Return a new Index with elements of index not in other.\n3218 |         Index.isin : Return a boolean array where the index values are in values.\n3219 | \n3220 |         Examples\n3221 |         --------\n3222 |         >>> idx1 = pd.Index([1, 2, 3, 4])\n3223 |         >>> idx2 = pd.Index([3, 4, 5, 6])\n3224 |         >>> idx1.intersection(idx2)\n3225 |         Index([3, 4], dtype='int64')\n3226 |         \"\"\"\n3227 |         self._validate_sort_keyword(sort)\n3228 |         self._assert_can_do_setop(other)\n3229 |         other, result_name = self._convert_can_do_setop(other)\n3230 | \n3231 |         if self.dtype != other.dtype:\n3232 |             self, other = self._dti_setop_align_tzs(other, \"intersection\")\n3233 | \n3234 |         if self.equals(other):\n3235 |             if not self.is_unique:\n3236 |                 result = self.unique()._get_reconciled_name_object(other)\n3237 |             else:\n3238 |                 result = self._get_reconciled_name_object(other)\n3239 |             if sort is True:\n3240 |                 result = result.sort_values()\n3241 |             return result\n3242 | \n3243 |         if len(self) == 0 or len(other) == 0:\n3244 |             # fastpath; we need to be careful about having commutativity\n3245 | \n3246 |             if self._is_multi or other._is_multi:\n3247 |                 # _convert_can_do_setop ensures that we have both or neither\n3248 |                 # We retain self.levels\n3249 |                 return self[:0].rename(result_name)\n3250 | \n3251 |             dtype = self._find_common_type_compat(other)\n3252 |             if self.dtype == dtype:\n3253 |                 # Slicing allows us to retain DTI/TDI.freq, RangeIndex\n3254 | \n3255 |                 # Note: self[:0] vs other[:0] affects\n3256 |                 #  1) which index's `freq` we get in DTI/TDI cases\n3257 |                 #     This may be a historical artifact, i.e. no documented\n3258 |                 #     reason for this choice.\n3259 |                 #  2) The `step` we get in RangeIndex cases\n3260 |                 if len(self) == 0:\n3261 |                     return self[:0].rename(result_name)\n3262 |                 else:\n3263 |                     return other[:0].rename(result_name)\n3264 | \n3265 |             return Index([], dtype=dtype, name=result_name)\n3266 | \n3267 |         elif not self._should_compare(other):\n3268 |             # We can infer that the intersection is empty.\n3269 |             if isinstance(self, ABCMultiIndex):\n3270 |                 return self[:0].rename(result_name)\n3271 |             return Index([], name=result_name)\n3272 | \n3273 |         elif self.dtype != other.dtype:\n3274 |             dtype = self._find_common_type_compat(other)\n3275 |             this = self.astype(dtype, copy=False)\n3276 |             other = other.astype(dtype, copy=False)\n3277 |             return this.intersection(other, sort=sort)\n3278 | \n3279 |         result = self._intersection(other, sort=sort)\n3280 |         return self._wrap_intersection_result(other, result)\n3281 | \n3282 |     def _intersection(self, other: Index, sort: bool = False):\n3283 |         \"\"\"\n3284 |         intersection specialized to the case with matching dtypes.\n3285 |         \"\"\"\n3286 |         if self._can_use_libjoin and other._can_use_libjoin:\n3287 |             try:\n3288 |                 res_indexer, indexer, _ = self._inner_indexer(other)\n3289 |             except TypeError:\n3290 |                 # non-comparable; should only be for object dtype\n3291 |                 pass\n3292 |             else:\n3293 |                 # TODO: algos.unique1d should preserve DTA/TDA\n3294 |                 if is_numeric_dtype(self.dtype):\n3295 |                     # This is faster, because Index.unique() checks for uniqueness\n3296 |                     # before calculating the unique values.\n3297 |                     res = algos.unique1d(res_indexer)\n3298 |                 else:\n3299 |                     result = self.take(indexer)\n3300 |                     res = result.drop_duplicates()\n3301 |                 return ensure_wrapped_if_datetimelike(res)\n3302 | \n3303 |         res_values = self._intersection_via_get_indexer(other, sort=sort)\n3304 |         res_values = _maybe_try_sort(res_values, sort)\n3305 |         return res_values\n3306 | \n3307 |     def _wrap_intersection_result(self, other, result):\n3308 |         # We will override for MultiIndex to handle empty results\n3309 |         return self._wrap_setop_result(other, result)\n3310 | \n3311 |     @final\n3312 |     def _intersection_via_get_indexer(\n3313 |         self, other: Index | MultiIndex, sort\n3314 |     ) -> ArrayLike | MultiIndex:\n3315 |         \"\"\"\n3316 |         Find the intersection of two Indexes using get_indexer.\n3317 | \n3318 |         Returns\n3319 |         -------\n3320 |         np.ndarray or ExtensionArray or MultiIndex\n3321 |             The returned array will be unique.\n3322 |         \"\"\"\n3323 |         left_unique = self.unique()\n3324 |         right_unique = other.unique()\n3325 | \n3326 |         # even though we are unique, we need get_indexer_for for IntervalIndex\n3327 |         indexer = left_unique.get_indexer_for(right_unique)\n3328 | \n3329 |         mask = indexer != -1\n3330 | \n3331 |         taker = indexer.take(mask.nonzero()[0])\n3332 |         if sort is False:\n3333 |             # sort bc we want the elements in the same order they are in self\n3334 |             # unnecessary in the case with sort=None bc we will sort later\n3335 |             taker = np.sort(taker)\n3336 | \n3337 |         result: MultiIndex | ExtensionArray | np.ndarray\n3338 |         if isinstance(left_unique, ABCMultiIndex):\n3339 |             result = left_unique.take(taker)\n3340 |         else:\n3341 |             result = left_unique.take(taker)._values\n3342 |         return result\n3343 | \n3344 |     @final\n3345 |     def difference(self, other, sort: bool | None = None):\n3346 |         \"\"\"\n3347 |         Return a new Index with elements of index not in `other`.\n3348 | \n3349 |         This is the set difference of two Index objects.\n3350 | \n3351 |         Parameters\n3352 |         ----------\n3353 |         other : Index or array-like\n3354 |             Index object or an array-like object containing elements to be compared\n3355 |             with the elements of the original Index.\n3356 |         sort : bool or None, default None\n3357 |             Whether to sort the resulting index. By default, the\n3358 |             values are attempted to be sorted, but any TypeError from\n3359 |             incomparable elements is caught by pandas.\n3360 | \n3361 |             * None : Attempt to sort the result, but catch any TypeErrors\n3362 |               from comparing incomparable elements.\n3363 |             * False : Do not sort the result.\n3364 |             * True : Sort the result (which may raise TypeError).\n3365 | \n3366 |         Returns\n3367 |         -------\n3368 |         Index\n3369 |             Returns a new Index object containing elements that are in the original\n3370 |             Index but not in the `other` Index.\n3371 | \n3372 |         See Also\n3373 |         --------\n3374 |         Index.symmetric_difference : Compute the symmetric difference of two Index\n3375 |             objects.\n3376 |         Index.intersection : Form the intersection of two Index objects.\n3377 | \n3378 |         Examples\n3379 |         --------\n3380 |         >>> idx1 = pd.Index([2, 1, 3, 4])\n3381 |         >>> idx2 = pd.Index([3, 4, 5, 6])\n3382 |         >>> idx1.difference(idx2)\n3383 |         Index([1, 2], dtype='int64')\n3384 |         >>> idx1.difference(idx2, sort=False)\n3385 |         Index([2, 1], dtype='int64')\n3386 |         \"\"\"\n3387 |         self._validate_sort_keyword(sort)\n3388 |         self._assert_can_do_setop(other)\n3389 |         other, result_name = self._convert_can_do_setop(other)\n3390 | \n3391 |         # Note: we do NOT call _dti_setop_align_tzs here, as there\n3392 |         #  is no requirement that .difference be commutative, so it does\n3393 |         #  not cast to object.\n3394 | \n3395 |         if self.equals(other):\n3396 |             # Note: we do not (yet) sort even if sort=None GH#24959\n3397 |             return self[:0].rename(result_name)\n3398 | \n3399 |         if len(other) == 0:\n3400 |             # Note: we do not (yet) sort even if sort=None GH#24959\n3401 |             result = self.unique().rename(result_name)\n3402 |             if sort is True:\n3403 |                 return result.sort_values()\n3404 |             return result\n3405 | \n3406 |         if not self._should_compare(other):\n3407 |             # Nothing matches -> difference is everything\n3408 |             result = self.unique().rename(result_name)\n3409 |             if sort is True:\n3410 |                 return result.sort_values()\n3411 |             return result\n3412 | \n3413 |         result = self._difference(other, sort=sort)\n3414 |         return self._wrap_difference_result(other, result)\n3415 | \n3416 |     def _difference(self, other, sort):\n3417 |         # overridden by RangeIndex\n3418 |         this = self\n3419 |         if isinstance(self, ABCCategoricalIndex) and self.hasnans and other.hasnans:\n3420 |             this = this.dropna()\n3421 |         other = other.unique()\n3422 |         the_diff = this[other.get_indexer_for(this) == -1]\n3423 |         the_diff = the_diff if this.is_unique else the_diff.unique()\n3424 |         the_diff = _maybe_try_sort(the_diff, sort)\n3425 |         return the_diff\n3426 | \n3427 |     def _wrap_difference_result(self, other, result):\n3428 |         # We will override for MultiIndex to handle empty results\n3429 |         return self._wrap_setop_result(other, result)\n3430 | \n3431 |     def symmetric_difference(\n3432 |         self,\n3433 |         other,\n3434 |         result_name: abc.Hashable | None = None,\n3435 |         sort: bool | None = None,\n3436 |     ):\n3437 |         \"\"\"\n3438 |         Compute the symmetric difference of two Index objects.\n3439 | \n3440 |         Parameters\n3441 |         ----------\n3442 |         other : Index or array-like\n3443 |             Index or an array-like object with elements to compute the symmetric\n3444 |             difference with the original Index.\n3445 |         result_name : str\n3446 |             A string representing the name of the resulting Index, if desired.\n3447 |         sort : bool or None, default None\n3448 |             Whether to sort the resulting index. By default, the\n3449 |             values are attempted to be sorted, but any TypeError from\n3450 |             incomparable elements is caught by pandas.\n3451 | \n3452 |             * None : Attempt to sort the result, but catch any TypeErrors\n3453 |               from comparing incomparable elements.\n3454 |             * False : Do not sort the result.\n3455 |             * True : Sort the result (which may raise TypeError).\n3456 | \n3457 |         Returns\n3458 |         -------\n3459 |         Index\n3460 |             Returns a new Index object containing elements that appear in either the\n3461 |             original Index or the `other` Index, but not both.\n3462 | \n3463 |         See Also\n3464 |         --------\n3465 |         Index.difference : Return a new Index with elements of index not in other.\n3466 |         Index.union : Form the union of two Index objects.\n3467 |         Index.intersection : Form the intersection of two Index objects.\n3468 | \n3469 |         Notes\n3470 |         -----\n3471 |         ``symmetric_difference`` contains elements that appear in either\n3472 |         ``idx1`` or ``idx2`` but not both. Equivalent to the Index created by\n3473 |         ``idx1.difference(idx2) | idx2.difference(idx1)`` with duplicates\n3474 |         dropped.\n3475 | \n3476 |         Examples\n3477 |         --------\n3478 |         >>> idx1 = pd.Index([1, 2, 3, 4])\n3479 |         >>> idx2 = pd.Index([2, 3, 4, 5])\n3480 |         >>> idx1.symmetric_difference(idx2)\n3481 |         Index([1, 5], dtype='int64')\n3482 |         \"\"\"\n3483 |         self._validate_sort_keyword(sort)\n3484 |         self._assert_can_do_setop(other)\n3485 |         other, result_name_update = self._convert_can_do_setop(other)\n3486 |         if result_name is None:\n3487 |             result_name = result_name_update\n3488 | \n3489 |         if self.dtype != other.dtype:\n3490 |             self, other = self._dti_setop_align_tzs(other, \"symmetric_difference\")\n3491 | \n3492 |         if not self._should_compare(other):\n3493 |             return self.union(other, sort=sort).rename(result_name)\n3494 | \n3495 |         elif self.dtype != other.dtype:\n3496 |             dtype = self._find_common_type_compat(other)\n3497 |             this = self.astype(dtype, copy=False)\n3498 |             that = other.astype(dtype, copy=False)\n3499 |             return this.symmetric_difference(that, sort=sort).rename(result_name)\n3500 | \n3501 |         this = self.unique()\n3502 |         other = other.unique()\n3503 |         indexer = this.get_indexer_for(other)\n3504 | \n3505 |         # {this} minus {other}\n3506 |         common_indexer = indexer.take((indexer != -1).nonzero()[0])\n3507 |         left_indexer = np.setdiff1d(\n3508 |             np.arange(this.size), common_indexer, assume_unique=True\n3509 |         )\n3510 |         left_diff = this.take(left_indexer)\n3511 | \n3512 |         # {other} minus {this}\n3513 |         right_indexer = (indexer == -1).nonzero()[0]\n3514 |         right_diff = other.take(right_indexer)\n3515 | \n3516 |         res_values = left_diff.append(right_diff)\n3517 |         result = _maybe_try_sort(res_values, sort)\n3518 | \n3519 |         if not self._is_multi:\n3520 |             return Index(result, name=result_name, dtype=res_values.dtype)\n3521 |         else:\n3522 |             left_diff = cast(\"MultiIndex\", left_diff)\n3523 |             if len(result) == 0:\n3524 |                 # result might be an Index, if other was an Index\n3525 |                 return left_diff.remove_unused_levels().set_names(result_name)\n3526 |             return result.set_names(result_name)\n3527 | \n3528 |     @final\n3529 |     def _assert_can_do_setop(self, other) -> bool:\n3530 |         if not is_list_like(other):\n3531 |             raise TypeError(\"Input must be Index or array-like\")\n3532 |         return True\n3533 | \n3534 |     def _convert_can_do_setop(self, other) -> tuple[Index, Hashable]:\n3535 |         if not isinstance(other, Index):\n3536 |             other = Index(other, name=self.name)\n3537 |             result_name = self.name\n3538 |         else:\n3539 |             result_name = get_op_result_name(self, other)\n3540 |         return other, result_name\n3541 | \n3542 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n3543 |     # Indexing Methods\n3544 | \n3545 |     def get_loc(self, key):\n3546 |         \"\"\"\n3547 |         Get integer location, slice or boolean mask for requested label.\n3548 | \n3549 |         Parameters\n3550 |         ----------\n3551 |         key : label\n3552 |             The key to check its location if it is present in the index.\n3553 | \n3554 |         Returns\n3555 |         -------\n3556 |         int if unique index, slice if monotonic index, else mask\n3557 |             Integer location, slice or boolean mask.\n3558 | \n3559 |         See Also\n3560 |         --------\n3561 |         Index.get_slice_bound : Calculate slice bound that corresponds to\n3562 |             given label.\n3563 |         Index.get_indexer : Computes indexer and mask for new index given\n3564 |             the current index.\n3565 |         Index.get_non_unique : Returns indexer and masks for new index given\n3566 |             the current index.\n3567 |         Index.get_indexer_for : Returns an indexer even when non-unique.\n3568 | \n3569 |         Examples\n3570 |         --------\n3571 |         >>> unique_index = pd.Index(list(\"abc\"))\n3572 |         >>> unique_index.get_loc(\"b\")\n3573 |         1\n3574 | \n3575 |         >>> monotonic_index = pd.Index(list(\"abbc\"))\n3576 |         >>> monotonic_index.get_loc(\"b\")\n3577 |         slice(1, 3, None)\n3578 | \n3579 |         >>> non_monotonic_index = pd.Index(list(\"abcb\"))\n3580 |         >>> non_monotonic_index.get_loc(\"b\")\n3581 |         array([False,  True, False,  True])\n3582 |         \"\"\"\n3583 |         casted_key = self._maybe_cast_indexer(key)\n3584 |         try:\n3585 |             return self._engine.get_loc(casted_key)\n3586 |         except KeyError as err:\n3587 |             if isinstance(casted_key, slice) or (\n3588 |                 isinstance(casted_key, abc.Iterable)\n3589 |                 and any(isinstance(x, slice) for x in casted_key)\n3590 |             ):\n3591 |                 raise InvalidIndexError(key) from err\n3592 |             raise KeyError(key) from err\n3593 |         except TypeError:\n3594 |             # If we have a listlike key, _check_indexing_error will raise\n3595 |             #  InvalidIndexError. Otherwise we fall through and re-raise\n3596 |             #  the TypeError.\n3597 |             self._check_indexing_error(key)\n3598 |             raise\n3599 | \n3600 |     @final\n3601 |     def get_indexer(\n3602 |         self,\n3603 |         target,\n3604 |         method: ReindexMethod | None = None,\n3605 |         limit: int | None = None,\n3606 |         tolerance=None,\n3607 |     ) -> npt.NDArray[np.intp]:\n3608 |         \"\"\"\n3609 |         Compute indexer and mask for new index given the current index.\n3610 | \n3611 |         The indexer should be then used as an input to ndarray.take to align the\n3612 |         current data to the new index.\n3613 | \n3614 |         Parameters\n3615 |         ----------\n3616 |         target : Index\n3617 |             An iterable containing the values to be used for computing indexer.\n3618 |         method : {None, 'pad'/'ffill', 'backfill'/'bfill', 'nearest'}, optional\n3619 |             * default: exact matches only.\n3620 |             * pad / ffill: find the PREVIOUS index value if no exact match.\n3621 |             * backfill / bfill: use NEXT index value if no exact match\n3622 |             * nearest: use the NEAREST index value if no exact match. Tied\n3623 |               distances are broken by preferring the larger index value.\n3624 |         limit : int, optional\n3625 |             Maximum number of consecutive labels in ``target`` to match for\n3626 |             inexact matches.\n3627 |         tolerance : optional\n3628 |             Maximum distance between original and new labels for inexact\n3629 |             matches. The values of the index at the matching locations must\n3630 |             satisfy the equation ``abs(index[indexer] - target) <= tolerance``.\n3631 | \n3632 |             Tolerance may be a scalar value, which applies the same tolerance\n3633 |             to all values, or list-like, which applies variable tolerance per\n3634 |             element. List-like includes list, tuple, array, Series, and must be\n3635 |             the same size as the index and its dtype must exactly match the\n3636 |             index's type.\n3637 | \n3638 |         Returns\n3639 |         -------\n3640 |         np.ndarray[np.intp]\n3641 |             Integers from 0 to n - 1 indicating that the index at these\n3642 |             positions matches the corresponding target values. Missing values\n3643 |             in the target are marked by -1.\n3644 | \n3645 |         See Also\n3646 |         --------\n3647 |         Index.get_indexer_for : Returns an indexer even when non-unique.\n3648 |         Index.get_non_unique : Returns indexer and masks for new index given\n3649 |             the current index.\n3650 | \n3651 |         Notes\n3652 |         -----\n3653 |         Returns -1 for unmatched values, for further explanation see the\n3654 |         example below.\n3655 | \n3656 |         Examples\n3657 |         --------\n3658 |         >>> index = pd.Index([\"c\", \"a\", \"b\"])\n3659 |         >>> index.get_indexer([\"a\", \"b\", \"x\"])\n3660 |         array([ 1,  2, -1])\n3661 | \n3662 |         Notice that the return value is an array of locations in ``index``\n3663 |         and ``x`` is marked by -1, as it is not in ``index``.\n3664 |         \"\"\"\n3665 |         method = clean_reindex_fill_method(method)\n3666 |         orig_target = target\n3667 |         target = self._maybe_cast_listlike_indexer(target)\n3668 | \n3669 |         self._check_indexing_method(method, limit, tolerance)\n3670 | \n3671 |         if not self._index_as_unique:\n3672 |             raise InvalidIndexError(self._requires_unique_msg)\n3673 | \n3674 |         if len(target) == 0:\n3675 |             return np.array([], dtype=np.intp)\n3676 | \n3677 |         if not self._should_compare(target) and not self._should_partial_index(target):\n3678 |             # IntervalIndex get special treatment bc numeric scalars can be\n3679 |             #  matched to Interval scalars\n3680 |             return self._get_indexer_non_comparable(target, method=method, unique=True)\n3681 | \n3682 |         if isinstance(self.dtype, CategoricalDtype):\n3683 |             # _maybe_cast_listlike_indexer ensures target has our dtype\n3684 |             #  (could improve perf by doing _should_compare check earlier?)\n3685 |             assert self.dtype == target.dtype\n3686 | \n3687 |             indexer = self._engine.get_indexer(target.codes)\n3688 |             if self.hasnans and target.hasnans:\n3689 |                 # After _maybe_cast_listlike_indexer, target elements which do not\n3690 |                 # belong to some category are changed to NaNs\n3691 |                 # Mask to track actual NaN values compared to inserted NaN values\n3692 |                 # GH#45361\n3693 |                 target_nans = isna(orig_target)\n3694 |                 loc = self.get_loc(np.nan)\n3695 |                 mask = target.isna()\n3696 |                 indexer[target_nans] = loc\n3697 |                 indexer[mask & ~target_nans] = -1\n3698 |             return indexer\n3699 | \n3700 |         if isinstance(target.dtype, CategoricalDtype):\n3701 |             # potential fastpath\n3702 |             # get an indexer for unique categories then propagate to codes via take_nd\n3703 |             # get_indexer instead of _get_indexer needed for MultiIndex cases\n3704 |             #  e.g. test_append_different_columns_types\n3705 |             categories_indexer = self.get_indexer(target.categories)\n3706 | \n3707 |             indexer = algos.take_nd(categories_indexer, target.codes, fill_value=-1)\n3708 | \n3709 |             if (not self._is_multi and self.hasnans) and target.hasnans:\n3710 |                 # Exclude MultiIndex because hasnans raises NotImplementedError\n3711 |                 # we should only get here if we are unique, so loc is an integer\n3712 |                 # GH#41934\n3713 |                 loc = self.get_loc(np.nan)\n3714 |                 mask = target.isna()\n3715 |                 indexer[mask] = loc\n3716 | \n3717 |             return ensure_platform_int(indexer)\n3718 | \n3719 |         pself, ptarget = self._maybe_downcast_for_indexing(target)\n3720 |         if pself is not self or ptarget is not target:\n3721 |             return pself.get_indexer(\n3722 |                 ptarget, method=method, limit=limit, tolerance=tolerance\n3723 |             )\n3724 | \n3725 |         if self.dtype == target.dtype and self.equals(target):\n3726 |             # Only call equals if we have same dtype to avoid inference/casting\n3727 |             return np.arange(len(target), dtype=np.intp)\n3728 | \n3729 |         if self.dtype != target.dtype and not self._should_partial_index(target):\n3730 |             # _should_partial_index e.g. IntervalIndex with numeric scalars\n3731 |             #  that can be matched to Interval scalars.\n3732 |             dtype = self._find_common_type_compat(target)\n3733 | \n3734 |             this = self.astype(dtype, copy=False)\n3735 |             target = target.astype(dtype, copy=False)\n3736 |             return this._get_indexer(\n3737 |                 target, method=method, limit=limit, tolerance=tolerance\n3738 |             )\n3739 | \n3740 |         return self._get_indexer(target, method, limit, tolerance)\n3741 | \n3742 |     def _get_indexer(\n3743 |         self,\n3744 |         target: Index,\n3745 |         method: str_t | None = None,\n3746 |         limit: int | None = None,\n3747 |         tolerance=None,\n3748 |     ) -> npt.NDArray[np.intp]:\n3749 |         if tolerance is not None:\n3750 |             tolerance = self._convert_tolerance(tolerance, target)\n3751 | \n3752 |         if method in [\"pad\", \"backfill\"]:\n3753 |             indexer = self._get_fill_indexer(target, method, limit, tolerance)\n3754 |         elif method == \"nearest\":\n3755 |             indexer = self._get_nearest_indexer(target, limit, tolerance)\n3756 |         else:\n3757 |             if target._is_multi and self._is_multi:\n3758 |                 engine = self._engine\n3759 |                 # error: Item \"IndexEngine\" of \"Union[IndexEngine, ExtensionEngine]\"\n3760 |                 # has no attribute \"_extract_level_codes\"\n3761 |                 tgt_values = engine._extract_level_codes(  # type: ignore[union-attr]\n3762 |                     target\n3763 |                 )\n3764 |             else:\n3765 |                 tgt_values = target._get_engine_target()\n3766 | \n3767 |             indexer = self._engine.get_indexer(tgt_values)\n3768 | \n3769 |         return ensure_platform_int(indexer)\n3770 | \n3771 |     @final\n3772 |     def _should_partial_index(self, target: Index) -> bool:\n3773 |         \"\"\"\n3774 |         Should we attempt partial-matching indexing?\n3775 |         \"\"\"\n3776 |         if isinstance(self.dtype, IntervalDtype):\n3777 |             if isinstance(target.dtype, IntervalDtype):\n3778 |                 return False\n3779 |             # \"Index\" has no attribute \"left\"\n3780 |             return self.left._should_compare(target)  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n3781 |         return False\n3782 | \n3783 |     @final\n3784 |     def _check_indexing_method(\n3785 |         self,\n3786 |         method: str_t | None,\n3787 |         limit: int | None = None,\n3788 |         tolerance=None,\n3789 |     ) -> None:\n3790 |         \"\"\"\n3791 |         Raise if we have a get_indexer `method` that is not supported or valid.\n3792 |         \"\"\"\n3793 |         if method not in [None, \"bfill\", \"backfill\", \"pad\", \"ffill\", \"nearest\"]:\n3794 |             # in practice the clean_reindex_fill_method call would raise\n3795 |             #  before we get here\n3796 |             raise ValueError(\"Invalid fill method\")  # pragma: no cover\n3797 | \n3798 |         if self._is_multi:\n3799 |             if method == \"nearest\":\n3800 |                 raise NotImplementedError(\n3801 |                     \"method='nearest' not implemented yet \"\n3802 |                     \"for MultiIndex; see GitHub issue 9365\"\n3803 |                 )\n3804 |             if method in (\"pad\", \"backfill\"):\n3805 |                 if tolerance is not None:\n3806 |                     raise NotImplementedError(\n3807 |                         \"tolerance not implemented yet for MultiIndex\"\n3808 |                     )\n3809 | \n3810 |         if isinstance(self.dtype, (IntervalDtype, CategoricalDtype)):\n3811 |             # GH#37871 for now this is only for IntervalIndex and CategoricalIndex\n3812 |             if method is not None:\n3813 |                 raise NotImplementedError(\n3814 |                     f\"method {method} not yet implemented for {type(self).__name__}\"\n3815 |                 )\n3816 | \n3817 |         if method is None:\n3818 |             if tolerance is not None:\n3819 |                 raise ValueError(\n3820 |                     \"tolerance argument only valid if doing pad, \"\n3821 |                     \"backfill or nearest reindexing\"\n3822 |                 )\n3823 |             if limit is not None:\n3824 |                 raise ValueError(\n3825 |                     \"limit argument only valid if doing pad, \"\n3826 |                     \"backfill or nearest reindexing\"\n3827 |                 )\n3828 | \n3829 |     def _convert_tolerance(self, tolerance, target: np.ndarray | Index) -> np.ndarray:\n3830 |         # override this method on subclasses\n3831 |         tolerance = np.asarray(tolerance)\n3832 |         if target.size != tolerance.size and tolerance.size > 1:\n3833 |             raise ValueError(\"list-like tolerance size must match target index size\")\n3834 |         elif is_numeric_dtype(self) and not np.issubdtype(tolerance.dtype, np.number):\n3835 |             if tolerance.ndim > 0:\n3836 |                 raise ValueError(\n3837 |                     f\"tolerance argument for {type(self).__name__} with dtype \"\n3838 |                     f\"{self.dtype} must contain numeric elements if it is list type\"\n3839 |                 )\n3840 | \n3841 |             raise ValueError(\n3842 |                 f\"tolerance argument for {type(self).__name__} with dtype {self.dtype} \"\n3843 |                 f\"must be numeric if it is a scalar: {tolerance!r}\"\n3844 |             )\n3845 |         return tolerance\n3846 | \n3847 |     @final\n3848 |     def _get_fill_indexer(\n3849 |         self, target: Index, method: str_t, limit: int | None = None, tolerance=None\n3850 |     ) -> npt.NDArray[np.intp]:\n3851 |         if self._is_multi:\n3852 |             if not (self.is_monotonic_increasing or self.is_monotonic_decreasing):\n3853 |                 raise ValueError(\"index must be monotonic increasing or decreasing\")\n3854 |             encoded = self.append(target)._engine.values  # type: ignore[union-attr]\n3855 |             self_encoded = Index(encoded[: len(self)])\n3856 |             target_encoded = Index(encoded[len(self) :])\n3857 |             return self_encoded._get_fill_indexer(\n3858 |                 target_encoded, method, limit, tolerance\n3859 |             )\n3860 | \n3861 |         if self.is_monotonic_increasing and target.is_monotonic_increasing:\n3862 |             target_values = target._get_engine_target()\n3863 |             own_values = self._get_engine_target()\n3864 |             if not isinstance(target_values, np.ndarray) or not isinstance(\n3865 |                 own_values, np.ndarray\n3866 |             ):\n3867 |                 raise NotImplementedError\n3868 | \n3869 |             if method == \"pad\":\n3870 |                 indexer = libalgos.pad(own_values, target_values, limit=limit)\n3871 |             else:\n3872 |                 # i.e. \"backfill\"\n3873 |                 indexer = libalgos.backfill(own_values, target_values, limit=limit)\n3874 |         else:\n3875 |             indexer = self._get_fill_indexer_searchsorted(target, method, limit)\n3876 |         if tolerance is not None and len(self):\n3877 |             indexer = self._filter_indexer_tolerance(target, indexer, tolerance)\n3878 |         return indexer\n3879 | \n3880 |     @final\n3881 |     def _get_fill_indexer_searchsorted(\n3882 |         self, target: Index, method: str_t, limit: int | None = None\n3883 |     ) -> npt.NDArray[np.intp]:\n3884 |         \"\"\"\n3885 |         Fallback pad/backfill get_indexer that works for monotonic decreasing\n3886 |         indexes and non-monotonic targets.\n3887 |         \"\"\"\n3888 |         if limit is not None:\n3889 |             raise ValueError(\n3890 |                 f\"limit argument for {method!r} method only well-defined \"\n3891 |                 \"if index and target are monotonic\"\n3892 |             )\n3893 | \n3894 |         side: Literal[\"left\", \"right\"] = \"left\" if method == \"pad\" else \"right\"\n3895 | \n3896 |         # find exact matches first (this simplifies the algorithm)\n3897 |         indexer = self.get_indexer(target)\n3898 |         nonexact = indexer == -1\n3899 |         indexer[nonexact] = self._searchsorted_monotonic(target[nonexact], side)\n3900 |         if side == \"left\":\n3901 |             # searchsorted returns \"indices into a sorted array such that,\n3902 |             # if the corresponding elements in v were inserted before the\n3903 |             # indices, the order of a would be preserved\".\n3904 |             # Thus, we need to subtract 1 to find values to the left.\n3905 |             indexer[nonexact] -= 1\n3906 |             # This also mapped not found values (values of 0 from\n3907 |             # np.searchsorted) to -1, which conveniently is also our\n3908 |             # sentinel for missing values\n3909 |         else:\n3910 |             # Mark indices to the right of the largest value as not found\n3911 |             indexer[indexer == len(self)] = -1\n3912 |         return indexer\n3913 | \n3914 |     @final\n3915 |     def _get_nearest_indexer(\n3916 |         self, target: Index, limit: int | None, tolerance\n3917 |     ) -> npt.NDArray[np.intp]:\n3918 |         \"\"\"\n3919 |         Get the indexer for the nearest index labels; requires an index with\n3920 |         values that can be subtracted from each other (e.g., not strings or\n3921 |         tuples).\n3922 |         \"\"\"\n3923 |         if not len(self):\n3924 |             return self._get_fill_indexer(target, \"pad\")\n3925 | \n3926 |         left_indexer = self.get_indexer(target, \"pad\", limit=limit)\n3927 |         right_indexer = self.get_indexer(target, \"backfill\", limit=limit)\n3928 | \n3929 |         left_distances = self._difference_compat(target, left_indexer)\n3930 |         right_distances = self._difference_compat(target, right_indexer)\n3931 | \n3932 |         op = operator.lt if self.is_monotonic_increasing else operator.le\n3933 |         indexer = np.where(\n3934 |             # error: Argument 1&2 has incompatible type \"Union[ExtensionArray,\n3935 |             # ndarray[Any, Any]]\"; expected \"Union[SupportsDunderLE,\n3936 |             # SupportsDunderGE, SupportsDunderGT, SupportsDunderLT]\"\n3937 |             op(left_distances, right_distances)  # type: ignore[arg-type]\n3938 |             | (right_indexer == -1),\n3939 |             left_indexer,\n3940 |             right_indexer,\n3941 |         )\n3942 |         if tolerance is not None:\n3943 |             indexer = self._filter_indexer_tolerance(target, indexer, tolerance)\n3944 |         return indexer\n3945 | \n3946 |     @final\n3947 |     def _filter_indexer_tolerance(\n3948 |         self,\n3949 |         target: Index,\n3950 |         indexer: npt.NDArray[np.intp],\n3951 |         tolerance,\n3952 |     ) -> npt.NDArray[np.intp]:\n3953 |         distance = self._difference_compat(target, indexer)\n3954 | \n3955 |         return np.where(distance <= tolerance, indexer, -1)\n3956 | \n3957 |     @final\n3958 |     def _difference_compat(\n3959 |         self, target: Index, indexer: npt.NDArray[np.intp]\n3960 |     ) -> ArrayLike:\n3961 |         # Compatibility for PeriodArray, for which __sub__ returns an ndarray[object]\n3962 |         #  of DateOffset objects, which do not support __abs__ (and would be slow\n3963 |         #  if they did)\n3964 | \n3965 |         if isinstance(self.dtype, PeriodDtype):\n3966 |             # Note: we only get here with matching dtypes\n3967 |             own_values = cast(\"PeriodArray\", self._data)._ndarray\n3968 |             target_values = cast(\"PeriodArray\", target._data)._ndarray\n3969 |             diff = own_values[indexer] - target_values\n3970 |         else:\n3971 |             # error: Unsupported left operand type for - (\"ExtensionArray\")\n3972 |             diff = self._values[indexer] - target._values  # type: ignore[operator]\n3973 |         return abs(diff)\n3974 | \n3975 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n3976 |     # Indexer Conversion Methods\n3977 | \n3978 |     @final\n3979 |     def _validate_positional_slice(self, key: slice) -> None:\n3980 |         \"\"\"\n3981 |         For positional indexing, a slice must have either int or None\n3982 |         for each of start, stop, and step.\n3983 |         \"\"\"\n3984 |         self._validate_indexer(\"positional\", key.start, \"iloc\")\n3985 |         self._validate_indexer(\"positional\", key.stop, \"iloc\")\n3986 |         self._validate_indexer(\"positional\", key.step, \"iloc\")\n3987 | \n3988 |     def _convert_slice_indexer(self, key: slice, kind: Literal[\"loc\", \"getitem\"]):\n3989 |         \"\"\"\n3990 |         Convert a slice indexer.\n3991 | \n3992 |         By definition, these are labels unless 'iloc' is passed in.\n3993 |         Floats are not allowed as the start, step, or stop of the slice.\n3994 | \n3995 |         Parameters\n3996 |         ----------\n3997 |         key : label of the slice bound\n3998 |         kind : {'loc', 'getitem'}\n3999 |         \"\"\"\n4000 | \n4001 |         # potentially cast the bounds to integers\n4002 |         start, stop, step = key.start, key.stop, key.step\n4003 | \n4004 |         # figure out if this is a positional indexer\n4005 |         is_index_slice = is_valid_positional_slice(key)\n4006 | \n4007 |         # TODO(GH#50617): once Series.__[gs]etitem__ is removed we should be able\n4008 |         #  to simplify this.\n4009 |         if kind == \"getitem\":\n4010 |             # called from the getitem slicers, validate that we are in fact integers\n4011 |             if is_index_slice:\n4012 |                 # In this case the _validate_indexer checks below are redundant\n4013 |                 return key\n4014 |             elif self.dtype.kind in \"iu\":\n4015 |                 # Note: these checks are redundant if we know is_index_slice\n4016 |                 self._validate_indexer(\"slice\", key.start, \"getitem\")\n4017 |                 self._validate_indexer(\"slice\", key.stop, \"getitem\")\n4018 |                 self._validate_indexer(\"slice\", key.step, \"getitem\")\n4019 |                 return key\n4020 | \n4021 |         # convert the slice to an indexer here; checking that the user didn't\n4022 |         #  pass a positional slice to loc\n4023 |         is_positional = is_index_slice and self._should_fallback_to_positional\n4024 | \n4025 |         # if we are mixed and have integers\n4026 |         if is_positional:\n4027 |             try:\n4028 |                 # Validate start & stop\n4029 |                 if start is not None:\n4030 |                     self.get_loc(start)\n4031 |                 if stop is not None:\n4032 |                     self.get_loc(stop)\n4033 |                 is_positional = False\n4034 |             except KeyError:\n4035 |                 pass\n4036 | \n4037 |         if com.is_null_slice(key):\n4038 |             # It doesn't matter if we are positional or label based\n4039 |             indexer = key\n4040 |         elif is_positional:\n4041 |             if kind == \"loc\":\n4042 |                 # GH#16121, GH#24612, GH#31810\n4043 |                 raise TypeError(\n4044 |                     \"Slicing a positional slice with .loc is not allowed, \"\n4045 |                     \"Use .loc with labels or .iloc with positions instead.\",\n4046 |                 )\n4047 |             indexer = key\n4048 |         else:\n4049 |             indexer = self.slice_indexer(start, stop, step)\n4050 | \n4051 |         return indexer\n4052 | \n4053 |     @final\n4054 |     def _raise_invalid_indexer(\n4055 |         self,\n4056 |         form: Literal[\"slice\", \"positional\"],\n4057 |         key,\n4058 |         reraise: lib.NoDefault | None | Exception = lib.no_default,\n4059 |     ) -> None:\n4060 |         \"\"\"\n4061 |         Raise consistent invalid indexer message.\n4062 |         \"\"\"\n4063 |         msg = (\n4064 |             f\"cannot do {form} indexing on {type(self).__name__} with these \"\n4065 |             f\"indexers [{key}] of type {type(key).__name__}\"\n4066 |         )\n4067 |         if reraise is not lib.no_default:\n4068 |             raise TypeError(msg) from reraise\n4069 |         raise TypeError(msg)\n4070 | \n4071 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n4072 |     # Reindex Methods\n4073 | \n4074 |     @final\n4075 |     def _validate_can_reindex(self, indexer: np.ndarray) -> None:\n4076 |         \"\"\"\n4077 |         Check if we are allowing reindexing with this particular indexer.\n4078 | \n4079 |         Parameters\n4080 |         ----------\n4081 |         indexer : an integer ndarray\n4082 | \n4083 |         Raises\n4084 |         ------\n4085 |         ValueError if its a duplicate axis\n4086 |         \"\"\"\n4087 |         # trying to reindex on an axis with duplicates\n4088 |         if not self._index_as_unique and len(indexer):\n4089 |             raise ValueError(\"cannot reindex on an axis with duplicate labels\")\n4090 | \n4091 |     def reindex(\n4092 |         self,\n4093 |         target,\n4094 |         method: ReindexMethod | None = None,\n4095 |         level=None,\n4096 |         limit: int | None = None,\n4097 |         tolerance: float | None = None,\n4098 |     ) -> tuple[Index, npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None]:\n4099 |         \"\"\"\n4100 |         Create index with target's values.\n4101 | \n4102 |         Parameters\n4103 |         ----------\n4104 |         target : an iterable\n4105 |             An iterable containing the values to be used for creating the new index.\n4106 |         method : {None, 'pad'/'ffill', 'backfill'/'bfill', 'nearest'}, optional\n4107 |             * default: exact matches only.\n4108 |             * pad / ffill: find the PREVIOUS index value if no exact match.\n4109 |             * backfill / bfill: use NEXT index value if no exact match\n4110 |             * nearest: use the NEAREST index value if no exact match. Tied\n4111 |               distances are broken by preferring the larger index value.\n4112 |         level : int, optional\n4113 |             Level of multiindex.\n4114 |         limit : int, optional\n4115 |             Maximum number of consecutive labels in ``target`` to match for\n4116 |             inexact matches.\n4117 |         tolerance : int or float, optional\n4118 |             Maximum distance between original and new labels for inexact\n4119 |             matches. The values of the index at the matching locations must\n4120 |             satisfy the equation ``abs(index[indexer] - target) <= tolerance``.\n4121 | \n4122 |             Tolerance may be a scalar value, which applies the same tolerance\n4123 |             to all values, or list-like, which applies variable tolerance per\n4124 |             element. List-like includes list, tuple, array, Series, and must be\n4125 |             the same size as the index and its dtype must exactly match the\n4126 |             index's type.\n4127 | \n4128 |         Returns\n4129 |         -------\n4130 |         new_index : pd.Index\n4131 |             Resulting index.\n4132 |         indexer : np.ndarray[np.intp] or None\n4133 |             Indices of output values in original index.\n4134 | \n4135 |         Raises\n4136 |         ------\n4137 |         TypeError\n4138 |             If ``method`` passed along with ``level``.\n4139 |         ValueError\n4140 |             If non-unique multi-index\n4141 |         ValueError\n4142 |             If non-unique index and ``method`` or ``limit`` passed.\n4143 | \n4144 |         See Also\n4145 |         --------\n4146 |         Series.reindex : Conform Series to new index with optional filling logic.\n4147 |         DataFrame.reindex : Conform DataFrame to new index with optional filling logic.\n4148 | \n4149 |         Examples\n4150 |         --------\n4151 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"car\", \"bike\", \"train\", \"tractor\"])\n4152 |         >>> idx\n4153 |         Index(['car', 'bike', 'train', 'tractor'], dtype='object')\n4154 |         >>> idx.reindex([\"car\", \"bike\"])\n4155 |         (Index(['car', 'bike'], dtype='object'), array([0, 1]))\n4156 |         \"\"\"\n4157 |         # GH6552: preserve names when reindexing to non-named target\n4158 |         # (i.e. neither Index nor Series).\n4159 |         preserve_names = not hasattr(target, \"name\")\n4160 | \n4161 |         # GH7774: preserve dtype/tz if target is empty and not an Index.\n4162 |         if is_iterator(target):\n4163 |             target = list(target)\n4164 | \n4165 |         if not isinstance(target, Index) and len(target) == 0:\n4166 |             if level is not None and self._is_multi:\n4167 |                 # \"Index\" has no attribute \"levels\"; maybe \"nlevels\"?\n4168 |                 idx = self.levels[level]  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n4169 |             else:\n4170 |                 idx = self\n4171 |             target = idx[:0]\n4172 |         else:\n4173 |             target = ensure_index(target)\n4174 | \n4175 |         if level is not None and (\n4176 |             isinstance(self, ABCMultiIndex) or isinstance(target, ABCMultiIndex)\n4177 |         ):\n4178 |             if method is not None:\n4179 |                 raise TypeError(\"Fill method not supported if level passed\")\n4180 | \n4181 |             # TODO: tests where passing `keep_order=not self._is_multi`\n4182 |             #  makes a difference for non-MultiIndex case\n4183 |             target, indexer, _ = self._join_level(\n4184 |                 target, level, how=\"right\", keep_order=not self._is_multi\n4185 |             )\n4186 | \n4187 |         else:\n4188 |             if self.equals(target):\n4189 |                 indexer = None\n4190 |             else:\n4191 |                 if self._index_as_unique:\n4192 |                     indexer = self.get_indexer(\n4193 |                         target, method=method, limit=limit, tolerance=tolerance\n4194 |                     )\n4195 |                 elif self._is_multi:\n4196 |                     raise ValueError(\"cannot handle a non-unique multi-index!\")\n4197 |                 elif not self.is_unique:\n4198 |                     # GH#42568\n4199 |                     raise ValueError(\"cannot reindex on an axis with duplicate labels\")\n4200 |                 else:\n4201 |                     indexer, _ = self.get_indexer_non_unique(target)\n4202 | \n4203 |         target = self._wrap_reindex_result(target, indexer, preserve_names)\n4204 |         return target, indexer\n4205 | \n4206 |     def _wrap_reindex_result(self, target, indexer, preserve_names: bool):\n4207 |         target = self._maybe_preserve_names(target, preserve_names)\n4208 |         return target\n4209 | \n4210 |     def _maybe_preserve_names(self, target: IndexT, preserve_names: bool) -> IndexT:\n4211 |         if preserve_names and target.nlevels == 1 and target.name != self.name:\n4212 |             target = target.copy(deep=False)\n4213 |             target.name = self.name\n4214 |         return target\n4215 | \n4216 |     @final\n4217 |     def _reindex_non_unique(\n4218 |         self, target: Index\n4219 |     ) -> tuple[Index, npt.NDArray[np.intp], npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None]:\n4220 |         \"\"\"\n4221 |         Create a new index with target's values (move/add/delete values as\n4222 |         necessary) use with non-unique Index and a possibly non-unique target.\n4223 | \n4224 |         Parameters\n4225 |         ----------\n4226 |         target : an iterable\n4227 | \n4228 |         Returns\n4229 |         -------\n4230 |         new_index : pd.Index\n4231 |             Resulting index.\n4232 |         indexer : np.ndarray[np.intp]\n4233 |             Indices of output values in original index.\n4234 |         new_indexer : np.ndarray[np.intp] or None\n4235 | \n4236 |         \"\"\"\n4237 |         target = ensure_index(target)\n4238 |         if len(target) == 0:\n4239 |             # GH#13691\n4240 |             return self[:0], np.array([], dtype=np.intp), None\n4241 | \n4242 |         indexer, missing = self.get_indexer_non_unique(target)\n4243 |         check = indexer != -1\n4244 |         new_labels: Index | np.ndarray = self.take(indexer[check])\n4245 |         new_indexer = None\n4246 | \n4247 |         if len(missing):\n4248 |             length = np.arange(len(indexer), dtype=np.intp)\n4249 | \n4250 |             missing = ensure_platform_int(missing)\n4251 |             missing_labels = target.take(missing)\n4252 |             missing_indexer = length[~check]\n4253 |             cur_labels = self.take(indexer[check]).values\n4254 |             cur_indexer = length[check]\n4255 | \n4256 |             # Index constructor below will do inference\n4257 |             new_labels = np.empty((len(indexer),), dtype=object)\n4258 |             new_labels[cur_indexer] = cur_labels\n4259 |             new_labels[missing_indexer] = missing_labels\n4260 | \n4261 |             # GH#38906\n4262 |             if not len(self):\n4263 |                 new_indexer = np.arange(0, dtype=np.intp)\n4264 | \n4265 |             # a unique indexer\n4266 |             elif target.is_unique:\n4267 |                 # see GH5553, make sure we use the right indexer\n4268 |                 new_indexer = np.arange(len(indexer), dtype=np.intp)\n4269 |                 new_indexer[cur_indexer] = np.arange(len(cur_labels))\n4270 |                 new_indexer[missing_indexer] = -1\n4271 | \n4272 |             # we have a non_unique selector, need to use the original\n4273 |             # indexer here\n4274 |             else:\n4275 |                 # need to retake to have the same size as the indexer\n4276 |                 indexer[~check] = -1\n4277 | \n4278 |                 # reset the new indexer to account for the new size\n4279 |                 new_indexer = np.arange(len(self.take(indexer)), dtype=np.intp)\n4280 |                 new_indexer[~check] = -1\n4281 | \n4282 |         if not isinstance(self, ABCMultiIndex):\n4283 |             new_index = Index(new_labels, name=self.name)\n4284 |         else:\n4285 |             new_index = type(self).from_tuples(new_labels, names=self.names)\n4286 |         return new_index, indexer, new_indexer\n4287 | \n4288 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n4289 |     # Join Methods\n4290 | \n4291 |     @overload\n4292 |     def join(\n4293 |         self,\n4294 |         other: Index,\n4295 |         *,\n4296 |         how: JoinHow = ...,\n4297 |         level: Level = ...,\n4298 |         return_indexers: Literal[True],\n4299 |         sort: bool = ...,\n4300 |     ) -> tuple[Index, npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None, npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None]: ...\n4301 | \n4302 |     @overload\n4303 |     def join(\n4304 |         self,\n4305 |         other: Index,\n4306 |         *,\n4307 |         how: JoinHow = ...,\n4308 |         level: Level = ...,\n4309 |         return_indexers: Literal[False] = ...,\n4310 |         sort: bool = ...,\n4311 |     ) -> Index: ...\n4312 | \n4313 |     @overload\n4314 |     def join(\n4315 |         self,\n4316 |         other: Index,\n4317 |         *,\n4318 |         how: JoinHow = ...,\n4319 |         level: Level = ...,\n4320 |         return_indexers: bool = ...,\n4321 |         sort: bool = ...,\n4322 |     ) -> (\n4323 |         Index | tuple[Index, npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None, npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None]\n4324 |     ): ...\n4325 | \n4326 |     @final\n4327 |     @_maybe_return_indexers\n4328 |     def join(\n4329 |         self,\n4330 |         other: Index,\n4331 |         *,\n4332 |         how: JoinHow = \"left\",\n4333 |         level: Level | None = None,\n4334 |         return_indexers: bool = False,\n4335 |         sort: bool = False,\n4336 |     ) -> Index | tuple[Index, npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None, npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None]:\n4337 |         \"\"\"\n4338 |         Compute join_index and indexers to conform data structures to the new index.\n4339 | \n4340 |         Parameters\n4341 |         ----------\n4342 |         other : Index\n4343 |             The other index on which join is performed.\n4344 |         how : {'left', 'right', 'inner', 'outer'}\n4345 |         level : int or level name, default None\n4346 |             It is either the integer position or the name of the level.\n4347 |         return_indexers : bool, default False\n4348 |             Whether to return the indexers or not for both the index objects.\n4349 |         sort : bool, default False\n4350 |             Sort the join keys lexicographically in the result Index. If False,\n4351 |             the order of the join keys depends on the join type (how keyword).\n4352 | \n4353 |         Returns\n4354 |         -------\n4355 |         join_index, (left_indexer, right_indexer)\n4356 |             The new index.\n4357 | \n4358 |         See Also\n4359 |         --------\n4360 |         DataFrame.join : Join columns with `other` DataFrame either on index\n4361 |             or on a key.\n4362 |         DataFrame.merge : Merge DataFrame or named Series objects with a\n4363 |             database-style join.\n4364 | \n4365 |         Examples\n4366 |         --------\n4367 |         >>> idx1 = pd.Index([1, 2, 3])\n4368 |         >>> idx2 = pd.Index([4, 5, 6])\n4369 |         >>> idx1.join(idx2, how=\"outer\")\n4370 |         Index([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], dtype='int64')\n4371 |         >>> idx1.join(other=idx2, how=\"outer\", return_indexers=True)\n4372 |         (Index([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], dtype='int64'),\n4373 |         array([ 0,  1,  2, -1, -1, -1]), array([-1, -1, -1,  0,  1,  2]))\n4374 |         \"\"\"\n4375 |         other = ensure_index(other)\n4376 |         sort = sort or how == \"outer\"\n4377 | \n4378 |         if isinstance(self, ABCDatetimeIndex) and isinstance(other, ABCDatetimeIndex):\n4379 |             if (self.tz is None) ^ (other.tz is None):\n4380 |                 # Raise instead of casting to object below.\n4381 |                 raise TypeError(\"Cannot join tz-naive with tz-aware DatetimeIndex\")\n4382 | \n4383 |         if not self._is_multi and not other._is_multi:\n4384 |             # We have specific handling for MultiIndex below\n4385 |             pself, pother = self._maybe_downcast_for_indexing(other)\n4386 |             if pself is not self or pother is not other:\n4387 |                 return pself.join(\n4388 |                     pother, how=how, level=level, return_indexers=True, sort=sort\n4389 |                 )\n4390 | \n4391 |         # try to figure out the join level\n4392 |         # GH3662\n4393 |         if level is None and (self._is_multi or other._is_multi):\n4394 |             # have the same levels/names so a simple join\n4395 |             if self.names == other.names:\n4396 |                 pass\n4397 |             else:\n4398 |                 return self._join_multi(other, how=how)\n4399 | \n4400 |         # join on the level\n4401 |         if level is not None and (self._is_multi or other._is_multi):\n4402 |             return self._join_level(other, level, how=how)\n4403 | \n4404 |         if len(self) == 0 or len(other) == 0:\n4405 |             try:\n4406 |                 return self._join_empty(other, how, sort)\n4407 |             except TypeError:\n4408 |                 # object dtype; non-comparable objects\n4409 |                 pass\n4410 | \n4411 |         if self.dtype != other.dtype:\n4412 |             dtype = self._find_common_type_compat(other)\n4413 |             this = self.astype(dtype, copy=False)\n4414 |             other = other.astype(dtype, copy=False)\n4415 |             return this.join(other, how=how, return_indexers=True)\n4416 |         elif (\n4417 |             isinstance(self, ABCCategoricalIndex)\n4418 |             and isinstance(other, ABCCategoricalIndex)\n4419 |             and not self.ordered\n4420 |             and not self.categories.equals(other.categories)\n4421 |         ):\n4422 |             # dtypes are \"equal\" but categories are in different order\n4423 |             other = Index(other._values.reorder_categories(self.categories))\n4424 | \n4425 |         _validate_join_method(how)\n4426 | \n4427 |         if (\n4428 |             self.is_monotonic_increasing\n4429 |             and other.is_monotonic_increasing\n4430 |             and self._can_use_libjoin\n4431 |             and other._can_use_libjoin\n4432 |             and (self.is_unique or other.is_unique)\n4433 |         ):\n4434 |             try:\n4435 |                 return self._join_monotonic(other, how=how)\n4436 |             except TypeError:\n4437 |                 # object dtype; non-comparable objects\n4438 |                 pass\n4439 |         elif not self.is_unique or not other.is_unique:\n4440 |             return self._join_non_unique(other, how=how, sort=sort)\n4441 | \n4442 |         return self._join_via_get_indexer(other, how, sort)\n4443 | \n4444 |     def _join_empty(\n4445 |         self, other: Index, how: JoinHow, sort: bool\n4446 |     ) -> tuple[Index, npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None, npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None]:\n4447 |         assert len(self) == 0 or len(other) == 0\n4448 |         _validate_join_method(how)\n4449 | \n4450 |         lidx: np.ndarray | None\n4451 |         ridx: np.ndarray | None\n4452 | \n4453 |         if len(other):\n4454 |             how = cast(JoinHow, {\"left\": \"right\", \"right\": \"left\"}.get(how, how))\n4455 |             join_index, ridx, lidx = other._join_empty(self, how, sort)\n4456 |         elif how in [\"left\", \"outer\"]:\n4457 |             if sort and not self.is_monotonic_increasing:\n4458 |                 lidx = self.argsort()\n4459 |                 join_index = self.take(lidx)\n4460 |             else:\n4461 |                 lidx = None\n4462 |                 join_index = self._view()\n4463 |             ridx = np.broadcast_to(np.intp(-1), len(join_index))\n4464 |         else:\n4465 |             join_index = other._view()\n4466 |             lidx = np.array([], dtype=np.intp)\n4467 |             ridx = None\n4468 |         return join_index, lidx, ridx\n4469 | \n4470 |     @final\n4471 |     def _join_via_get_indexer(\n4472 |         self, other: Index, how: JoinHow, sort: bool\n4473 |     ) -> tuple[Index, npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None, npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None]:\n4474 |         # Fallback if we do not have any fastpaths available based on\n4475 |         #  uniqueness/monotonicity\n4476 | \n4477 |         # Note: at this point we have checked matching dtypes\n4478 |         lindexer: npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None\n4479 |         rindexer: npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None\n4480 | \n4481 |         if how == \"left\":\n4482 |             if sort:\n4483 |                 join_index, lindexer = self.sort_values(return_indexer=True)\n4484 |                 rindexer = other.get_indexer_for(join_index)\n4485 |                 return join_index, lindexer, rindexer\n4486 |             else:\n4487 |                 join_index = self\n4488 |         elif how == \"right\":\n4489 |             if sort:\n4490 |                 join_index, rindexer = other.sort_values(return_indexer=True)\n4491 |                 lindexer = self.get_indexer_for(join_index)\n4492 |                 return join_index, lindexer, rindexer\n4493 |             else:\n4494 |                 join_index = other\n4495 |         elif how == \"inner\":\n4496 |             join_index = self.intersection(other, sort=sort)\n4497 |         elif how == \"outer\":\n4498 |             try:\n4499 |                 join_index = self.union(other, sort=sort)\n4500 |             except TypeError:\n4501 |                 join_index = self.union(other)\n4502 |                 try:\n4503 |                     join_index = _maybe_try_sort(join_index, sort)\n4504 |                 except TypeError:\n4505 |                     pass\n4506 | \n4507 |         names = other.names if how == \"right\" else self.names\n4508 |         if join_index.names != names:\n4509 |             join_index = join_index.set_names(names)\n4510 | \n4511 |         if join_index is self:\n4512 |             lindexer = None\n4513 |         else:\n4514 |             lindexer = self.get_indexer_for(join_index)\n4515 |         if join_index is other:\n4516 |             rindexer = None\n4517 |         else:\n4518 |             rindexer = other.get_indexer_for(join_index)\n4519 |         return join_index, lindexer, rindexer\n4520 | \n4521 |     @final\n4522 |     def _join_multi(self, other: Index, how: JoinHow):\n4523 |         from pandas.core.indexes.multi import MultiIndex\n4524 |         from pandas.core.reshape.merge import restore_dropped_levels_multijoin\n4525 | \n4526 |         # figure out join names\n4527 |         self_names_list = list(self.names)\n4528 |         other_names_list = list(other.names)\n4529 |         self_names_order = self_names_list.index\n4530 |         other_names_order = other_names_list.index\n4531 |         self_names = set(self_names_list)\n4532 |         other_names = set(other_names_list)\n4533 |         overlap = self_names & other_names\n4534 | \n4535 |         # need at least 1 in common\n4536 |         if not overlap:\n4537 |             raise ValueError(\"cannot join with no overlapping index names\")\n4538 | \n4539 |         if isinstance(self, MultiIndex) and isinstance(other, MultiIndex):\n4540 |             # Drop the non-matching levels from left and right respectively\n4541 |             ldrop_names = sorted(self_names - overlap, key=self_names_order)\n4542 |             rdrop_names = sorted(other_names - overlap, key=other_names_order)\n4543 | \n4544 |             # if only the order differs\n4545 |             if not len(ldrop_names + rdrop_names):\n4546 |                 self_jnlevels = self\n4547 |                 other_jnlevels = other.reorder_levels(self.names)\n4548 |             else:\n4549 |                 self_jnlevels = self.droplevel(ldrop_names)\n4550 |                 other_jnlevels = other.droplevel(rdrop_names)\n4551 | \n4552 |             # Join left and right\n4553 |             # Join on same leveled multi-index frames is supported\n4554 |             join_idx, lidx, ridx = self_jnlevels.join(\n4555 |                 other_jnlevels, how=how, return_indexers=True\n4556 |             )\n4557 | \n4558 |             # Restore the dropped levels\n4559 |             # Returned index level order is\n4560 |             # common levels, ldrop_names, rdrop_names\n4561 |             dropped_names = ldrop_names + rdrop_names\n4562 | \n4563 |             # error: Argument 5/6 to \"restore_dropped_levels_multijoin\" has\n4564 |             # incompatible type \"Optional[ndarray[Any, dtype[signedinteger[Any\n4565 |             # ]]]]\"; expected \"ndarray[Any, dtype[signedinteger[Any]]]\"\n4566 |             levels, codes, names = restore_dropped_levels_multijoin(\n4567 |                 self,\n4568 |                 other,\n4569 |                 dropped_names,\n4570 |                 join_idx,\n4571 |                 lidx,  # type: ignore[arg-type]\n4572 |                 ridx,  # type: ignore[arg-type]\n4573 |             )\n4574 | \n4575 |             # Re-create the multi-index\n4576 |             multi_join_idx = MultiIndex(\n4577 |                 levels=levels, codes=codes, names=names, verify_integrity=False\n4578 |             )\n4579 | \n4580 |             multi_join_idx = multi_join_idx.remove_unused_levels()\n4581 | \n4582 |             # maintain the order of the index levels\n4583 |             if how == \"right\":\n4584 |                 level_order = other_names_list + ldrop_names\n4585 |             else:\n4586 |                 level_order = self_names_list + rdrop_names\n4587 |             multi_join_idx = multi_join_idx.reorder_levels(level_order)\n4588 | \n4589 |             return multi_join_idx, lidx, ridx\n4590 | \n4591 |         jl = next(iter(overlap))\n4592 | \n4593 |         # Case where only one index is multi\n4594 |         # make the indices into mi's that match\n4595 |         flip_order = False\n4596 |         if isinstance(self, MultiIndex):\n4597 |             self, other = other, self\n4598 |             flip_order = True\n4599 |             # flip if join method is right or left\n4600 |             flip: dict[JoinHow, JoinHow] = {\"right\": \"left\", \"left\": \"right\"}\n4601 |             how = flip.get(how, how)\n4602 | \n4603 |         level = other.names.index(jl)\n4604 |         result = self._join_level(other, level, how=how)\n4605 | \n4606 |         if flip_order:\n4607 |             return result[0], result[2], result[1]\n4608 |         return result\n4609 | \n4610 |     @final\n4611 |     def _join_non_unique(\n4612 |         self, other: Index, how: JoinHow = \"left\", sort: bool = False\n4613 |     ) -> tuple[Index, npt.NDArray[np.intp], npt.NDArray[np.intp]]:\n4614 |         from pandas.core.reshape.merge import get_join_indexers_non_unique\n4615 | \n4616 |         # We only get here if dtypes match\n4617 |         assert self.dtype == other.dtype\n4618 | \n4619 |         left_idx, right_idx = get_join_indexers_non_unique(\n4620 |             self._values, other._values, how=how, sort=sort\n4621 |         )\n4622 | \n4623 |         if how == \"right\":\n4624 |             join_index = other.take(right_idx)\n4625 |         else:\n4626 |             join_index = self.take(left_idx)\n4627 | \n4628 |         if how == \"outer\":\n4629 |             mask = left_idx == -1\n4630 |             if mask.any():\n4631 |                 right = other.take(right_idx)\n4632 |                 join_index = join_index.putmask(mask, right)\n4633 | \n4634 |         if isinstance(join_index, ABCMultiIndex) and how == \"outer\":\n4635 |             # test_join_index_levels\n4636 |             join_index = join_index._sort_levels_monotonic()\n4637 |         return join_index, left_idx, right_idx\n4638 | \n4639 |     @final\n4640 |     def _join_level(\n4641 |         self, other: Index, level, how: JoinHow = \"left\", keep_order: bool = True\n4642 |     ) -> tuple[MultiIndex, npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None, npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None]:\n4643 |         \"\"\"\n4644 |         The join method *only* affects the level of the resulting\n4645 |         MultiIndex. Otherwise it just exactly aligns the Index data to the\n4646 |         labels of the level in the MultiIndex.\n4647 | \n4648 |         If ```keep_order == True```, the order of the data indexed by the\n4649 |         MultiIndex will not be changed; otherwise, it will tie out\n4650 |         with `other`.\n4651 |         \"\"\"\n4652 |         from pandas.core.indexes.multi import MultiIndex\n4653 | \n4654 |         def _get_leaf_sorter(labels: list[np.ndarray]) -> npt.NDArray[np.intp]:\n4655 |             \"\"\"\n4656 |             Returns sorter for the inner most level while preserving the\n4657 |             order of higher levels.\n4658 | \n4659 |             Parameters\n4660 |             ----------\n4661 |             labels : list[np.ndarray]\n4662 |                 Each ndarray has signed integer dtype, not necessarily identical.\n4663 | \n4664 |             Returns\n4665 |             -------\n4666 |             np.ndarray[np.intp]\n4667 |             \"\"\"\n4668 |             if labels[0].size == 0:\n4669 |                 return np.empty(0, dtype=np.intp)\n4670 | \n4671 |             if len(labels) == 1:\n4672 |                 return get_group_index_sorter(ensure_platform_int(labels[0]))\n4673 | \n4674 |             # find indexers of beginning of each set of\n4675 |             # same-key labels w.r.t all but last level\n4676 |             tic = labels[0][:-1] != labels[0][1:]\n4677 |             for lab in labels[1:-1]:\n4678 |                 tic |= lab[:-1] != lab[1:]\n4679 | \n4680 |             starts = np.hstack(([True], tic, [True])).nonzero()[0]\n4681 |             lab = ensure_int64(labels[-1])\n4682 |             return lib.get_level_sorter(lab, ensure_platform_int(starts))\n4683 | \n4684 |         if isinstance(self, MultiIndex) and isinstance(other, MultiIndex):\n4685 |             raise TypeError(\"Join on level between two MultiIndex objects is ambiguous\")\n4686 | \n4687 |         left, right = self, other\n4688 | \n4689 |         flip_order = not isinstance(self, MultiIndex)\n4690 |         if flip_order:\n4691 |             left, right = right, left\n4692 |             flip: dict[JoinHow, JoinHow] = {\"right\": \"left\", \"left\": \"right\"}\n4693 |             how = flip.get(how, how)\n4694 | \n4695 |         assert isinstance(left, MultiIndex)\n4696 | \n4697 |         level = left._get_level_number(level)\n4698 |         old_level = left.levels[level]\n4699 | \n4700 |         if not right.is_unique:\n4701 |             raise NotImplementedError(\n4702 |                 \"Index._join_level on non-unique index is not implemented\"\n4703 |             )\n4704 | \n4705 |         new_level, left_lev_indexer, right_lev_indexer = old_level.join(\n4706 |             right, how=how, return_indexers=True\n4707 |         )\n4708 | \n4709 |         if left_lev_indexer is None:\n4710 |             if keep_order or len(left) == 0:\n4711 |                 left_indexer = None\n4712 |                 join_index = left\n4713 |             else:  # sort the leaves\n4714 |                 left_indexer = _get_leaf_sorter(left.codes[: level + 1])\n4715 |                 join_index = left[left_indexer]\n4716 | \n4717 |         else:\n4718 |             left_lev_indexer = ensure_platform_int(left_lev_indexer)\n4719 |             rev_indexer = lib.get_reverse_indexer(left_lev_indexer, len(old_level))\n4720 |             old_codes = left.codes[level]\n4721 | \n4722 |             taker = old_codes[old_codes != -1]\n4723 |             new_lev_codes = rev_indexer.take(taker)\n4724 | \n4725 |             new_codes = list(left.codes)\n4726 |             new_codes[level] = new_lev_codes\n4727 | \n4728 |             new_levels = list(left.levels)\n4729 |             new_levels[level] = new_level\n4730 | \n4731 |             if keep_order:  # just drop missing values. o.w. keep order\n4732 |                 left_indexer = np.arange(len(left), dtype=np.intp)\n4733 |                 left_indexer = cast(np.ndarray, left_indexer)\n4734 |                 mask = new_lev_codes != -1\n4735 |                 if not mask.all():\n4736 |                     new_codes = [lab[mask] for lab in new_codes]\n4737 |                     left_indexer = left_indexer[mask]\n4738 | \n4739 |             else:  # tie out the order with other\n4740 |                 if level == 0:  # outer most level, take the fast route\n4741 |                     max_new_lev = 0 if len(new_lev_codes) == 0 else new_lev_codes.max()\n4742 |                     ngroups = 1 + max_new_lev\n4743 |                     left_indexer, counts = libalgos.groupsort_indexer(\n4744 |                         new_lev_codes, ngroups\n4745 |                     )\n4746 | \n4747 |                     # missing values are placed first; drop them!\n4748 |                     left_indexer = left_indexer[counts[0] :]\n4749 |                     new_codes = [lab[left_indexer] for lab in new_codes]\n4750 | \n4751 |                 else:  # sort the leaves\n4752 |                     mask = new_lev_codes != -1\n4753 |                     mask_all = mask.all()\n4754 |                     if not mask_all:\n4755 |                         new_codes = [lab[mask] for lab in new_codes]\n4756 | \n4757 |                     left_indexer = _get_leaf_sorter(new_codes[: level + 1])\n4758 |                     new_codes = [lab[left_indexer] for lab in new_codes]\n4759 | \n4760 |                     # left_indexers are w.r.t masked frame.\n4761 |                     # reverse to original frame!\n4762 |                     if not mask_all:\n4763 |                         left_indexer = mask.nonzero()[0][left_indexer]\n4764 | \n4765 |             join_index = MultiIndex(\n4766 |                 levels=new_levels,\n4767 |                 codes=new_codes,\n4768 |                 names=left.names,\n4769 |                 verify_integrity=False,\n4770 |             )\n4771 | \n4772 |         if right_lev_indexer is not None:\n4773 |             right_indexer = right_lev_indexer.take(join_index.codes[level])\n4774 |         else:\n4775 |             right_indexer = join_index.codes[level]\n4776 | \n4777 |         if flip_order:\n4778 |             left_indexer, right_indexer = right_indexer, left_indexer\n4779 | \n4780 |         left_indexer = (\n4781 |             None if left_indexer is None else ensure_platform_int(left_indexer)\n4782 |         )\n4783 |         right_indexer = (\n4784 |             None if right_indexer is None else ensure_platform_int(right_indexer)\n4785 |         )\n4786 |         return join_index, left_indexer, right_indexer\n4787 | \n4788 |     def _join_monotonic(\n4789 |         self, other: Index, how: JoinHow = \"left\"\n4790 |     ) -> tuple[Index, npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None, npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None]:\n4791 |         # We only get here with (caller is responsible for ensuring):\n4792 |         #  1) matching dtypes\n4793 |         #  2) both monotonic increasing\n4794 |         #  3) other.is_unique or self.is_unique\n4795 |         assert other.dtype == self.dtype\n4796 |         assert self._can_use_libjoin and other._can_use_libjoin\n4797 | \n4798 |         if self.equals(other):\n4799 |             # This is a convenient place for this check, but its correctness\n4800 |             #  does not depend on monotonicity, so it could go earlier\n4801 |             #  in the calling method.\n4802 |             ret_index = other if how == \"right\" else self\n4803 |             return ret_index, None, None\n4804 | \n4805 |         ridx: npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None\n4806 |         lidx: npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None\n4807 | \n4808 |         if how == \"left\":\n4809 |             if other.is_unique:\n4810 |                 # We can perform much better than the general case\n4811 |                 join_index = self\n4812 |                 lidx = None\n4813 |                 ridx = self._left_indexer_unique(other)\n4814 |             else:\n4815 |                 join_array, lidx, ridx = self._left_indexer(other)\n4816 |                 join_index, lidx, ridx = self._wrap_join_result(\n4817 |                     join_array, other, lidx, ridx, how\n4818 |                 )\n4819 |         elif how == \"right\":\n4820 |             if self.is_unique:\n4821 |                 # We can perform much better than the general case\n4822 |                 join_index = other\n4823 |                 lidx = other._left_indexer_unique(self)\n4824 |                 ridx = None\n4825 |             else:\n4826 |                 join_array, ridx, lidx = other._left_indexer(self)\n4827 |                 join_index, lidx, ridx = self._wrap_join_result(\n4828 |                     join_array, other, lidx, ridx, how\n4829 |                 )\n4830 |         elif how == \"inner\":\n4831 |             join_array, lidx, ridx = self._inner_indexer(other)\n4832 |             join_index, lidx, ridx = self._wrap_join_result(\n4833 |                 join_array, other, lidx, ridx, how\n4834 |             )\n4835 |         elif how == \"outer\":\n4836 |             join_array, lidx, ridx = self._outer_indexer(other)\n4837 |             join_index, lidx, ridx = self._wrap_join_result(\n4838 |                 join_array, other, lidx, ridx, how\n4839 |             )\n4840 | \n4841 |         lidx = None if lidx is None else ensure_platform_int(lidx)\n4842 |         ridx = None if ridx is None else ensure_platform_int(ridx)\n4843 |         return join_index, lidx, ridx\n4844 | \n4845 |     def _wrap_join_result(\n4846 |         self,\n4847 |         joined: ArrayLike,\n4848 |         other: Self,\n4849 |         lidx: npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None,\n4850 |         ridx: npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None,\n4851 |         how: JoinHow,\n4852 |     ) -> tuple[Self, npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None, npt.NDArray[np.intp] | None]:\n4853 |         assert other.dtype == self.dtype\n4854 | \n4855 |         if lidx is not None and lib.is_range_indexer(lidx, len(self)):\n4856 |             lidx = None\n4857 |         if ridx is not None and lib.is_range_indexer(ridx, len(other)):\n4858 |             ridx = None\n4859 | \n4860 |         # return self or other if possible to maintain cached attributes\n4861 |         if lidx is None:\n4862 |             join_index = self\n4863 |         elif ridx is None:\n4864 |             join_index = other\n4865 |         else:\n4866 |             join_index = self._constructor._with_infer(joined, dtype=self.dtype)\n4867 | \n4868 |         names = other.names if how == \"right\" else self.names\n4869 |         if join_index.names != names:\n4870 |             join_index = join_index.set_names(names)\n4871 | \n4872 |         return join_index, lidx, ridx\n4873 | \n4874 |     @final\n4875 |     @cache_readonly\n4876 |     def _can_use_libjoin(self) -> bool:\n4877 |         \"\"\"\n4878 |         Whether we can use the fastpaths implemented in _libs.join.\n4879 | \n4880 |         This is driven by whether (in monotonic increasing cases that are\n4881 |         guaranteed not to have NAs) we can convert to a np.ndarray without\n4882 |         making a copy. If we cannot, this negates the performance benefit\n4883 |         of using libjoin.\n4884 |         \"\"\"\n4885 |         if not self.is_monotonic_increasing:\n4886 |             # The libjoin functions all assume monotonicity.\n4887 |             return False\n4888 | \n4889 |         if type(self) is Index:\n4890 |             # excludes EAs, but include masks, we get here with monotonic\n4891 |             # values only, meaning no NA\n4892 |             return (\n4893 |                 isinstance(self.dtype, np.dtype)\n4894 |                 or isinstance(self._values, (ArrowExtensionArray, BaseMaskedArray))\n4895 |                 or (\n4896 |                     isinstance(self.dtype, StringDtype)\n4897 |                     and self.dtype.storage == \"python\"\n4898 |                 )\n4899 |             )\n4900 |         # Exclude index types where the conversion to numpy converts to object dtype,\n4901 |         #  which negates the performance benefit of libjoin\n4902 |         # Subclasses should override to return False if _get_join_target is\n4903 |         #  not zero-copy.\n4904 |         # TODO: exclude RangeIndex (which allocates memory)?\n4905 |         #  Doing so seems to break test_concat_datetime_timezone\n4906 |         return not isinstance(self, (ABCIntervalIndex, ABCMultiIndex))\n4907 | \n4908 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n4909 |     # Uncategorized Methods\n4910 | \n4911 |     @property\n4912 |     def values(self) -> ArrayLike:\n4913 |         \"\"\"\n4914 |         Return an array representing the data in the Index.\n4915 | \n4916 |         .. warning::\n4917 | \n4918 |            We recommend using :attr:`Index.array` or\n4919 |            :meth:`Index.to_numpy`, depending on whether you need\n4920 |            a reference to the underlying data or a NumPy array.\n4921 | \n4922 |         Returns\n4923 |         -------\n4924 |         array: numpy.ndarray or ExtensionArray\n4925 | \n4926 |         See Also\n4927 |         --------\n4928 |         Index.array : Reference to the underlying data.\n4929 |         Index.to_numpy : A NumPy array representing the underlying data.\n4930 | \n4931 |         Examples\n4932 |         --------\n4933 |         For :class:`pandas.Index`:\n4934 | \n4935 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3])\n4936 |         >>> idx\n4937 |         Index([1, 2, 3], dtype='int64')\n4938 |         >>> idx.values\n4939 |         array([1, 2, 3])\n4940 | \n4941 |         For :class:`pandas.IntervalIndex`:\n4942 | \n4943 |         >>> idx = pd.interval_range(start=0, end=5)\n4944 |         >>> idx.values\n4945 |         <IntervalArray>\n4946 |         [(0, 1], (1, 2], (2, 3], (3, 4], (4, 5]]\n4947 |         Length: 5, dtype: interval[int64, right]\n4948 |         \"\"\"\n4949 |         data = self._data\n4950 |         if isinstance(data, np.ndarray):\n4951 |             data = data.view()\n4952 |             data.flags.writeable = False\n4953 |         return data\n4954 | \n4955 |     @cache_readonly\n4956 |     @doc(IndexOpsMixin.array)\n4957 |     def array(self) -> ExtensionArray:\n4958 |         array = self._data\n4959 |         if isinstance(array, np.ndarray):\n4960 |             from pandas.core.arrays.numpy_ import NumpyExtensionArray\n4961 | \n4962 |             array = NumpyExtensionArray(array)\n4963 |         return array\n4964 | \n4965 |     @property\n4966 |     def _values(self) -> ExtensionArray | np.ndarray:\n4967 |         \"\"\"\n4968 |         The best array representation.\n4969 | \n4970 |         This is an ndarray or ExtensionArray.\n4971 | \n4972 |         ``_values`` are consistent between ``Series`` and ``Index``.\n4973 | \n4974 |         It may differ from the public '.values' method.\n4975 | \n4976 |         index             | values          | _values       |\n4977 |         ----------------- | --------------- | ------------- |\n4978 |         Index             | ndarray         | ndarray       |\n4979 |         CategoricalIndex  | Categorical     | Categorical   |\n4980 |         DatetimeIndex     | ndarray[M8ns]   | DatetimeArray |\n4981 |         DatetimeIndex[tz] | ndarray[M8ns]   | DatetimeArray |\n4982 |         PeriodIndex       | ndarray[object] | PeriodArray   |\n4983 |         IntervalIndex     | IntervalArray   | IntervalArray |\n4984 | \n4985 |         See Also\n4986 |         --------\n4987 |         values : Values\n4988 |         \"\"\"\n4989 |         return self._data\n4990 | \n4991 |     def _get_engine_target(self) -> ArrayLike:\n4992 |         \"\"\"\n4993 |         Get the ndarray or ExtensionArray that we can pass to the IndexEngine\n4994 |         constructor.\n4995 |         \"\"\"\n4996 |         vals = self._values\n4997 |         if isinstance(vals, StringArray):\n4998 |             # GH#45652 much more performant than ExtensionEngine\n4999 |             return vals._ndarray\n5000 |         if isinstance(vals, ArrowExtensionArray) and self.dtype.kind in \"Mm\":\n5001 |             import pyarrow as pa\n5002 | \n5003 |             pa_type = vals._pa_array.type\n5004 |             if pa.types.is_timestamp(pa_type):\n5005 |                 vals = vals._to_datetimearray()\n5006 |                 return vals._ndarray.view(\"i8\")\n5007 |             elif pa.types.is_duration(pa_type):\n5008 |                 vals = vals._to_timedeltaarray()\n5009 |                 return vals._ndarray.view(\"i8\")\n5010 |         if (\n5011 |             type(self) is Index\n5012 |             and isinstance(self._values, ExtensionArray)\n5013 |             and not isinstance(self._values, BaseMaskedArray)\n5014 |             and not (\n5015 |                 isinstance(self._values, ArrowExtensionArray)\n5016 |                 and is_numeric_dtype(self.dtype)\n5017 |                 # Exclude decimal\n5018 |                 and self.dtype.kind != \"O\"\n5019 |             )\n5020 |         ):\n5021 |             # TODO(ExtensionIndex): remove special-case, just use self._values\n5022 |             return self._values.astype(object)\n5023 |         return vals\n5024 | \n5025 |     @final\n5026 |     def _get_join_target(self) -> np.ndarray:\n5027 |         \"\"\"\n5028 |         Get the ndarray or ExtensionArray that we can pass to the join\n5029 |         functions.\n5030 |         \"\"\"\n5031 |         if isinstance(self._values, BaseMaskedArray):\n5032 |             # This is only used if our array is monotonic, so no NAs present\n5033 |             return self._values._data\n5034 |         elif isinstance(self._values, ArrowExtensionArray):\n5035 |             # This is only used if our array is monotonic, so no missing values\n5036 |             # present\n5037 |             return self._values.to_numpy()\n5038 | \n5039 |         # TODO: exclude ABCRangeIndex case here as it copies\n5040 |         target = self._get_engine_target()\n5041 |         if not isinstance(target, np.ndarray):\n5042 |             raise ValueError(\"_can_use_libjoin should return False.\")\n5043 |         return target\n5044 | \n5045 |     def _from_join_target(self, result: np.ndarray) -> ArrayLike:\n5046 |         \"\"\"\n5047 |         Cast the ndarray returned from one of the libjoin.foo_indexer functions\n5048 |         back to type(self._data).\n5049 |         \"\"\"\n5050 |         if isinstance(self.values, BaseMaskedArray):\n5051 |             return type(self.values)(result, np.zeros(result.shape, dtype=np.bool_))\n5052 |         elif isinstance(self.values, (ArrowExtensionArray, StringArray)):\n5053 |             return type(self.values)._from_sequence(result, dtype=self.dtype)\n5054 |         return result\n5055 | \n5056 |     @doc(IndexOpsMixin._memory_usage)\n5057 |     def memory_usage(self, deep: bool = False) -> int:\n5058 |         result = self._memory_usage(deep=deep)\n5059 | \n5060 |         # include our engine hashtable, only if it's already cached\n5061 |         if \"_engine\" in self._cache:\n5062 |             result += self._engine.sizeof(deep=deep)\n5063 |         return result\n5064 | \n5065 |     @final\n5066 |     def where(self, cond, other=None) -> Index:\n5067 |         \"\"\"\n5068 |         Replace values where the condition is False.\n5069 | \n5070 |         The replacement is taken from other.\n5071 | \n5072 |         Parameters\n5073 |         ----------\n5074 |         cond : bool array-like with the same length as self\n5075 |             Condition to select the values on.\n5076 |         other : scalar, or array-like, default None\n5077 |             Replacement if the condition is False.\n5078 | \n5079 |         Returns\n5080 |         -------\n5081 |         pandas.Index\n5082 |             A copy of self with values replaced from other\n5083 |             where the condition is False.\n5084 | \n5085 |         See Also\n5086 |         --------\n5087 |         Series.where : Same method for Series.\n5088 |         DataFrame.where : Same method for DataFrame.\n5089 | \n5090 |         Examples\n5091 |         --------\n5092 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"car\", \"bike\", \"train\", \"tractor\"])\n5093 |         >>> idx\n5094 |         Index(['car', 'bike', 'train', 'tractor'], dtype='object')\n5095 |         >>> idx.where(idx.isin([\"car\", \"train\"]), \"other\")\n5096 |         Index(['car', 'other', 'train', 'other'], dtype='object')\n5097 |         \"\"\"\n5098 |         if isinstance(self, ABCMultiIndex):\n5099 |             raise NotImplementedError(\n5100 |                 \".where is not supported for MultiIndex operations\"\n5101 |             )\n5102 |         cond = np.asarray(cond, dtype=bool)\n5103 |         return self.putmask(~cond, other)\n5104 | \n5105 |     # construction helpers\n5106 |     @final\n5107 |     @classmethod\n5108 |     def _raise_scalar_data_error(cls, data):\n5109 |         # We return the TypeError so that we can raise it from the constructor\n5110 |         #  in order to keep mypy happy\n5111 |         raise TypeError(\n5112 |             f\"{cls.__name__}(...) must be called with a collection of some \"\n5113 |             f\"kind, {repr(data) if not isinstance(data, np.generic) else str(data)} \"\n5114 |             \"was passed\"\n5115 |         )\n5116 | \n5117 |     def _validate_fill_value(self, value):\n5118 |         \"\"\"\n5119 |         Check if the value can be inserted into our array without casting,\n5120 |         and convert it to an appropriate native type if necessary.\n5121 | \n5122 |         Raises\n5123 |         ------\n5124 |         TypeError\n5125 |             If the value cannot be inserted into an array of this dtype.\n5126 |         \"\"\"\n5127 |         dtype = self.dtype\n5128 |         if isinstance(dtype, np.dtype) and dtype.kind not in \"mM\":\n5129 |             # return np_can_hold_element(dtype, value)\n5130 |             try:\n5131 |                 return np_can_hold_element(dtype, value)\n5132 |             except LossySetitemError as err:\n5133 |                 # re-raise as TypeError for consistency\n5134 |                 raise TypeError from err\n5135 |         elif not can_hold_element(self._values, value):\n5136 |             raise TypeError\n5137 |         return value\n5138 | \n5139 |     @cache_readonly\n5140 |     def _is_memory_usage_qualified(self) -> bool:\n5141 |         \"\"\"\n5142 |         Return a boolean if we need a qualified .info display.\n5143 |         \"\"\"\n5144 |         return is_object_dtype(self.dtype) or (\n5145 |             is_string_dtype(self.dtype) and self.dtype.storage == \"python\"  # type: ignore[union-attr]\n5146 |         )\n5147 | \n5148 |     def __contains__(self, key: Any) -> bool:\n5149 |         \"\"\"\n5150 |         Return a boolean indicating whether the provided key is in the index.\n5151 | \n5152 |         Parameters\n5153 |         ----------\n5154 |         key : label\n5155 |             The key to check if it is present in the index.\n5156 | \n5157 |         Returns\n5158 |         -------\n5159 |         bool\n5160 |             Whether the key search is in the index.\n5161 | \n5162 |         Raises\n5163 |         ------\n5164 |         TypeError\n5165 |             If the key is not hashable.\n5166 | \n5167 |         See Also\n5168 |         --------\n5169 |         Index.isin : Returns an ndarray of boolean dtype indicating whether the\n5170 |             list-like key is in the index.\n5171 | \n5172 |         Examples\n5173 |         --------\n5174 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3, 4])\n5175 |         >>> idx\n5176 |         Index([1, 2, 3, 4], dtype='int64')\n5177 | \n5178 |         >>> 2 in idx\n5179 |         True\n5180 |         >>> 6 in idx\n5181 |         False\n5182 |         \"\"\"\n5183 |         hash(key)\n5184 |         try:\n5185 |             return key in self._engine\n5186 |         except (OverflowError, TypeError, ValueError):\n5187 |             return False\n5188 | \n5189 |     # https://github.com/python/typeshed/issues/2148#issuecomment-520783318\n5190 |     # Incompatible types in assignment (expression has type \"None\", base class\n5191 |     # \"object\" defined the type as \"Callable[[object], int]\")\n5192 |     __hash__: ClassVar[None]  # type: ignore[assignment]\n5193 | \n5194 |     @final\n5195 |     def __setitem__(self, key, value) -> None:\n5196 |         raise TypeError(\"Index does not support mutable operations\")\n5197 | \n5198 |     def __getitem__(self, key):\n5199 |         \"\"\"\n5200 |         Override numpy.ndarray's __getitem__ method to work as desired.\n5201 | \n5202 |         This function adds lists and Series as valid boolean indexers\n5203 |         (ndarrays only supports ndarray with dtype=bool).\n5204 | \n5205 |         If resulting ndim != 1, plain ndarray is returned instead of\n5206 |         corresponding `Index` subclass.\n5207 | \n5208 |         \"\"\"\n5209 |         getitem = self._data.__getitem__\n5210 | \n5211 |         if is_integer(key) or is_float(key):\n5212 |             # GH#44051 exclude bool, which would return a 2d ndarray\n5213 |             key = com.cast_scalar_indexer(key)\n5214 |             return getitem(key)\n5215 | \n5216 |         if isinstance(key, slice):\n5217 |             # This case is separated from the conditional above to avoid\n5218 |             # pessimization com.is_bool_indexer and ndim checks.\n5219 |             return self._getitem_slice(key)\n5220 | \n5221 |         if com.is_bool_indexer(key):\n5222 |             # if we have list[bools, length=1e5] then doing this check+convert\n5223 |             #  takes 166 \u00b5s + 2.1 ms and cuts the ndarray.__getitem__\n5224 |             #  time below from 3.8 ms to 496 \u00b5s\n5225 |             # if we already have ndarray[bool], the overhead is 1.4 \u00b5s or .25%\n5226 |             if isinstance(getattr(key, \"dtype\", None), ExtensionDtype):\n5227 |                 key = key.to_numpy(dtype=bool, na_value=False)\n5228 |             else:\n5229 |                 key = np.asarray(key, dtype=bool)\n5230 | \n5231 |             if not isinstance(self.dtype, ExtensionDtype):\n5232 |                 if len(key) == 0 and len(key) != len(self):\n5233 |                     raise ValueError(\n5234 |                         \"The length of the boolean indexer cannot be 0 \"\n5235 |                         \"when the Index has length greater than 0.\"\n5236 |                     )\n5237 | \n5238 |         result = getitem(key)\n5239 |         # Because we ruled out integer above, we always get an arraylike here\n5240 |         if result.ndim > 1:\n5241 |             disallow_ndim_indexing(result)\n5242 | \n5243 |         # NB: Using _constructor._simple_new would break if MultiIndex\n5244 |         #  didn't override __getitem__\n5245 |         return self._constructor._simple_new(result, name=self._name)\n5246 | \n5247 |     def _getitem_slice(self, slobj: slice) -> Self:\n5248 |         \"\"\"\n5249 |         Fastpath for __getitem__ when we know we have a slice.\n5250 |         \"\"\"\n5251 |         res = self._data[slobj]\n5252 |         result = type(self)._simple_new(res, name=self._name, refs=self._references)\n5253 |         if \"_engine\" in self._cache:\n5254 |             reverse = slobj.step is not None and slobj.step < 0\n5255 |             result._engine._update_from_sliced(self._engine, reverse=reverse)  # type: ignore[union-attr]\n5256 | \n5257 |         return result\n5258 | \n5259 |     @final\n5260 |     def _can_hold_identifiers_and_holds_name(self, name) -> bool:\n5261 |         \"\"\"\n5262 |         Faster check for ``name in self`` when we know `name` is a Python\n5263 |         identifier (e.g. in NDFrame.__getattr__, which hits this to support\n5264 |         . key lookup). For indexes that can't hold identifiers (everything\n5265 |         but object & categorical) we just return False.\n5266 | \n5267 |         https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/issues/19764\n5268 |         \"\"\"\n5269 |         if (\n5270 |             is_object_dtype(self.dtype)\n5271 |             or is_string_dtype(self.dtype)\n5272 |             or isinstance(self.dtype, CategoricalDtype)\n5273 |         ):\n5274 |             return name in self\n5275 |         return False\n5276 | \n5277 |     def append(self, other: Index | Sequence[Index]) -> Index:\n5278 |         \"\"\"\n5279 |         Append a collection of Index options together.\n5280 | \n5281 |         Parameters\n5282 |         ----------\n5283 |         other : Index or list/tuple of indices\n5284 |             Single Index or a collection of indices, which can be either a list or a\n5285 |             tuple.\n5286 | \n5287 |         Returns\n5288 |         -------\n5289 |         Index\n5290 |             Returns a new Index object resulting from appending the provided other\n5291 |             indices to the original Index.\n5292 | \n5293 |         See Also\n5294 |         --------\n5295 |         Index.insert : Make new Index inserting new item at location.\n5296 | \n5297 |         Examples\n5298 |         --------\n5299 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3])\n5300 |         >>> idx.append(pd.Index([4]))\n5301 |         Index([1, 2, 3, 4], dtype='int64')\n5302 |         \"\"\"\n5303 |         to_concat = [self]\n5304 | \n5305 |         if isinstance(other, (list, tuple)):\n5306 |             to_concat += list(other)\n5307 |         else:\n5308 |             # error: Argument 1 to \"append\" of \"list\" has incompatible type\n5309 |             # \"Union[Index, Sequence[Index]]\"; expected \"Index\"\n5310 |             to_concat.append(other)  # type: ignore[arg-type]\n5311 | \n5312 |         for obj in to_concat:\n5313 |             if not isinstance(obj, Index):\n5314 |                 raise TypeError(\"all inputs must be Index\")\n5315 | \n5316 |         names = {obj.name for obj in to_concat}\n5317 |         name = None if len(names) > 1 else self.name\n5318 | \n5319 |         return self._concat(to_concat, name)\n5320 | \n5321 |     def _concat(self, to_concat: list[Index], name: Hashable) -> Index:\n5322 |         \"\"\"\n5323 |         Concatenate multiple Index objects.\n5324 |         \"\"\"\n5325 |         to_concat_vals = [x._values for x in to_concat]\n5326 | \n5327 |         result = concat_compat(to_concat_vals)\n5328 | \n5329 |         return Index._with_infer(result, name=name)\n5330 | \n5331 |     def putmask(self, mask, value) -> Index:\n5332 |         \"\"\"\n5333 |         Return a new Index of the values set with the mask.\n5334 | \n5335 |         Parameters\n5336 |         ----------\n5337 |         mask : np.ndarray[bool]\n5338 |             Array of booleans denoting where values in the original\n5339 |             data are not ``NA``.\n5340 |         value : scalar\n5341 |             Scalar value to use to fill holes (e.g. 0).\n5342 |             This value cannot be a list-likes.\n5343 | \n5344 |         Returns\n5345 |         -------\n5346 |         Index\n5347 |             A new Index of the values set with the mask.\n5348 | \n5349 |         See Also\n5350 |         --------\n5351 |         numpy.ndarray.putmask : Changes elements of an array\n5352 |             based on conditional and input values.\n5353 | \n5354 |         Examples\n5355 |         --------\n5356 |         >>> idx1 = pd.Index([1, 2, 3])\n5357 |         >>> idx2 = pd.Index([5, 6, 7])\n5358 |         >>> idx1.putmask([True, False, False], idx2)\n5359 |         Index([5, 2, 3], dtype='int64')\n5360 |         \"\"\"\n5361 |         mask, noop = validate_putmask(self._values, mask)\n5362 |         if noop:\n5363 |             return self.copy()\n5364 | \n5365 |         if self.dtype != object and is_valid_na_for_dtype(value, self.dtype):\n5366 |             # e.g. None -> np.nan, see also Block._standardize_fill_value\n5367 |             value = self._na_value\n5368 | \n5369 |         try:\n5370 |             converted = self._validate_fill_value(value)\n5371 |         except (LossySetitemError, ValueError, TypeError) as err:\n5372 |             if is_object_dtype(self.dtype):  # pragma: no cover\n5373 |                 raise err\n5374 | \n5375 |             # See also: Block.coerce_to_target_dtype\n5376 |             dtype = self._find_common_type_compat(value)\n5377 |             return self.astype(dtype).putmask(mask, value)\n5378 | \n5379 |         values = self._values.copy()\n5380 | \n5381 |         if isinstance(values, np.ndarray):\n5382 |             converted = setitem_datetimelike_compat(values, mask.sum(), converted)\n5383 |             np.putmask(values, mask, converted)\n5384 | \n5385 |         else:\n5386 |             # Note: we use the original value here, not converted, as\n5387 |             #  _validate_fill_value is not idempotent\n5388 |             values._putmask(mask, value)\n5389 | \n5390 |         return self._shallow_copy(values)\n5391 | \n5392 |     def equals(self, other: Any) -> bool:\n5393 |         \"\"\"\n5394 |         Determine if two Index object are equal.\n5395 | \n5396 |         The things that are being compared are:\n5397 | \n5398 |         * The elements inside the Index object.\n5399 |         * The order of the elements inside the Index object.\n5400 | \n5401 |         Parameters\n5402 |         ----------\n5403 |         other : Any\n5404 |             The other object to compare against.\n5405 | \n5406 |         Returns\n5407 |         -------\n5408 |         bool\n5409 |             True if \"other\" is an Index and it has the same elements and order\n5410 |             as the calling index; False otherwise.\n5411 | \n5412 |         See Also\n5413 |         --------\n5414 |         Index.identical: Checks that object attributes and types are also equal.\n5415 |         Index.has_duplicates: Check if the Index has duplicate values.\n5416 |         Index.is_unique: Return if the index has unique values.\n5417 | \n5418 |         Examples\n5419 |         --------\n5420 |         >>> idx1 = pd.Index([1, 2, 3])\n5421 |         >>> idx1\n5422 |         Index([1, 2, 3], dtype='int64')\n5423 |         >>> idx1.equals(pd.Index([1, 2, 3]))\n5424 |         True\n5425 | \n5426 |         The elements inside are compared\n5427 | \n5428 |         >>> idx2 = pd.Index([\"1\", \"2\", \"3\"])\n5429 |         >>> idx2\n5430 |         Index(['1', '2', '3'], dtype='object')\n5431 | \n5432 |         >>> idx1.equals(idx2)\n5433 |         False\n5434 | \n5435 |         The order is compared\n5436 | \n5437 |         >>> ascending_idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3])\n5438 |         >>> ascending_idx\n5439 |         Index([1, 2, 3], dtype='int64')\n5440 |         >>> descending_idx = pd.Index([3, 2, 1])\n5441 |         >>> descending_idx\n5442 |         Index([3, 2, 1], dtype='int64')\n5443 |         >>> ascending_idx.equals(descending_idx)\n5444 |         False\n5445 | \n5446 |         The dtype is *not* compared\n5447 | \n5448 |         >>> int64_idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3], dtype=\"int64\")\n5449 |         >>> int64_idx\n5450 |         Index([1, 2, 3], dtype='int64')\n5451 |         >>> uint64_idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3], dtype=\"uint64\")\n5452 |         >>> uint64_idx\n5453 |         Index([1, 2, 3], dtype='uint64')\n5454 |         >>> int64_idx.equals(uint64_idx)\n5455 |         True\n5456 |         \"\"\"\n5457 |         if self.is_(other):\n5458 |             return True\n5459 | \n5460 |         if not isinstance(other, Index):\n5461 |             return False\n5462 | \n5463 |         if len(self) != len(other):\n5464 |             # quickly return if the lengths are different\n5465 |             return False\n5466 | \n5467 |         if (\n5468 |             isinstance(self.dtype, StringDtype)\n5469 |             and self.dtype.na_value is np.nan\n5470 |             and other.dtype != self.dtype\n5471 |         ):\n5472 |             # TODO(infer_string) can we avoid this special case?\n5473 |             # special case for object behavior\n5474 |             return other.equals(self.astype(object))\n5475 | \n5476 |         if is_object_dtype(self.dtype) and not is_object_dtype(other.dtype):\n5477 |             # if other is not object, use other's logic for coercion\n5478 |             return other.equals(self)\n5479 | \n5480 |         if isinstance(other, ABCMultiIndex):\n5481 |             # d-level MultiIndex can equal d-tuple Index\n5482 |             return other.equals(self)\n5483 | \n5484 |         if isinstance(self._values, ExtensionArray):\n5485 |             # Dispatch to the ExtensionArray's .equals method.\n5486 |             if not isinstance(other, type(self)):\n5487 |                 return False\n5488 | \n5489 |             earr = cast(ExtensionArray, self._data)\n5490 |             return earr.equals(other._data)\n5491 | \n5492 |         if isinstance(other.dtype, ExtensionDtype):\n5493 |             # All EA-backed Index subclasses override equals\n5494 |             return other.equals(self)\n5495 | \n5496 |         return array_equivalent(self._values, other._values)\n5497 | \n5498 |     @final\n5499 |     def identical(self, other) -> bool:\n5500 |         \"\"\"\n5501 |         Similar to equals, but checks that object attributes and types are also equal.\n5502 | \n5503 |         Parameters\n5504 |         ----------\n5505 |         other : Index\n5506 |             The Index object you want to compare with the current Index object.\n5507 | \n5508 |         Returns\n5509 |         -------\n5510 |         bool\n5511 |             If two Index objects have equal elements and same type True,\n5512 |             otherwise False.\n5513 | \n5514 |         See Also\n5515 |         --------\n5516 |         Index.equals: Determine if two Index object are equal.\n5517 |         Index.has_duplicates: Check if the Index has duplicate values.\n5518 |         Index.is_unique: Return if the index has unique values.\n5519 | \n5520 |         Examples\n5521 |         --------\n5522 |         >>> idx1 = pd.Index([\"1\", \"2\", \"3\"])\n5523 |         >>> idx2 = pd.Index([\"1\", \"2\", \"3\"])\n5524 |         >>> idx2.identical(idx1)\n5525 |         True\n5526 | \n5527 |         >>> idx1 = pd.Index([\"1\", \"2\", \"3\"], name=\"A\")\n5528 |         >>> idx2 = pd.Index([\"1\", \"2\", \"3\"], name=\"B\")\n5529 |         >>> idx2.identical(idx1)\n5530 |         False\n5531 |         \"\"\"\n5532 |         return (\n5533 |             self.equals(other)\n5534 |             and all(\n5535 |                 getattr(self, c, None) == getattr(other, c, None)\n5536 |                 for c in self._comparables\n5537 |             )\n5538 |             and type(self) == type(other)\n5539 |             and self.dtype == other.dtype\n5540 |         )\n5541 | \n5542 |     @final\n5543 |     def asof(self, label):\n5544 |         \"\"\"\n5545 |         Return the label from the index, or, if not present, the previous one.\n5546 | \n5547 |         Assuming that the index is sorted, return the passed index label if it\n5548 |         is in the index, or return the previous index label if the passed one\n5549 |         is not in the index.\n5550 | \n5551 |         Parameters\n5552 |         ----------\n5553 |         label : object\n5554 |             The label up to which the method returns the latest index label.\n5555 | \n5556 |         Returns\n5557 |         -------\n5558 |         object\n5559 |             The passed label if it is in the index. The previous label if the\n5560 |             passed label is not in the sorted index or `NaN` if there is no\n5561 |             such label.\n5562 | \n5563 |         See Also\n5564 |         --------\n5565 |         Series.asof : Return the latest value in a Series up to the\n5566 |             passed index.\n5567 |         merge_asof : Perform an asof merge (similar to left join but it\n5568 |             matches on nearest key rather than equal key).\n5569 |         Index.get_loc : An `asof` is a thin wrapper around `get_loc`\n5570 |             with method='pad'.\n5571 | \n5572 |         Examples\n5573 |         --------\n5574 |         `Index.asof` returns the latest index label up to the passed label.\n5575 | \n5576 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"2013-12-31\", \"2014-01-02\", \"2014-01-03\"])\n5577 |         >>> idx.asof(\"2014-01-01\")\n5578 |         '2013-12-31'\n5579 | \n5580 |         If the label is in the index, the method returns the passed label.\n5581 | \n5582 |         >>> idx.asof(\"2014-01-02\")\n5583 |         '2014-01-02'\n5584 | \n5585 |         If all of the labels in the index are later than the passed label,\n5586 |         NaN is returned.\n5587 | \n5588 |         >>> idx.asof(\"1999-01-02\")\n5589 |         nan\n5590 | \n5591 |         If the index is not sorted, an error is raised.\n5592 | \n5593 |         >>> idx_not_sorted = pd.Index([\"2013-12-31\", \"2015-01-02\", \"2014-01-03\"])\n5594 |         >>> idx_not_sorted.asof(\"2013-12-31\")\n5595 |         Traceback (most recent call last):\n5596 |         ValueError: index must be monotonic increasing or decreasing\n5597 |         \"\"\"\n5598 |         self._searchsorted_monotonic(label)  # validate sortedness\n5599 |         try:\n5600 |             loc = self.get_loc(label)\n5601 |         except (KeyError, TypeError) as err:\n5602 |             # KeyError -> No exact match, try for padded\n5603 |             # TypeError -> passed e.g. non-hashable, fall through to get\n5604 |             #  the tested exception message\n5605 |             indexer = self.get_indexer([label], method=\"pad\")\n5606 |             if indexer.ndim > 1 or indexer.size > 1:\n5607 |                 raise TypeError(\"asof requires scalar valued input\") from err\n5608 |             loc = indexer.item()\n5609 |             if loc == -1:\n5610 |                 return self._na_value\n5611 |         else:\n5612 |             if isinstance(loc, slice):\n5613 |                 loc = loc.indices(len(self))[-1]\n5614 | \n5615 |         return self[loc]\n5616 | \n5617 |     def asof_locs(\n5618 |         self, where: Index, mask: npt.NDArray[np.bool_]\n5619 |     ) -> npt.NDArray[np.intp]:\n5620 |         \"\"\"\n5621 |         Return the locations (indices) of labels in the index.\n5622 | \n5623 |         As in the :meth:`pandas.Index.asof`, if the label (a particular entry in\n5624 |         ``where``) is not in the index, the latest index label up to the\n5625 |         passed label is chosen and its index returned.\n5626 | \n5627 |         If all of the labels in the index are later than a label in ``where``,\n5628 |         -1 is returned.\n5629 | \n5630 |         ``mask`` is used to ignore ``NA`` values in the index during calculation.\n5631 | \n5632 |         Parameters\n5633 |         ----------\n5634 |         where : Index\n5635 |             An Index consisting of an array of timestamps.\n5636 |         mask : np.ndarray[bool]\n5637 |             Array of booleans denoting where values in the original\n5638 |             data are not ``NA``.\n5639 | \n5640 |         Returns\n5641 |         -------\n5642 |         np.ndarray[np.intp]\n5643 |             An array of locations (indices) of the labels from the index\n5644 |             which correspond to the return values of :meth:`pandas.Index.asof`\n5645 |             for every element in ``where``.\n5646 | \n5647 |         See Also\n5648 |         --------\n5649 |         Index.asof : Return the label from the index, or, if not present, the\n5650 |             previous one.\n5651 | \n5652 |         Examples\n5653 |         --------\n5654 |         >>> idx = pd.date_range(\"2023-06-01\", periods=3, freq=\"D\")\n5655 |         >>> where = pd.DatetimeIndex(\n5656 |         ...     [\"2023-05-30 00:12:00\", \"2023-06-01 00:00:00\", \"2023-06-02 23:59:59\"]\n5657 |         ... )\n5658 |         >>> mask = np.ones(3, dtype=bool)\n5659 |         >>> idx.asof_locs(where, mask)\n5660 |         array([-1,  0,  1])\n5661 | \n5662 |         We can use ``mask`` to ignore certain values in the index during calculation.\n5663 | \n5664 |         >>> mask[1] = False\n5665 |         >>> idx.asof_locs(where, mask)\n5666 |         array([-1,  0,  0])\n5667 |         \"\"\"\n5668 |         # error: No overload variant of \"searchsorted\" of \"ndarray\" matches argument\n5669 |         # types \"Union[ExtensionArray, ndarray[Any, Any]]\", \"str\"\n5670 |         # TODO: will be fixed when ExtensionArray.searchsorted() is fixed\n5671 |         locs = self._values[mask].searchsorted(\n5672 |             where._values,\n5673 |             side=\"right\",  # type: ignore[call-overload]\n5674 |         )\n5675 |         locs = np.where(locs > 0, locs - 1, 0)\n5676 | \n5677 |         result = np.arange(len(self), dtype=np.intp)[mask].take(locs)\n5678 | \n5679 |         first_value = self._values[mask.argmax()]\n5680 |         result[(locs == 0) & (where._values < first_value)] = -1\n5681 | \n5682 |         return result\n5683 | \n5684 |     @overload\n5685 |     def sort_values(\n5686 |         self,\n5687 |         *,\n5688 |         return_indexer: Literal[False] = ...,\n5689 |         ascending: bool = ...,\n5690 |         na_position: NaPosition = ...,\n5691 |         key: Callable | None = ...,\n5692 |     ) -> Self: ...\n5693 | \n5694 |     @overload\n5695 |     def sort_values(\n5696 |         self,\n5697 |         *,\n5698 |         return_indexer: Literal[True],\n5699 |         ascending: bool = ...,\n5700 |         na_position: NaPosition = ...,\n5701 |         key: Callable | None = ...,\n5702 |     ) -> tuple[Self, np.ndarray]: ...\n5703 | \n5704 |     @overload\n5705 |     def sort_values(\n5706 |         self,\n5707 |         *,\n5708 |         return_indexer: bool = ...,\n5709 |         ascending: bool = ...,\n5710 |         na_position: NaPosition = ...,\n5711 |         key: Callable | None = ...,\n5712 |     ) -> Self | tuple[Self, np.ndarray]: ...\n5713 | \n5714 |     def sort_values(\n5715 |         self,\n5716 |         *,\n5717 |         return_indexer: bool = False,\n5718 |         ascending: bool = True,\n5719 |         na_position: NaPosition = \"last\",\n5720 |         key: Callable | None = None,\n5721 |     ) -> Self | tuple[Self, np.ndarray]:\n5722 |         \"\"\"\n5723 |         Return a sorted copy of the index.\n5724 | \n5725 |         Return a sorted copy of the index, and optionally return the indices\n5726 |         that sorted the index itself.\n5727 | \n5728 |         Parameters\n5729 |         ----------\n5730 |         return_indexer : bool, default False\n5731 |             Should the indices that would sort the index be returned.\n5732 |         ascending : bool, default True\n5733 |             Should the index values be sorted in an ascending order.\n5734 |         na_position : {'first' or 'last'}, default 'last'\n5735 |             Argument 'first' puts NaNs at the beginning, 'last' puts NaNs at\n5736 |             the end.\n5737 |         key : callable, optional\n5738 |             If not None, apply the key function to the index values\n5739 |             before sorting. This is similar to the `key` argument in the\n5740 |             builtin :meth:`sorted` function, with the notable difference that\n5741 |             this `key` function should be *vectorized*. It should expect an\n5742 |             ``Index`` and return an ``Index`` of the same shape.\n5743 | \n5744 |         Returns\n5745 |         -------\n5746 |         sorted_index : pandas.Index\n5747 |             Sorted copy of the index.\n5748 |         indexer : numpy.ndarray, optional\n5749 |             The indices that the index itself was sorted by.\n5750 | \n5751 |         See Also\n5752 |         --------\n5753 |         Series.sort_values : Sort values of a Series.\n5754 |         DataFrame.sort_values : Sort values in a DataFrame.\n5755 | \n5756 |         Examples\n5757 |         --------\n5758 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([10, 100, 1, 1000])\n5759 |         >>> idx\n5760 |         Index([10, 100, 1, 1000], dtype='int64')\n5761 | \n5762 |         Sort values in ascending order (default behavior).\n5763 | \n5764 |         >>> idx.sort_values()\n5765 |         Index([1, 10, 100, 1000], dtype='int64')\n5766 | \n5767 |         Sort values in descending order, and also get the indices `idx` was\n5768 |         sorted by.\n5769 | \n5770 |         >>> idx.sort_values(ascending=False, return_indexer=True)\n5771 |         (Index([1000, 100, 10, 1], dtype='int64'), array([3, 1, 0, 2]))\n5772 |         \"\"\"\n5773 |         if key is None and (\n5774 |             (ascending and self.is_monotonic_increasing)\n5775 |             or (not ascending and self.is_monotonic_decreasing)\n5776 |         ):\n5777 |             if return_indexer:\n5778 |                 indexer = np.arange(len(self), dtype=np.intp)\n5779 |                 return self.copy(), indexer\n5780 |             else:\n5781 |                 return self.copy()\n5782 | \n5783 |         # GH 35584. Sort missing values according to na_position kwarg\n5784 |         # ignore na_position for MultiIndex\n5785 |         if not isinstance(self, ABCMultiIndex):\n5786 |             _as = nargsort(\n5787 |                 items=self, ascending=ascending, na_position=na_position, key=key\n5788 |             )\n5789 |         else:\n5790 |             idx = cast(Index, ensure_key_mapped(self, key))\n5791 |             _as = idx.argsort(na_position=na_position)\n5792 |             if not ascending:\n5793 |                 _as = _as[::-1]\n5794 | \n5795 |         sorted_index = self.take(_as)\n5796 | \n5797 |         if return_indexer:\n5798 |             return sorted_index, _as\n5799 |         else:\n5800 |             return sorted_index\n5801 | \n5802 |     def shift(self, periods: int = 1, freq=None) -> Self:\n5803 |         \"\"\"\n5804 |         Shift index by desired number of time frequency increments.\n5805 | \n5806 |         This method is for shifting the values of datetime-like indexes\n5807 |         by a specified time increment a given number of times.\n5808 | \n5809 |         Parameters\n5810 |         ----------\n5811 |         periods : int, default 1\n5812 |             Number of periods (or increments) to shift by,\n5813 |             can be positive or negative.\n5814 |         freq : pandas.DateOffset, pandas.Timedelta or str, optional\n5815 |             Frequency increment to shift by.\n5816 |             If None, the index is shifted by its own `freq` attribute.\n5817 |             Offset aliases are valid strings, e.g., 'D', 'W', 'M' etc.\n5818 | \n5819 |         Returns\n5820 |         -------\n5821 |         pandas.Index\n5822 |             Shifted index.\n5823 | \n5824 |         See Also\n5825 |         --------\n5826 |         Series.shift : Shift values of Series.\n5827 | \n5828 |         Notes\n5829 |         -----\n5830 |         This method is only implemented for datetime-like index classes,\n5831 |         i.e., DatetimeIndex, PeriodIndex and TimedeltaIndex.\n5832 | \n5833 |         Examples\n5834 |         --------\n5835 |         Put the first 5 month starts of 2011 into an index.\n5836 | \n5837 |         >>> month_starts = pd.date_range(\"1/1/2011\", periods=5, freq=\"MS\")\n5838 |         >>> month_starts\n5839 |         DatetimeIndex(['2011-01-01', '2011-02-01', '2011-03-01', '2011-04-01',\n5840 |                        '2011-05-01'],\n5841 |                       dtype='datetime64[ns]', freq='MS')\n5842 | \n5843 |         Shift the index by 10 days.\n5844 | \n5845 |         >>> month_starts.shift(10, freq=\"D\")\n5846 |         DatetimeIndex(['2011-01-11', '2011-02-11', '2011-03-11', '2011-04-11',\n5847 |                        '2011-05-11'],\n5848 |                       dtype='datetime64[ns]', freq=None)\n5849 | \n5850 |         The default value of `freq` is the `freq` attribute of the index,\n5851 |         which is 'MS' (month start) in this example.\n5852 | \n5853 |         >>> month_starts.shift(10)\n5854 |         DatetimeIndex(['2011-11-01', '2011-12-01', '2012-01-01', '2012-02-01',\n5855 |                        '2012-03-01'],\n5856 |                       dtype='datetime64[ns]', freq='MS')\n5857 |         \"\"\"\n5858 |         raise NotImplementedError(\n5859 |             f\"This method is only implemented for DatetimeIndex, PeriodIndex and \"\n5860 |             f\"TimedeltaIndex; Got type {type(self).__name__}\"\n5861 |         )\n5862 | \n5863 |     def argsort(self, *args, **kwargs) -> npt.NDArray[np.intp]:\n5864 |         \"\"\"\n5865 |         Return the integer indices that would sort the index.\n5866 | \n5867 |         Parameters\n5868 |         ----------\n5869 |         *args\n5870 |             Passed to `numpy.ndarray.argsort`.\n5871 |         **kwargs\n5872 |             Passed to `numpy.ndarray.argsort`.\n5873 | \n5874 |         Returns\n5875 |         -------\n5876 |         np.ndarray[np.intp]\n5877 |             Integer indices that would sort the index if used as\n5878 |             an indexer.\n5879 | \n5880 |         See Also\n5881 |         --------\n5882 |         numpy.argsort : Similar method for NumPy arrays.\n5883 |         Index.sort_values : Return sorted copy of Index.\n5884 | \n5885 |         Examples\n5886 |         --------\n5887 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"b\", \"a\", \"d\", \"c\"])\n5888 |         >>> idx\n5889 |         Index(['b', 'a', 'd', 'c'], dtype='object')\n5890 | \n5891 |         >>> order = idx.argsort()\n5892 |         >>> order\n5893 |         array([1, 0, 3, 2])\n5894 | \n5895 |         >>> idx[order]\n5896 |         Index(['a', 'b', 'c', 'd'], dtype='object')\n5897 |         \"\"\"\n5898 |         # This works for either ndarray or EA, is overridden\n5899 |         #  by RangeIndex, MultIIndex\n5900 |         return self._data.argsort(*args, **kwargs)\n5901 | \n5902 |     def _check_indexing_error(self, key) -> None:\n5903 |         if not is_scalar(key):\n5904 |             # if key is not a scalar, directly raise an error (the code below\n5905 |             # would convert to numpy arrays and raise later any way) - GH29926\n5906 |             raise InvalidIndexError(key)\n5907 | \n5908 |     @cache_readonly\n5909 |     def _should_fallback_to_positional(self) -> bool:\n5910 |         \"\"\"\n5911 |         Should an integer key be treated as positional?\n5912 |         \"\"\"\n5913 |         return self.inferred_type not in {\n5914 |             \"integer\",\n5915 |             \"mixed-integer\",\n5916 |             \"floating\",\n5917 |             \"complex\",\n5918 |         }\n5919 | \n5920 |     _index_shared_docs[\"get_indexer_non_unique\"] = \"\"\"\n5921 |         Compute indexer and mask for new index given the current index.\n5922 | \n5923 |         The indexer should be then used as an input to ndarray.take to align the\n5924 |         current data to the new index.\n5925 | \n5926 |         Parameters\n5927 |         ----------\n5928 |         target : %(target_klass)s\n5929 |             An iterable containing the values to be used for computing indexer.\n5930 | \n5931 |         Returns\n5932 |         -------\n5933 |         indexer : np.ndarray[np.intp]\n5934 |             Integers from 0 to n - 1 indicating that the index at these\n5935 |             positions matches the corresponding target values. Missing values\n5936 |             in the target are marked by -1.\n5937 |         missing : np.ndarray[np.intp]\n5938 |             An indexer into the target of the values not found.\n5939 |             These correspond to the -1 in the indexer array.\n5940 | \n5941 |         See Also\n5942 |         --------\n5943 |         Index.get_indexer : Computes indexer and mask for new index given\n5944 |             the current index.\n5945 |         Index.get_indexer_for : Returns an indexer even when non-unique.\n5946 | \n5947 |         Examples\n5948 |         --------\n5949 |         >>> index = pd.Index(['c', 'b', 'a', 'b', 'b'])\n5950 |         >>> index.get_indexer_non_unique(['b', 'b'])\n5951 |         (array([1, 3, 4, 1, 3, 4]), array([], dtype=int64))\n5952 | \n5953 |         In the example below there are no matched values.\n5954 | \n5955 |         >>> index = pd.Index(['c', 'b', 'a', 'b', 'b'])\n5956 |         >>> index.get_indexer_non_unique(['q', 'r', 't'])\n5957 |         (array([-1, -1, -1]), array([0, 1, 2]))\n5958 | \n5959 |         For this reason, the returned ``indexer`` contains only integers equal to -1.\n5960 |         It demonstrates that there's no match between the index and the ``target``\n5961 |         values at these positions. The mask [0, 1, 2] in the return value shows that\n5962 |         the first, second, and third elements are missing.\n5963 | \n5964 |         Notice that the return value is a tuple contains two items. In the example\n5965 |         below the first item is an array of locations in ``index``. The second\n5966 |         item is a mask shows that the first and third elements are missing.\n5967 | \n5968 |         >>> index = pd.Index(['c', 'b', 'a', 'b', 'b'])\n5969 |         >>> index.get_indexer_non_unique(['f', 'b', 's'])\n5970 |         (array([-1,  1,  3,  4, -1]), array([0, 2]))\n5971 |         \"\"\"\n5972 | \n5973 |     @Appender(_index_shared_docs[\"get_indexer_non_unique\"] % _index_doc_kwargs)\n5974 |     def get_indexer_non_unique(\n5975 |         self, target\n5976 |     ) -> tuple[npt.NDArray[np.intp], npt.NDArray[np.intp]]:\n5977 |         target = self._maybe_cast_listlike_indexer(target)\n5978 | \n5979 |         if not self._should_compare(target) and not self._should_partial_index(target):\n5980 |             # _should_partial_index e.g. IntervalIndex with numeric scalars\n5981 |             #  that can be matched to Interval scalars.\n5982 |             return self._get_indexer_non_comparable(target, method=None, unique=False)\n5983 | \n5984 |         pself, ptarget = self._maybe_downcast_for_indexing(target)\n5985 |         if pself is not self or ptarget is not target:\n5986 |             return pself.get_indexer_non_unique(ptarget)\n5987 | \n5988 |         if self.dtype != target.dtype:\n5989 |             # TODO: if object, could use infer_dtype to preempt costly\n5990 |             #  conversion if still non-comparable?\n5991 |             dtype = self._find_common_type_compat(target)\n5992 | \n5993 |             this = self.astype(dtype, copy=False)\n5994 |             that = target.astype(dtype, copy=False)\n5995 |             return this.get_indexer_non_unique(that)\n5996 | \n5997 |         # TODO: get_indexer has fastpaths for both Categorical-self and\n5998 |         #  Categorical-target. Can we do something similar here?\n5999 | \n6000 |         # Note: _maybe_downcast_for_indexing ensures we never get here\n6001 |         #  with MultiIndex self and non-Multi target\n6002 |         if self._is_multi and target._is_multi:\n6003 |             engine = self._engine\n6004 |             # Item \"IndexEngine\" of \"Union[IndexEngine, ExtensionEngine]\" has\n6005 |             # no attribute \"_extract_level_codes\"\n6006 |             tgt_values = engine._extract_level_codes(target)  # type: ignore[union-attr]\n6007 |         else:\n6008 |             tgt_values = target._get_engine_target()\n6009 | \n6010 |         indexer, missing = self._engine.get_indexer_non_unique(tgt_values)\n6011 |         return ensure_platform_int(indexer), ensure_platform_int(missing)\n6012 | \n6013 |     @final\n6014 |     def get_indexer_for(self, target) -> npt.NDArray[np.intp]:\n6015 |         \"\"\"\n6016 |         Guaranteed return of an indexer even when non-unique.\n6017 | \n6018 |         This dispatches to get_indexer or get_indexer_non_unique\n6019 |         as appropriate.\n6020 | \n6021 |         Parameters\n6022 |         ----------\n6023 |         target : Index\n6024 |             An iterable containing the values to be used for computing indexer.\n6025 | \n6026 |         Returns\n6027 |         -------\n6028 |         np.ndarray[np.intp]\n6029 |             List of indices.\n6030 | \n6031 |         See Also\n6032 |         --------\n6033 |         Index.get_indexer : Computes indexer and mask for new index given\n6034 |             the current index.\n6035 |         Index.get_non_unique : Returns indexer and masks for new index given\n6036 |             the current index.\n6037 | \n6038 |         Examples\n6039 |         --------\n6040 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([np.nan, \"var1\", np.nan])\n6041 |         >>> idx.get_indexer_for([np.nan])\n6042 |         array([0, 2])\n6043 |         \"\"\"\n6044 |         if self._index_as_unique:\n6045 |             return self.get_indexer(target)\n6046 |         indexer, _ = self.get_indexer_non_unique(target)\n6047 |         return indexer\n6048 | \n6049 |     def _get_indexer_strict(self, key, axis_name: str_t) -> tuple[Index, np.ndarray]:\n6050 |         \"\"\"\n6051 |         Analogue to get_indexer that raises if any elements are missing.\n6052 |         \"\"\"\n6053 |         keyarr = key\n6054 |         if not isinstance(keyarr, Index):\n6055 |             keyarr = com.asarray_tuplesafe(keyarr)\n6056 | \n6057 |         if self._index_as_unique:\n6058 |             indexer = self.get_indexer_for(keyarr)\n6059 |             keyarr = self.reindex(keyarr)[0]\n6060 |         else:\n6061 |             keyarr, indexer, new_indexer = self._reindex_non_unique(keyarr)\n6062 | \n6063 |         self._raise_if_missing(keyarr, indexer, axis_name)\n6064 | \n6065 |         keyarr = self.take(indexer)\n6066 |         if isinstance(key, Index):\n6067 |             # GH 42790 - Preserve name from an Index\n6068 |             keyarr.name = key.name\n6069 |         if lib.is_np_dtype(keyarr.dtype, \"mM\") or isinstance(\n6070 |             keyarr.dtype, DatetimeTZDtype\n6071 |         ):\n6072 |             # DTI/TDI.take can infer a freq in some cases when we dont want one\n6073 |             if isinstance(key, list) or (\n6074 |                 isinstance(key, type(self))\n6075 |                 # \"Index\" has no attribute \"freq\"\n6076 |                 and key.freq is None  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n6077 |             ):\n6078 |                 keyarr = keyarr._with_freq(None)\n6079 | \n6080 |         return keyarr, indexer\n6081 | \n6082 |     def _raise_if_missing(self, key, indexer, axis_name: str_t) -> None:\n6083 |         \"\"\"\n6084 |         Check that indexer can be used to return a result.\n6085 | \n6086 |         e.g. at least one element was found,\n6087 |         unless the list of keys was actually empty.\n6088 | \n6089 |         Parameters\n6090 |         ----------\n6091 |         key : list-like\n6092 |             Targeted labels (only used to show correct error message).\n6093 |         indexer: array-like of booleans\n6094 |             Indices corresponding to the key,\n6095 |             (with -1 indicating not found).\n6096 |         axis_name : str\n6097 | \n6098 |         Raises\n6099 |         ------\n6100 |         KeyError\n6101 |             If at least one key was requested but none was found.\n6102 |         \"\"\"\n6103 |         if len(key) == 0:\n6104 |             return\n6105 | \n6106 |         # Count missing values\n6107 |         missing_mask = indexer < 0\n6108 |         nmissing = missing_mask.sum()\n6109 | \n6110 |         if nmissing:\n6111 |             if nmissing == len(indexer):\n6112 |                 raise KeyError(f\"None of [{key}] are in the [{axis_name}]\")\n6113 | \n6114 |             not_found = list(ensure_index(key)[missing_mask.nonzero()[0]].unique())\n6115 |             raise KeyError(f\"{not_found} not in index\")\n6116 | \n6117 |     @overload\n6118 |     def _get_indexer_non_comparable(\n6119 |         self, target: Index, method, unique: Literal[True] = ...\n6120 |     ) -> npt.NDArray[np.intp]: ...\n6121 | \n6122 |     @overload\n6123 |     def _get_indexer_non_comparable(\n6124 |         self, target: Index, method, unique: Literal[False]\n6125 |     ) -> tuple[npt.NDArray[np.intp], npt.NDArray[np.intp]]: ...\n6126 | \n6127 |     @overload\n6128 |     def _get_indexer_non_comparable(\n6129 |         self, target: Index, method, unique: bool = True\n6130 |     ) -> npt.NDArray[np.intp] | tuple[npt.NDArray[np.intp], npt.NDArray[np.intp]]: ...\n6131 | \n6132 |     @final\n6133 |     def _get_indexer_non_comparable(\n6134 |         self, target: Index, method, unique: bool = True\n6135 |     ) -> npt.NDArray[np.intp] | tuple[npt.NDArray[np.intp], npt.NDArray[np.intp]]:\n6136 |         \"\"\"\n6137 |         Called from get_indexer or get_indexer_non_unique when the target\n6138 |         is of a non-comparable dtype.\n6139 | \n6140 |         For get_indexer lookups with method=None, get_indexer is an _equality_\n6141 |         check, so non-comparable dtypes mean we will always have no matches.\n6142 | \n6143 |         For get_indexer lookups with a method, get_indexer is an _inequality_\n6144 |         check, so non-comparable dtypes mean we will always raise TypeError.\n6145 | \n6146 |         Parameters\n6147 |         ----------\n6148 |         target : Index\n6149 |         method : str or None\n6150 |         unique : bool, default True\n6151 |             * True if called from get_indexer.\n6152 |             * False if called from get_indexer_non_unique.\n6153 | \n6154 |         Raises\n6155 |         ------\n6156 |         TypeError\n6157 |             If doing an inequality check, i.e. method is not None.\n6158 |         \"\"\"\n6159 |         if method is not None:\n6160 |             other_dtype = _unpack_nested_dtype(target)\n6161 |             raise TypeError(f\"Cannot compare dtypes {self.dtype} and {other_dtype}\")\n6162 | \n6163 |         no_matches = -1 * np.ones(target.shape, dtype=np.intp)\n6164 |         if unique:\n6165 |             # This is for get_indexer\n6166 |             return no_matches\n6167 |         else:\n6168 |             # This is for get_indexer_non_unique\n6169 |             missing = np.arange(len(target), dtype=np.intp)\n6170 |             return no_matches, missing\n6171 | \n6172 |     @property\n6173 |     def _index_as_unique(self) -> bool:\n6174 |         \"\"\"\n6175 |         Whether we should treat this as unique for the sake of\n6176 |         get_indexer vs get_indexer_non_unique.\n6177 | \n6178 |         For IntervalIndex compat.\n6179 |         \"\"\"\n6180 |         return self.is_unique\n6181 | \n6182 |     _requires_unique_msg = \"Reindexing only valid with uniquely valued Index objects\"\n6183 | \n6184 |     @final\n6185 |     def _maybe_downcast_for_indexing(self, other: Index) -> tuple[Index, Index]:\n6186 |         \"\"\"\n6187 |         When dealing with an object-dtype Index and a non-object Index, see\n6188 |         if we can upcast the object-dtype one to improve performance.\n6189 |         \"\"\"\n6190 | \n6191 |         if isinstance(self, ABCDatetimeIndex) and isinstance(other, ABCDatetimeIndex):\n6192 |             if (\n6193 |                 self.tz is not None\n6194 |                 and other.tz is not None\n6195 |                 and not tz_compare(self.tz, other.tz)\n6196 |             ):\n6197 |                 # standardize on UTC\n6198 |                 return self.tz_convert(\"UTC\"), other.tz_convert(\"UTC\")\n6199 | \n6200 |         elif self.inferred_type == \"date\" and isinstance(other, ABCDatetimeIndex):\n6201 |             try:\n6202 |                 return type(other)(self), other\n6203 |             except OutOfBoundsDatetime:\n6204 |                 return self, other\n6205 |         elif self.inferred_type == \"timedelta\" and isinstance(other, ABCTimedeltaIndex):\n6206 |             # TODO: we dont have tests that get here\n6207 |             return type(other)(self), other\n6208 | \n6209 |         elif self.dtype.kind == \"u\" and other.dtype.kind == \"i\":\n6210 |             # GH#41873\n6211 |             if other.min() >= 0:\n6212 |                 # lookup min as it may be cached\n6213 |                 # TODO: may need itemsize check if we have non-64-bit Indexes\n6214 |                 return self, other.astype(self.dtype)\n6215 | \n6216 |         elif self._is_multi and not other._is_multi:\n6217 |             try:\n6218 |                 # \"Type[Index]\" has no attribute \"from_tuples\"\n6219 |                 other = type(self).from_tuples(other)  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n6220 |             except (TypeError, ValueError):\n6221 |                 # let's instead try with a straight Index\n6222 |                 self = Index(self._values)\n6223 | \n6224 |         if not is_object_dtype(self.dtype) and is_object_dtype(other.dtype):\n6225 |             # Reverse op so we dont need to re-implement on the subclasses\n6226 |             other, self = other._maybe_downcast_for_indexing(self)\n6227 | \n6228 |         return self, other\n6229 | \n6230 |     @final\n6231 |     def _find_common_type_compat(self, target) -> DtypeObj:\n6232 |         \"\"\"\n6233 |         Implementation of find_common_type that adjusts for Index-specific\n6234 |         special cases.\n6235 |         \"\"\"\n6236 |         target_dtype, _ = infer_dtype_from(target)\n6237 | \n6238 |         # special case: if one dtype is uint64 and the other a signed int, return object\n6239 |         # See https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/issues/26778 for discussion\n6240 |         # Now it's:\n6241 |         # * float | [u]int -> float\n6242 |         # * uint64 | signed int  -> object\n6243 |         # We may change union(float | [u]int) to go to object.\n6244 |         if self.dtype == \"uint64\" or target_dtype == \"uint64\":\n6245 |             if is_signed_integer_dtype(self.dtype) or is_signed_integer_dtype(\n6246 |                 target_dtype\n6247 |             ):\n6248 |                 return _dtype_obj\n6249 | \n6250 |         dtype = find_result_type(self.dtype, target)\n6251 |         dtype = common_dtype_categorical_compat([self, target], dtype)\n6252 |         return dtype\n6253 | \n6254 |     @final\n6255 |     def _should_compare(self, other: Index) -> bool:\n6256 |         \"\"\"\n6257 |         Check if `self == other` can ever have non-False entries.\n6258 |         \"\"\"\n6259 | \n6260 |         # NB: we use inferred_type rather than is_bool_dtype to catch\n6261 |         #  object_dtype_of_bool and categorical[object_dtype_of_bool] cases\n6262 |         if (\n6263 |             other.inferred_type == \"boolean\" and is_any_real_numeric_dtype(self.dtype)\n6264 |         ) or (\n6265 |             self.inferred_type == \"boolean\" and is_any_real_numeric_dtype(other.dtype)\n6266 |         ):\n6267 |             # GH#16877 Treat boolean labels passed to a numeric index as not\n6268 |             #  found. Without this fix False and True would be treated as 0 and 1\n6269 |             #  respectively.\n6270 |             return False\n6271 | \n6272 |         dtype = _unpack_nested_dtype(other)\n6273 |         return (\n6274 |             self._is_comparable_dtype(dtype)\n6275 |             or is_object_dtype(dtype)\n6276 |             or is_string_dtype(dtype)\n6277 |         )\n6278 | \n6279 |     def _is_comparable_dtype(self, dtype: DtypeObj) -> bool:\n6280 |         \"\"\"\n6281 |         Can we compare values of the given dtype to our own?\n6282 |         \"\"\"\n6283 |         if self.dtype.kind == \"b\":\n6284 |             return dtype.kind == \"b\"\n6285 |         elif is_numeric_dtype(self.dtype):\n6286 |             return is_numeric_dtype(dtype)\n6287 |         # TODO: this was written assuming we only get here with object-dtype,\n6288 |         #  which is no longer correct. Can we specialize for EA?\n6289 |         return True\n6290 | \n6291 |     @final\n6292 |     def groupby(self, values) -> PrettyDict[Hashable, Index]:\n6293 |         \"\"\"\n6294 |         Group the index labels by a given array of values.\n6295 | \n6296 |         Parameters\n6297 |         ----------\n6298 |         values : array\n6299 |             Values used to determine the groups.\n6300 | \n6301 |         Returns\n6302 |         -------\n6303 |         dict\n6304 |             {group name -> group labels}\n6305 |         \"\"\"\n6306 |         # TODO: if we are a MultiIndex, we can do better\n6307 |         # that converting to tuples\n6308 |         if isinstance(values, ABCMultiIndex):\n6309 |             values = values._values\n6310 |         values = Categorical(values)\n6311 |         result = values._reverse_indexer()\n6312 | \n6313 |         # map to the label\n6314 |         result = {k: self.take(v) for k, v in result.items()}\n6315 | \n6316 |         return PrettyDict(result)\n6317 | \n6318 |     def map(self, mapper, na_action: Literal[\"ignore\"] | None = None):\n6319 |         \"\"\"\n6320 |         Map values using an input mapping or function.\n6321 | \n6322 |         Parameters\n6323 |         ----------\n6324 |         mapper : function, dict, or Series\n6325 |             Mapping correspondence.\n6326 |         na_action : {None, 'ignore'}\n6327 |             If 'ignore', propagate NA values, without passing them to the\n6328 |             mapping correspondence.\n6329 | \n6330 |         Returns\n6331 |         -------\n6332 |         Union[Index, MultiIndex]\n6333 |             The output of the mapping function applied to the index.\n6334 |             If the function returns a tuple with more than one element\n6335 |             a MultiIndex will be returned.\n6336 | \n6337 |         See Also\n6338 |         --------\n6339 |         Index.where : Replace values where the condition is False.\n6340 | \n6341 |         Examples\n6342 |         --------\n6343 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3])\n6344 |         >>> idx.map({1: \"a\", 2: \"b\", 3: \"c\"})\n6345 |         Index(['a', 'b', 'c'], dtype='object')\n6346 | \n6347 |         Using `map` with a function:\n6348 | \n6349 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3])\n6350 |         >>> idx.map(\"I am a {}\".format)\n6351 |         Index(['I am a 1', 'I am a 2', 'I am a 3'], dtype='object')\n6352 | \n6353 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"])\n6354 |         >>> idx.map(lambda x: x.upper())\n6355 |         Index(['A', 'B', 'C'], dtype='object')\n6356 |         \"\"\"\n6357 |         from pandas.core.indexes.multi import MultiIndex\n6358 | \n6359 |         new_values = self._map_values(mapper, na_action=na_action)\n6360 | \n6361 |         # we can return a MultiIndex\n6362 |         if new_values.size and isinstance(new_values[0], tuple):\n6363 |             if isinstance(self, MultiIndex):\n6364 |                 names = self.names\n6365 |             elif self.name:\n6366 |                 names = [self.name] * len(new_values[0])\n6367 |             else:\n6368 |                 names = None\n6369 |             return MultiIndex.from_tuples(new_values, names=names)\n6370 | \n6371 |         dtype = None\n6372 |         if not new_values.size:\n6373 |             # empty\n6374 |             dtype = self.dtype\n6375 | \n6376 |         # e.g. if we are floating and new_values is all ints, then we\n6377 |         #  don't want to cast back to floating.  But if we are UInt64\n6378 |         #  and new_values is all ints, we want to try.\n6379 |         same_dtype = lib.infer_dtype(new_values, skipna=False) == self.inferred_type\n6380 |         if same_dtype:\n6381 |             new_values = maybe_cast_pointwise_result(\n6382 |                 new_values, self.dtype, same_dtype=same_dtype\n6383 |             )\n6384 | \n6385 |         return Index._with_infer(new_values, dtype=dtype, copy=False, name=self.name)\n6386 | \n6387 |     # TODO: De-duplicate with map, xref GH#32349\n6388 |     @final\n6389 |     def _transform_index(self, func, *, level=None) -> Index:\n6390 |         \"\"\"\n6391 |         Apply function to all values found in index.\n6392 | \n6393 |         This includes transforming multiindex entries separately.\n6394 |         Only apply function to one level of the MultiIndex if level is specified.\n6395 |         \"\"\"\n6396 |         if isinstance(self, ABCMultiIndex):\n6397 |             values = [\n6398 |                 self.get_level_values(i).map(func)\n6399 |                 if i == level or level is None\n6400 |                 else self.get_level_values(i)\n6401 |                 for i in range(self.nlevels)\n6402 |             ]\n6403 |             return type(self).from_arrays(values)\n6404 |         else:\n6405 |             items = [func(x) for x in self]\n6406 |             return Index(items, name=self.name, tupleize_cols=False)\n6407 | \n6408 |     def isin(self, values, level: str_t | int | None = None) -> npt.NDArray[np.bool_]:\n6409 |         \"\"\"\n6410 |         Return a boolean array where the index values are in `values`.\n6411 | \n6412 |         Compute boolean array of whether each index value is found in the\n6413 |         passed set of values. The length of the returned boolean array matches\n6414 |         the length of the index.\n6415 | \n6416 |         Parameters\n6417 |         ----------\n6418 |         values : set or list-like\n6419 |             Sought values.\n6420 |         level : str or int, optional\n6421 |             Name or position of the index level to use (if the index is a\n6422 |             `MultiIndex`).\n6423 | \n6424 |         Returns\n6425 |         -------\n6426 |         np.ndarray[bool]\n6427 |             NumPy array of boolean values.\n6428 | \n6429 |         See Also\n6430 |         --------\n6431 |         Series.isin : Same for Series.\n6432 |         DataFrame.isin : Same method for DataFrames.\n6433 | \n6434 |         Notes\n6435 |         -----\n6436 |         In the case of `MultiIndex` you must either specify `values` as a\n6437 |         list-like object containing tuples that are the same length as the\n6438 |         number of levels, or specify `level`. Otherwise it will raise a\n6439 |         ``ValueError``.\n6440 | \n6441 |         If `level` is specified:\n6442 | \n6443 |         - if it is the name of one *and only one* index level, use that level;\n6444 |         - otherwise it should be a number indicating level position.\n6445 | \n6446 |         Examples\n6447 |         --------\n6448 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([1, 2, 3])\n6449 |         >>> idx\n6450 |         Index([1, 2, 3], dtype='int64')\n6451 | \n6452 |         Check whether each index value in a list of values.\n6453 | \n6454 |         >>> idx.isin([1, 4])\n6455 |         array([ True, False, False])\n6456 | \n6457 |         >>> midx = pd.MultiIndex.from_arrays(\n6458 |         ...     [[1, 2, 3], [\"red\", \"blue\", \"green\"]], names=[\"number\", \"color\"]\n6459 |         ... )\n6460 |         >>> midx\n6461 |         MultiIndex([(1,   'red'),\n6462 |                     (2,  'blue'),\n6463 |                     (3, 'green')],\n6464 |                    names=['number', 'color'])\n6465 | \n6466 |         Check whether the strings in the 'color' level of the MultiIndex\n6467 |         are in a list of colors.\n6468 | \n6469 |         >>> midx.isin([\"red\", \"orange\", \"yellow\"], level=\"color\")\n6470 |         array([ True, False, False])\n6471 | \n6472 |         To check across the levels of a MultiIndex, pass a list of tuples:\n6473 | \n6474 |         >>> midx.isin([(1, \"red\"), (3, \"red\")])\n6475 |         array([ True, False, False])\n6476 |         \"\"\"\n6477 |         if level is not None:\n6478 |             self._validate_index_level(level)\n6479 |         return algos.isin(self._values, values)\n6480 | \n6481 |     def _get_string_slice(self, key: str_t):\n6482 |         # this is for partial string indexing,\n6483 |         # overridden in DatetimeIndex, TimedeltaIndex and PeriodIndex\n6484 |         raise NotImplementedError\n6485 | \n6486 |     def slice_indexer(\n6487 |         self,\n6488 |         start: Hashable | None = None,\n6489 |         end: Hashable | None = None,\n6490 |         step: int | None = None,\n6491 |     ) -> slice:\n6492 |         \"\"\"\n6493 |         Compute the slice indexer for input labels and step.\n6494 | \n6495 |         Index needs to be ordered and unique.\n6496 | \n6497 |         Parameters\n6498 |         ----------\n6499 |         start : label, default None\n6500 |             If None, defaults to the beginning.\n6501 |         end : label, default None\n6502 |             If None, defaults to the end.\n6503 |         step : int, default None\n6504 |             If None, defaults to 1.\n6505 | \n6506 |         Returns\n6507 |         -------\n6508 |         slice\n6509 |             A slice object.\n6510 | \n6511 |         Raises\n6512 |         ------\n6513 |         KeyError : If key does not exist, or key is not unique and index is\n6514 |             not ordered.\n6515 | \n6516 |         See Also\n6517 |         --------\n6518 |         Index.slice_locs : Computes slice locations for input labels.\n6519 |         Index.get_slice_bound : Retrieves slice bound that corresponds to given label.\n6520 | \n6521 |         Notes\n6522 |         -----\n6523 |         This function assumes that the data is sorted, so use at your own peril.\n6524 | \n6525 |         Examples\n6526 |         --------\n6527 |         This is a method on all index types. For example you can do:\n6528 | \n6529 |         >>> idx = pd.Index(list(\"abcd\"))\n6530 |         >>> idx.slice_indexer(start=\"b\", end=\"c\")\n6531 |         slice(1, 3, None)\n6532 | \n6533 |         >>> idx = pd.MultiIndex.from_arrays([list(\"abcd\"), list(\"efgh\")])\n6534 |         >>> idx.slice_indexer(start=\"b\", end=(\"c\", \"g\"))\n6535 |         slice(1, 3, None)\n6536 |         \"\"\"\n6537 |         start_slice, end_slice = self.slice_locs(start, end, step=step)\n6538 | \n6539 |         # return a slice\n6540 |         if not is_scalar(start_slice):\n6541 |             raise AssertionError(\"Start slice bound is non-scalar\")\n6542 |         if not is_scalar(end_slice):\n6543 |             raise AssertionError(\"End slice bound is non-scalar\")\n6544 | \n6545 |         return slice(start_slice, end_slice, step)\n6546 | \n6547 |     def _maybe_cast_indexer(self, key):\n6548 |         \"\"\"\n6549 |         If we have a float key and are not a floating index, then try to cast\n6550 |         to an int if equivalent.\n6551 |         \"\"\"\n6552 |         return key\n6553 | \n6554 |     def _maybe_cast_listlike_indexer(self, target) -> Index:\n6555 |         \"\"\"\n6556 |         Analogue to maybe_cast_indexer for get_indexer instead of get_loc.\n6557 |         \"\"\"\n6558 |         target_index = ensure_index(target)\n6559 |         if (\n6560 |             not hasattr(target, \"dtype\")\n6561 |             and self.dtype == object\n6562 |             and target_index.dtype == \"string\"\n6563 |         ):\n6564 |             # If we started with a list-like, avoid inference to string dtype if self\n6565 |             # is object dtype (coercing to string dtype will alter the missing values)\n6566 |             target_index = Index(target, dtype=self.dtype)\n6567 |         return target_index\n6568 | \n6569 |     @final\n6570 |     def _validate_indexer(\n6571 |         self,\n6572 |         form: Literal[\"positional\", \"slice\"],\n6573 |         key,\n6574 |         kind: Literal[\"getitem\", \"iloc\"],\n6575 |     ) -> None:\n6576 |         \"\"\"\n6577 |         If we are positional indexer, validate that we have appropriate\n6578 |         typed bounds must be an integer.\n6579 |         \"\"\"\n6580 |         if not lib.is_int_or_none(key):\n6581 |             self._raise_invalid_indexer(form, key)\n6582 | \n6583 |     def _maybe_cast_slice_bound(self, label, side: str_t):\n6584 |         \"\"\"\n6585 |         This function should be overloaded in subclasses that allow non-trivial\n6586 |         casting on label-slice bounds, e.g. datetime-like indices allowing\n6587 |         strings containing formatted datetimes.\n6588 | \n6589 |         Parameters\n6590 |         ----------\n6591 |         label : object\n6592 |         side : {'left', 'right'}\n6593 | \n6594 |         Returns\n6595 |         -------\n6596 |         label : object\n6597 | \n6598 |         Notes\n6599 |         -----\n6600 |         Value of `side` parameter should be validated in caller.\n6601 |         \"\"\"\n6602 | \n6603 |         # We are a plain index here (sub-class override this method if they\n6604 |         # wish to have special treatment for floats/ints, e.g. datetimelike Indexes\n6605 | \n6606 |         if is_numeric_dtype(self.dtype):\n6607 |             return self._maybe_cast_indexer(label)\n6608 | \n6609 |         # reject them, if index does not contain label\n6610 |         if (is_float(label) or is_integer(label)) and label not in self:\n6611 |             self._raise_invalid_indexer(\"slice\", label)\n6612 | \n6613 |         return label\n6614 | \n6615 |     def _searchsorted_monotonic(self, label, side: Literal[\"left\", \"right\"] = \"left\"):\n6616 |         if self.is_monotonic_increasing:\n6617 |             return self.searchsorted(label, side=side)\n6618 |         elif self.is_monotonic_decreasing:\n6619 |             # np.searchsorted expects ascending sort order, have to reverse\n6620 |             # everything for it to work (element ordering, search side and\n6621 |             # resulting value).\n6622 |             pos = self[::-1].searchsorted(\n6623 |                 label, side=\"right\" if side == \"left\" else \"left\"\n6624 |             )\n6625 |             return len(self) - pos\n6626 | \n6627 |         raise ValueError(\"index must be monotonic increasing or decreasing\")\n6628 | \n6629 |     def get_slice_bound(self, label, side: Literal[\"left\", \"right\"]) -> int:\n6630 |         \"\"\"\n6631 |         Calculate slice bound that corresponds to given label.\n6632 | \n6633 |         Returns leftmost (one-past-the-rightmost if ``side=='right'``) position\n6634 |         of given label.\n6635 | \n6636 |         Parameters\n6637 |         ----------\n6638 |         label : object\n6639 |             The label for which to calculate the slice bound.\n6640 |         side : {'left', 'right'}\n6641 |             if 'left' return leftmost position of given label.\n6642 |             if 'right' return one-past-the-rightmost position of given label.\n6643 | \n6644 |         Returns\n6645 |         -------\n6646 |         int\n6647 |             Index of label.\n6648 | \n6649 |         See Also\n6650 |         --------\n6651 |         Index.get_loc : Get integer location, slice or boolean mask for requested\n6652 |             label.\n6653 | \n6654 |         Examples\n6655 |         --------\n6656 |         >>> idx = pd.RangeIndex(5)\n6657 |         >>> idx.get_slice_bound(3, \"left\")\n6658 |         3\n6659 | \n6660 |         >>> idx.get_slice_bound(3, \"right\")\n6661 |         4\n6662 | \n6663 |         If ``label`` is non-unique in the index, an error will be raised.\n6664 | \n6665 |         >>> idx_duplicate = pd.Index([\"a\", \"b\", \"a\", \"c\", \"d\"])\n6666 |         >>> idx_duplicate.get_slice_bound(\"a\", \"left\")\n6667 |         Traceback (most recent call last):\n6668 |         KeyError: Cannot get left slice bound for non-unique label: 'a'\n6669 |         \"\"\"\n6670 | \n6671 |         if side not in (\"left\", \"right\"):\n6672 |             raise ValueError(\n6673 |                 \"Invalid value for side kwarg, must be either \"\n6674 |                 f\"'left' or 'right': {side}\"\n6675 |             )\n6676 | \n6677 |         original_label = label\n6678 | \n6679 |         # For datetime indices label may be a string that has to be converted\n6680 |         # to datetime boundary according to its resolution.\n6681 |         label = self._maybe_cast_slice_bound(label, side)\n6682 | \n6683 |         # we need to look up the label\n6684 |         try:\n6685 |             slc = self.get_loc(label)\n6686 |         except KeyError as err:\n6687 |             try:\n6688 |                 return self._searchsorted_monotonic(label, side)\n6689 |             except ValueError:\n6690 |                 # raise the original KeyError\n6691 |                 raise err from None\n6692 | \n6693 |         if isinstance(slc, np.ndarray):\n6694 |             # get_loc may return a boolean array, which\n6695 |             # is OK as long as they are representable by a slice.\n6696 |             assert is_bool_dtype(slc.dtype)\n6697 |             slc = lib.maybe_booleans_to_slice(slc.view(\"u1\"))\n6698 |             if isinstance(slc, np.ndarray):\n6699 |                 raise KeyError(\n6700 |                     f\"Cannot get {side} slice bound for non-unique \"\n6701 |                     f\"label: {original_label!r}\"\n6702 |                 )\n6703 | \n6704 |         if isinstance(slc, slice):\n6705 |             if side == \"left\":\n6706 |                 return slc.start\n6707 |             else:\n6708 |                 return slc.stop\n6709 |         else:\n6710 |             if side == \"right\":\n6711 |                 return slc + 1\n6712 |             else:\n6713 |                 return slc\n6714 | \n6715 |     def slice_locs(\n6716 |         self,\n6717 |         start: SliceType = None,\n6718 |         end: SliceType = None,\n6719 |         step: int | None = None,\n6720 |     ) -> tuple[int, int]:\n6721 |         \"\"\"\n6722 |         Compute slice locations for input labels.\n6723 | \n6724 |         Parameters\n6725 |         ----------\n6726 |         start : label, default None\n6727 |             If None, defaults to the beginning.\n6728 |         end : label, default None\n6729 |             If None, defaults to the end.\n6730 |         step : int, defaults None\n6731 |             If None, defaults to 1.\n6732 | \n6733 |         Returns\n6734 |         -------\n6735 |         tuple[int, int]\n6736 |             Returns a tuple of two integers representing the slice locations for the\n6737 |             input labels within the index.\n6738 | \n6739 |         See Also\n6740 |         --------\n6741 |         Index.get_loc : Get location for a single label.\n6742 | \n6743 |         Notes\n6744 |         -----\n6745 |         This method only works if the index is monotonic or unique.\n6746 | \n6747 |         Examples\n6748 |         --------\n6749 |         >>> idx = pd.Index(list(\"abcd\"))\n6750 |         >>> idx.slice_locs(start=\"b\", end=\"c\")\n6751 |         (1, 3)\n6752 | \n6753 |         >>> idx = pd.Index(list(\"bcde\"))\n6754 |         >>> idx.slice_locs(start=\"a\", end=\"c\")\n6755 |         (0, 2)\n6756 |         \"\"\"\n6757 |         inc = step is None or step >= 0\n6758 | \n6759 |         if not inc:\n6760 |             # If it's a reverse slice, temporarily swap bounds.\n6761 |             start, end = end, start\n6762 | \n6763 |         # GH 16785: If start and end happen to be date strings with UTC offsets\n6764 |         # attempt to parse and check that the offsets are the same\n6765 |         if isinstance(start, (str, datetime)) and isinstance(end, (str, datetime)):\n6766 |             try:\n6767 |                 ts_start = Timestamp(start)\n6768 |                 ts_end = Timestamp(end)\n6769 |             except (ValueError, TypeError):\n6770 |                 pass\n6771 |             else:\n6772 |                 if not tz_compare(ts_start.tzinfo, ts_end.tzinfo):\n6773 |                     raise ValueError(\"Both dates must have the same UTC offset\")\n6774 | \n6775 |         start_slice = None\n6776 |         if start is not None:\n6777 |             start_slice = self.get_slice_bound(start, \"left\")\n6778 |         if start_slice is None:\n6779 |             start_slice = 0\n6780 | \n6781 |         end_slice = None\n6782 |         if end is not None:\n6783 |             end_slice = self.get_slice_bound(end, \"right\")\n6784 |         if end_slice is None:\n6785 |             end_slice = len(self)\n6786 | \n6787 |         if not inc:\n6788 |             # Bounds at this moment are swapped, swap them back and shift by 1.\n6789 |             #\n6790 |             # slice_locs('B', 'A', step=-1): s='B', e='A'\n6791 |             #\n6792 |             #              s='A'                 e='B'\n6793 |             # AFTER SWAP:    |                     |\n6794 |             #                v ------------------> V\n6795 |             #           -----------------------------------\n6796 |             #           | | |A|A|A|A| | | | | |B|B| | | | |\n6797 |             #           -----------------------------------\n6798 |             #              ^ <------------------ ^\n6799 |             # SHOULD BE:   |                     |\n6800 |             #           end=s-1              start=e-1\n6801 |             #\n6802 |             end_slice, start_slice = start_slice - 1, end_slice - 1\n6803 | \n6804 |             # i == -1 triggers ``len(self) + i`` selection that points to the\n6805 |             # last element, not before-the-first one, subtracting len(self)\n6806 |             # compensates that.\n6807 |             if end_slice == -1:\n6808 |                 end_slice -= len(self)\n6809 |             if start_slice == -1:\n6810 |                 start_slice -= len(self)\n6811 | \n6812 |         return start_slice, end_slice\n6813 | \n6814 |     def delete(\n6815 |         self, loc: int | np.integer | list[int] | npt.NDArray[np.integer]\n6816 |     ) -> Self:\n6817 |         \"\"\"\n6818 |         Make new Index with passed location(-s) deleted.\n6819 | \n6820 |         Parameters\n6821 |         ----------\n6822 |         loc : int or list of int\n6823 |             Location of item(-s) which will be deleted.\n6824 |             Use a list of locations to delete more than one value at the same time.\n6825 | \n6826 |         Returns\n6827 |         -------\n6828 |         Index\n6829 |             Will be same type as self, except for RangeIndex.\n6830 | \n6831 |         See Also\n6832 |         --------\n6833 |         numpy.delete : Delete any rows and column from NumPy array (ndarray).\n6834 | \n6835 |         Examples\n6836 |         --------\n6837 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"])\n6838 |         >>> idx.delete(1)\n6839 |         Index(['a', 'c'], dtype='object')\n6840 | \n6841 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"])\n6842 |         >>> idx.delete([0, 2])\n6843 |         Index(['b'], dtype='object')\n6844 |         \"\"\"\n6845 |         values = self._values\n6846 |         res_values: ArrayLike\n6847 |         if isinstance(values, np.ndarray):\n6848 |             # TODO(__array_function__): special casing will be unnecessary\n6849 |             res_values = np.delete(values, loc)\n6850 |         else:\n6851 |             res_values = values.delete(loc)\n6852 | \n6853 |         # _constructor so RangeIndex-> Index with an int64 dtype\n6854 |         return self._constructor._simple_new(res_values, name=self.name)\n6855 | \n6856 |     def insert(self, loc: int, item) -> Index:\n6857 |         \"\"\"\n6858 |         Make new Index inserting new item at location.\n6859 | \n6860 |         Follows Python numpy.insert semantics for negative values.\n6861 | \n6862 |         Parameters\n6863 |         ----------\n6864 |         loc : int\n6865 |             The integer location where the new item will be inserted.\n6866 |         item : object\n6867 |             The new item to be inserted into the Index.\n6868 | \n6869 |         Returns\n6870 |         -------\n6871 |         Index\n6872 |             Returns a new Index object resulting from inserting the specified item at\n6873 |             the specified location within the original Index.\n6874 | \n6875 |         See Also\n6876 |         --------\n6877 |         Index.append : Append a collection of Indexes together.\n6878 | \n6879 |         Examples\n6880 |         --------\n6881 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"])\n6882 |         >>> idx.insert(1, \"x\")\n6883 |         Index(['a', 'x', 'b', 'c'], dtype='object')\n6884 |         \"\"\"\n6885 |         item = lib.item_from_zerodim(item)\n6886 |         if is_valid_na_for_dtype(item, self.dtype) and self.dtype != object:\n6887 |             item = self._na_value\n6888 | \n6889 |         arr = self._values\n6890 | \n6891 |         try:\n6892 |             if isinstance(arr, ExtensionArray):\n6893 |                 res_values = arr.insert(loc, item)\n6894 |                 return type(self)._simple_new(res_values, name=self.name)\n6895 |             else:\n6896 |                 item = self._validate_fill_value(item)\n6897 |         except (TypeError, ValueError, LossySetitemError):\n6898 |             # e.g. trying to insert an integer into a DatetimeIndex\n6899 |             #  We cannot keep the same dtype, so cast to the (often object)\n6900 |             #  minimal shared dtype before doing the insert.\n6901 |             dtype = self._find_common_type_compat(item)\n6902 |             if dtype == self.dtype:\n6903 |                 # EA's might run into recursion errors if loc is invalid\n6904 |                 raise\n6905 |             return self.astype(dtype).insert(loc, item)\n6906 | \n6907 |         if arr.dtype != object or not isinstance(\n6908 |             item, (tuple, np.datetime64, np.timedelta64)\n6909 |         ):\n6910 |             # with object-dtype we need to worry about numpy incorrectly casting\n6911 |             # dt64/td64 to integer, also about treating tuples as sequences\n6912 |             # special-casing dt64/td64 https://github.com/numpy/numpy/issues/12550\n6913 |             casted = arr.dtype.type(item)\n6914 |             new_values = np.insert(arr, loc, casted)\n6915 | \n6916 |         else:\n6917 |             # error: No overload variant of \"insert\" matches argument types\n6918 |             # \"ndarray[Any, Any]\", \"int\", \"None\"\n6919 |             new_values = np.insert(arr, loc, None)  # type: ignore[call-overload]\n6920 |             loc = loc if loc >= 0 else loc - 1\n6921 |             new_values[loc] = item\n6922 | \n6923 |         # GH#51363 stopped doing dtype inference here\n6924 |         out = Index(new_values, dtype=new_values.dtype, name=self.name)\n6925 |         return out\n6926 | \n6927 |     def drop(\n6928 |         self,\n6929 |         labels: Index | np.ndarray | Iterable[Hashable],\n6930 |         errors: IgnoreRaise = \"raise\",\n6931 |     ) -> Index:\n6932 |         \"\"\"\n6933 |         Make new Index with passed list of labels deleted.\n6934 | \n6935 |         Parameters\n6936 |         ----------\n6937 |         labels : array-like or scalar\n6938 |             Array-like object or a scalar value, representing the labels to be removed\n6939 |             from the Index.\n6940 |         errors : {'ignore', 'raise'}, default 'raise'\n6941 |             If 'ignore', suppress error and existing labels are dropped.\n6942 | \n6943 |         Returns\n6944 |         -------\n6945 |         Index\n6946 |             Will be same type as self, except for RangeIndex.\n6947 | \n6948 |         Raises\n6949 |         ------\n6950 |         KeyError\n6951 |             If not all of the labels are found in the selected axis\n6952 | \n6953 |         See Also\n6954 |         --------\n6955 |         Index.dropna : Return Index without NA/NaN values.\n6956 |         Index.drop_duplicates : Return Index with duplicate values removed.\n6957 | \n6958 |         Examples\n6959 |         --------\n6960 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"])\n6961 |         >>> idx.drop([\"a\"])\n6962 |         Index(['b', 'c'], dtype='object')\n6963 |         \"\"\"\n6964 |         if not isinstance(labels, Index):\n6965 |             # avoid materializing e.g. RangeIndex\n6966 |             arr_dtype = \"object\" if self.dtype == \"object\" else None\n6967 |             labels = com.index_labels_to_array(labels, dtype=arr_dtype)\n6968 | \n6969 |         indexer = self.get_indexer_for(labels)\n6970 |         mask = indexer == -1\n6971 |         if mask.any():\n6972 |             if errors != \"ignore\":\n6973 |                 raise KeyError(f\"{labels[mask].tolist()} not found in axis\")\n6974 |             indexer = indexer[~mask]\n6975 |         return self.delete(indexer)\n6976 | \n6977 |     @final\n6978 |     def infer_objects(self, copy: bool = True) -> Index:\n6979 |         \"\"\"\n6980 |         If we have an object dtype, try to infer a non-object dtype.\n6981 | \n6982 |         Parameters\n6983 |         ----------\n6984 |         copy : bool, default True\n6985 |             Whether to make a copy in cases where no inference occurs.\n6986 |         \"\"\"\n6987 |         if self._is_multi:\n6988 |             raise NotImplementedError(\n6989 |                 \"infer_objects is not implemented for MultiIndex. \"\n6990 |                 \"Use index.to_frame().infer_objects() instead.\"\n6991 |             )\n6992 |         if self.dtype != object:\n6993 |             return self.copy() if copy else self\n6994 | \n6995 |         values = self._values\n6996 |         values = cast(\"npt.NDArray[np.object_]\", values)\n6997 |         res_values = lib.maybe_convert_objects(\n6998 |             values,\n6999 |             convert_non_numeric=True,\n7000 |         )\n7001 |         if copy and res_values is values:\n7002 |             return self.copy()\n7003 |         result = Index(res_values, name=self.name)\n7004 |         if not copy and res_values is values and self._references is not None:\n7005 |             result._references = self._references\n7006 |             result._references.add_index_reference(result)\n7007 |         return result\n7008 | \n7009 |     @final\n7010 |     def diff(self, periods: int = 1) -> Index:\n7011 |         \"\"\"\n7012 |         Computes the difference between consecutive values in the Index object.\n7013 | \n7014 |         If periods is greater than 1, computes the difference between values that\n7015 |         are `periods` number of positions apart.\n7016 | \n7017 |         Parameters\n7018 |         ----------\n7019 |         periods : int, optional\n7020 |             The number of positions between the current and previous\n7021 |             value to compute the difference with. Default is 1.\n7022 | \n7023 |         Returns\n7024 |         -------\n7025 |         Index\n7026 |             A new Index object with the computed differences.\n7027 | \n7028 |         Examples\n7029 |         --------\n7030 |         >>> import pandas as pd\n7031 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([10, 20, 30, 40, 50])\n7032 |         >>> idx.diff()\n7033 |         Index([nan, 10.0, 10.0, 10.0, 10.0], dtype='float64')\n7034 | \n7035 |         \"\"\"\n7036 |         return Index(self.to_series().diff(periods))\n7037 | \n7038 |     def round(self, decimals: int = 0) -> Self:\n7039 |         \"\"\"\n7040 |         Round each value in the Index to the given number of decimals.\n7041 | \n7042 |         Parameters\n7043 |         ----------\n7044 |         decimals : int, optional\n7045 |             Number of decimal places to round to. If decimals is negative,\n7046 |             it specifies the number of positions to the left of the decimal point.\n7047 | \n7048 |         Returns\n7049 |         -------\n7050 |         Index\n7051 |             A new Index with the rounded values.\n7052 | \n7053 |         Examples\n7054 |         --------\n7055 |         >>> import pandas as pd\n7056 |         >>> idx = pd.Index([10.1234, 20.5678, 30.9123, 40.4567, 50.7890])\n7057 |         >>> idx.round(decimals=2)\n7058 |         Index([10.12, 20.57, 30.91, 40.46, 50.79], dtype='float64')\n7059 | \n7060 |         \"\"\"\n7061 |         return self._constructor(self.to_series().round(decimals))\n7062 | \n7063 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n7064 |     # Generated Arithmetic, Comparison, and Unary Methods\n7065 | \n7066 |     def _cmp_method(self, other, op):\n7067 |         \"\"\"\n7068 |         Wrapper used to dispatch comparison operations.\n7069 |         \"\"\"\n7070 |         if self.is_(other):\n7071 |             # fastpath\n7072 |             if op in {operator.eq, operator.le, operator.ge}:\n7073 |                 arr = np.ones(len(self), dtype=bool)\n7074 |                 if self._can_hold_na and not isinstance(self, ABCMultiIndex):\n7075 |                     # TODO: should set MultiIndex._can_hold_na = False?\n7076 |                     arr[self.isna()] = False\n7077 |                 return arr\n7078 |             elif op is operator.ne:\n7079 |                 arr = np.zeros(len(self), dtype=bool)\n7080 |                 if self._can_hold_na and not isinstance(self, ABCMultiIndex):\n7081 |                     arr[self.isna()] = True\n7082 |                 return arr\n7083 | \n7084 |         if isinstance(other, (np.ndarray, Index, ABCSeries, ExtensionArray)) and len(\n7085 |             self\n7086 |         ) != len(other):\n7087 |             raise ValueError(\"Lengths must match to compare\")\n7088 | \n7089 |         if not isinstance(other, ABCMultiIndex):\n7090 |             other = extract_array(other, extract_numpy=True)\n7091 |         else:\n7092 |             other = np.asarray(other)\n7093 | \n7094 |         if is_object_dtype(self.dtype) and isinstance(other, ExtensionArray):\n7095 |             # e.g. PeriodArray, Categorical\n7096 |             result = op(self._values, other)\n7097 | \n7098 |         elif isinstance(self._values, ExtensionArray):\n7099 |             result = op(self._values, other)\n7100 | \n7101 |         elif is_object_dtype(self.dtype) and not isinstance(self, ABCMultiIndex):\n7102 |             # don't pass MultiIndex\n7103 |             result = ops.comp_method_OBJECT_ARRAY(op, self._values, other)\n7104 | \n7105 |         else:\n7106 |             result = ops.comparison_op(self._values, other, op)\n7107 | \n7108 |         return result\n7109 | \n7110 |     @final\n7111 |     def _logical_method(self, other, op):\n7112 |         res_name = ops.get_op_result_name(self, other)\n7113 | \n7114 |         lvalues = self._values\n7115 |         rvalues = extract_array(other, extract_numpy=True, extract_range=True)\n7116 | \n7117 |         res_values = ops.logical_op(lvalues, rvalues, op)\n7118 |         return self._construct_result(res_values, name=res_name)\n7119 | \n7120 |     @final\n7121 |     def _construct_result(self, result, name):\n7122 |         if isinstance(result, tuple):\n7123 |             return (\n7124 |                 Index(result[0], name=name, dtype=result[0].dtype),\n7125 |                 Index(result[1], name=name, dtype=result[1].dtype),\n7126 |             )\n7127 |         return Index(result, name=name, dtype=result.dtype)\n7128 | \n7129 |     def _arith_method(self, other, op):\n7130 |         if (\n7131 |             isinstance(other, Index)\n7132 |             and is_object_dtype(other.dtype)\n7133 |             and type(other) is not Index\n7134 |         ):\n7135 |             # We return NotImplemented for object-dtype index *subclasses* so they have\n7136 |             # a chance to implement ops before we unwrap them.\n7137 |             # See https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/issues/31109\n7138 |             return NotImplemented\n7139 | \n7140 |         return super()._arith_method(other, op)\n7141 | \n7142 |     @final\n7143 |     def _unary_method(self, op):\n7144 |         result = op(self._values)\n7145 |         return Index(result, name=self.name)\n7146 | \n7147 |     def __abs__(self) -> Index:\n7148 |         return self._unary_method(operator.abs)\n7149 | \n7150 |     def __neg__(self) -> Index:\n7151 |         return self._unary_method(operator.neg)\n7152 | \n7153 |     def __pos__(self) -> Index:\n7154 |         return self._unary_method(operator.pos)\n7155 | \n7156 |     def __invert__(self) -> Index:\n7157 |         # GH#8875\n7158 |         return self._unary_method(operator.inv)\n7159 | \n7160 |     # --------------------------------------------------------------------\n7161 |     # Reductions\n7162 | \n7163 |     def any(self, *args, **kwargs):\n7164 |         \"\"\"\n7165 |         Return whether any element is Truthy.\n7166 | \n7167 |         Parameters\n7168 |         ----------\n7169 |         *args\n7170 |             Required for compatibility with numpy.\n7171 |         **kwargs\n7172 |             Required for compatibility with numpy.\n7173 | \n7174 |         Returns\n7175 |         -------\n7176 |         bool or array-like (if axis is specified)\n7177 |             A single element array-like may be converted to bool.\n7178 | \n7179 |         See Also\n7180 |         --------\n7181 |         Index.all : Return whether all elements are True.\n7182 |         Series.all : Return whether all elements are True.\n7183 | \n7184 |         Notes\n7185 |         -----\n7186 |         Not a Number (NaN), positive infinity and negative infinity\n7187 |         evaluate to True because these are not equal to zero.\n7188 | \n7189 |         Examples\n7190 |         --------\n7191 |         >>> index = pd.Index([0, 1, 2])\n7192 |         >>> index.any()\n7193 |         True\n7194 | \n7195 |         >>> index = pd.Index([0, 0, 0])\n7196 |         >>> index.any()\n7197 |         False\n7198 |         \"\"\"\n7199 |         nv.validate_any(args, kwargs)\n7200 |         self._maybe_disable_logical_methods(\"any\")\n7201 |         vals = self._values\n7202 |         if not isinstance(vals, np.ndarray):\n7203 |             # i.e. EA, call _reduce instead of \"any\" to get TypeError instead\n7204 |             #  of AttributeError\n7205 |             return vals._reduce(\"any\")\n7206 |         return np.any(vals)\n7207 | \n7208 |     def all(self, *args, **kwargs):\n7209 |         \"\"\"\n7210 |         Return whether all elements are Truthy.\n7211 | \n7212 |         Parameters\n7213 |         ----------\n7214 |         *args\n7215 |             Required for compatibility with numpy.\n7216 |         **kwargs\n7217 |             Required for compatibility with numpy.\n7218 | \n7219 |         Returns\n7220 |         -------\n7221 |         bool or array-like (if axis is specified)\n7222 |             A single element array-like may be converted to bool.\n7223 | \n7224 |         See Also\n7225 |         --------\n7226 |         Index.any : Return whether any element in an Index is True.\n7227 |         Series.any : Return whether any element in a Series is True.\n7228 |         Series.all : Return whether all elements in a Series are True.\n7229 | \n7230 |         Notes\n7231 |         -----\n7232 |         Not a Number (NaN), positive infinity and negative infinity\n7233 |         evaluate to True because these are not equal to zero.\n7234 | \n7235 |         Examples\n7236 |         --------\n7237 |         True, because nonzero integers are considered True.\n7238 | \n7239 |         >>> pd.Index([1, 2, 3]).all()\n7240 |         True\n7241 | \n7242 |         False, because ``0`` is considered False.\n7243 | \n7244 |         >>> pd.Index([0, 1, 2]).all()\n7245 |         False\n7246 |         \"\"\"\n7247 |         nv.validate_all(args, kwargs)\n7248 |         self._maybe_disable_logical_methods(\"all\")\n7249 |         vals = self._values\n7250 |         if not isinstance(vals, np.ndarray):\n7251 |             # i.e. EA, call _reduce instead of \"all\" to get TypeError instead\n7252 |             #  of AttributeError\n7253 |             return vals._reduce(\"all\")\n7254 |         return np.all(vals)\n7255 | \n7256 |     @final\n7257 |     def _maybe_disable_logical_methods(self, opname: str_t) -> None:\n7258 |         \"\"\"\n7259 |         raise if this Index subclass does not support any or all.\n7260 |         \"\"\"\n7261 |         if isinstance(self, ABCMultiIndex):\n7262 |             raise TypeError(f\"cannot perform {opname} with {type(self).__name__}\")\n7263 | \n7264 |     @Appender(IndexOpsMixin.argmin.__doc__)\n7265 |     def argmin(\n7266 |         self, axis: AxisInt | None = None, skipna: bool = True, *args, **kwargs\n7267 |     ) -> int:\n7268 |         nv.validate_argmin(args, kwargs)\n7269 |         nv.validate_minmax_axis(axis)\n7270 | \n7271 |         if not self._is_multi and self.hasnans:\n7272 |             if not skipna:\n7273 |                 raise ValueError(\"Encountered an NA value with skipna=False\")\n7274 |             elif self._isnan.all():\n7275 |                 raise ValueError(\"Encountered all NA values\")\n7276 | \n7277 |         return super().argmin(skipna=skipna)\n7278 | \n7279 |     @Appender(IndexOpsMixin.argmax.__doc__)\n7280 |     def argmax(\n7281 |         self, axis: AxisInt | None = None, skipna: bool = True, *args, **kwargs\n7282 |     ) -> int:\n7283 |         nv.validate_argmax(args, kwargs)\n7284 |         nv.validate_minmax_axis(axis)\n7285 | \n7286 |         if not self._is_multi and self.hasnans:\n7287 |             if not skipna:\n7288 |                 raise ValueError(\"Encountered an NA value with skipna=False\")\n7289 |             elif self._isnan.all():\n7290 |                 raise ValueError(\"Encountered all NA values\")\n7291 |         return super().argmax(skipna=skipna)\n7292 | \n7293 |     def min(self, axis: AxisInt | None = None, skipna: bool = True, *args, **kwargs):\n7294 |         \"\"\"\n7295 |         Return the minimum value of the Index.\n7296 | \n7297 |         Parameters\n7298 |         ----------\n7299 |         axis : {None}\n7300 |             Dummy